//@version=6
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  PRAGATI · प्रगति  ·  MULTI-TIMEFRAME CONVICTION OSCILLATOR              v5
//  "Is this move paid for - and is it paid for on every timeframe?"
//
//  Pragati is progress: travel that turns into displacement and holds under
//  load. Its CONVICTION measures how consistently, and with how much
//  participation behind it, bars resolve one way; Samanvaya's VALUE, carried
//  whole, measures where price stands against fair. The pane draws one TRACE
//  of the two - blended by default, or either alone - and its histogram. Four
//  tapes read a ladder of timeframes through the two instruments, each on its
//  own ladder setting: whether conviction HOLDS across the frames (ladder
//  down by default), and where the frames stand in VALUE (ladder up by
//  default, as in Samanvaya). Each instrument has a LEVEL tape and a MOMENTUM
//  tape.
//
//      +90  MTF CONVICTION MOMENTUM  which way the faster frames lead
//      +82  MTF CONVICTION TAPE      who controls the ladder, and how far
//      PANE TRACE                    conviction × value by default, or either
//                                    alone · histogram, rails, signals
//      −82  MTF VALUE TAPE           Samanvaya's MTF bias: rich or cheap
//                                    across the ladder, and how far -
//                                    colours flipped
//      −90  MTF VALUE MOMENTUM       whether the faster frames widen the
//                                    dislocation or already close it
//
//  THE PANE draws the trace chosen by 'Trace' (THE TRACE, below). On the
//  Conviction trace it is the single-frame engine, bit-identical to the
//  version published as Nishchaya v3 - the engine every number in the
//  EVIDENCE section was measured on - and the whole script reads as it did
//  before the trace had a choice. The tapes never change the trace or its
//  histogram. The two level tapes are layers of every SIGNAL: nothing fires
//  unless the pane and both of them agree. The two momentum tapes are
//  context: no signal reads them.
//
//  ═══ WHAT IS ON THE PANE, TOP TO BOTTOM ═══════════════════════════════════
//      +90   MTF conviction momentum tape              faster frames − slower
//      +82   MTF conviction tape                       who controls the ladder
//      +60   dashed rail · bearish R H                  divergence
//      +in   dotted rail · ▼ SELL declaration, short ◆ signals
//        0   trace, signal line, histogram             the trace chosen
//      −in   dotted rail · ▲ BUY declaration, long ◆   signals
//      −60   dashed rail · bullish R H                  divergence
//      −82   MTF value tape                            rich or cheap on the ladder
//      −90   MTF value momentum tape                   faster frames − slower
//  The rails follow the trace: the inner rail is the conviction zone (±30) on
//  the Conviction trace and θ (±43 at the default 1.5) on the other two; the
//  outer rail is ±60 on all three at the defaults.
//  Right edge: the trace's name on its inner rail; each ladder's name, frames
//  and two readings - its tape, then its momentum (↺ = fallback direction);
//  and, only when the stack cannot judge, a gold SIGNALS PAUSED label naming
//  why. Optional panel on the right. On the price chart: the volume profile,
//  the readout - an OI row and a Conviction-Value Grid row in one table -
//  and, off the Conviction trace, the divergence connectors.
//
//  ═══ THE SETTINGS, IN MENU ORDER ══════════════════════════════════════════
//      1 · Conviction engine        the pane's measurement: lookback, smoothing,
//                                   signal, normalization, participation
//      2 · Trace and MTF tapes      the TRACE; which ladder each instrument
//                                   reads, and each momentum tape on or off
//      3 · Value model · Samanvaya  macro hedge, model clock, leg mix, θ, custom
//                                   driver - everything that moves the value tape
//      4 · Signals · ▲ ▼ ◆          the two signal families and their windows
//      5 · Divergence · R H         the pivot engine
//      6 · Zones                    conviction's inner and outer - the
//                                   rails on the Conviction trace
//      7 · Display                  view, markers, histogram, trace, palette
//      8 · Information panel
//      9 · Volume profile           price-chart overlay, display only
//     10 · Open interest            its readout row, and where the readout
//                                   sits on the price chart
//     11 · Conviction-Value Grid    its readout row
//
//  ═══ THE TRACE · CONVICTION × VALUE, CONVICTION, OR VALUE ════════════════
//  One setting, 'Trace' (2 · Trace and MTF tapes), chooses what the pane
//  draws. Its histogram is always the trace minus the trace's signal EMA.
//
//      CONVICTION × VALUE   default. Both ingredients in σ, blended one level
//                           up the way Samanvaya blends its own two legs:
//              z_c   = raw / σ, smoothed         conviction in σ
//              z_v   = Samanvaya's unified z     value in σ
//              trace = 100 · softbound( 0.5 · (z_c + z_v) / √(2·(1 + ρ)) )
//                           ρ their correlation over 200 bars, which restores
//                           the blend's variance. The softbound and the 0.5
//                           are Samanvaya's, so θ lands on the trace at ±43
//                           exactly as on the value tape. It reads how far
//                           the move is STRETCHED - in one-sided effort and
//                           in price against fair.
//      CONVICTION           the single-frame engine alone, bit-identical to
//                           Nishchaya v3, on its own scale: 100 · tanh(raw /
//                           3σ), against the conviction zones.
//      VALUE                Samanvaya's value on this chart alone, its own
//                           pane reading, against θ.
//
//  THE RAILS FOLLOW THE TRACE. On the Conviction trace they are the zones
//  (±30, ±60); on the other two, θ and ±60 - a 3σ stretch on Samanvaya's
//  scale, twice the default θ. The trace's intensity grades on the same two.
//
//  WHAT READS THE TRACE, AND WHAT STILL READS CONVICTION. The trace and its
//  histogram stand wherever a rule reads the pane: the columns and their
//  tiers, the impulse gate, the pullback, the ▲▼ push, the rails, the
//  panel's headline. Wherever a rule asks about CONVICTION itself, it reads
//  conviction whichever trace is drawn:
//      · the chart rung of the conviction ladder, and its momentum tape
//      · control on the chart for a ◆ - conviction above zero
//      · the divergence pivots: R and H are found on conviction's own
//        pivots, where they were measured. Off the Conviction trace their
//        connector is drawn on the price chart, between the swing extremes
//      · the quiet regime - conviction's σ; it desaturates the columns only
//        while the trace carries conviction
//      · the Conviction-Value Grid: Pragyam reads conviction's histogram, so
//        the readout does too - the pane's own on the Conviction trace
//  The ▲▼ ARM follows the trace when it carries value - the trace itself
//  crossing back through θ. On the Conviction trace it stays Samanvaya's
//  value on this chart; on the Value trace the two are one series.
//
//  A BLEND DIMS while its ingredients point opposite ways - cheap with
//  buyers pushing is accumulation, rich with sellers pushing distribution -
//  and while the value basket settles after a rotation: a blended trace near
//  zero is not always quiet. The panel's 'trace' row gives both ingredients
//  in σ, and their ρ.
//
//  CALIBRATION, NOT EDGE. Rebuilt approximately from Pragyam's daily panels
//  (Dow 30, Nifty 50, an ETF book): the ingredients' ρ has a median of +0.45
//  to +0.49; the blend sits past θ on 19-21% of days, as value alone does
//  (20-21%), so θ keeps its meaning; past ±60 on only 0.1-0.2%, where
//  conviction's outer zone holds about 5% - the outer rail is a rare 3σ mark,
//  not a working zone. The ingredients point opposite ways a third of the
//  time, always with the blend inside θ, so the dim never hides a stretch.
//
//  UNMEASURED. The blended trace and its histogram are new objects: nothing
//  above says whether they predict anything. Every figure in EVIDENCE was
//  measured on the Conviction trace; Samanvaya's on the Value trace's level.
//
//  ═══ READING THE TAPES WITH THE PANE ══════════════════════════════════════
//  All four tapes use the pane's colour language, so nothing new is learned:
//  green and red are direction, INTENSITY is how far it has run, pale is
//  weak.
//
//  MTF CONVICTION TAPE (+82) - WHO CONTROLS. Pragati's own ladder. Hue: which
//      side controls across the frames. Intensity: how far that control has
//      run, graded on the conviction zones - faint inside the inner zone, a
//      clear step brighter past it, solid past the outer zone. With Ladder up
//      it is the tide the pane's signals swim in; with Ladder down (default)
//      it is the current inside each bar - faster than chart conviction.
//
//          ◆ needs it past the inner zone on the ◆'s side
//          ▲▼ need it on the signal's side, or turning toward it
//          R needs it to fail to confirm the swing, as conviction did
//
//  MTF VALUE TAPE (−82) - WHERE PRICE STANDS. Samanvaya's MTF bias, carried in
//      whole (section 4c): its RV + breadth composite on the chart and on every
//      rung, averaged across the ladder. Hue: GREEN = RICH, RED = CHEAP. That is
//      Samanvaya's colouring FLIPPED on purpose: a rally makes price rich AND
//      conviction positive, so both tapes now turn green together, and a split
//      in hue between them is the thing the eye catches. Intensity: faint inside
//      θ entry, a clear step brighter at it, solid toward ±70 on Samanvaya's
//      scale. The frames must agree for it to brighten - a bright value tape is
//      a dislocation every horizon sees, not one.
//
//          both tapes SOLID, same hue   control that has already been paid for
//                                       across horizons - stretched, not early
//          conviction bright, value     control building while price is still
//            pale or opposite           fair or on the other side of it
//          ▲ after a SOLID RED tape     a BUY declared out of a dislocation
//          ▼ after a SOLID GREEN tape   every horizon saw - what ▲▼ require
//
//  MTF CONVICTION MOMENTUM (+90) - THE LEAD. Dhṛti's momentum tape: the
//      faster view of conviction minus the slower, in the tape's own points.
//      Hue: which way the faster view is pulling from the slower. Lightness:
//      the pane's own three column tiers - near-solid while the pull grows
//      AND is past its own impulse threshold, moderate while it grows below
//      it, pale while it shrinks. Dhṛti drew it at −82 until Samanvaya's
//      value tape took that place; it now sits above the conviction tape it
//      belongs to.
//
//          LADDER DOWN   the bar's insides minus the bar. Bright against
//                        chart conviction's side is the inside of the bars
//                        turning before the chart does - the early read for
//                        a turn the chart has not made yet.
//                        Label: BUILDING / TURNING INSIDE.
//          LADDER UP     the chart minus the frames that contain it. Bright
//                        in the conviction tape's hue: the chart running
//                        ahead of its ladder. Bright against it: a pullback
//                        in progress. Label: RUNNING AHEAD / PULLBACK.
//
//  MTF VALUE MOMENTUM (−90) - THE PULL. Samanvaya's momentum tape, carried
//      exactly: the faster view of value minus the slower, on the same three
//      tiers. Coloured as the value tape is - GREEN toward rich, RED toward
//      cheap. WIDENING when it agrees with the value tape: the faster frames
//      are stretching the dislocation. REVERTING when it opposes it: the fast
//      end has already turned back toward fair value - the timing a turn out
//      of a dislocation wants. Ladder down's value rungs are breadth only, so
//      there it is partly a LEG difference (breadth against the composite),
//      not only a horizon difference.
//
//  Every signal ENFORCES both level tapes (see THE SIGNALS); the tapes are
//  drawn so the reading behind a marker can be checked at a glance. The
//  momentum tapes are context: no signal reads them, so switching either off
//  (each has its own switch under 2 · MTF tapes) changes nothing that fires.
//  None of it is measured.
//
//  ═══ TWO DIRECTIONS, ONE SETTING PER TAPE ════════════════════════════════
//  Each tape has its own ladder input, so they can read opposite directions.
//
//  MTF CONVICTION · "MTF conviction ladder" (Core) · default LADDER DOWN.
//      Down asks whether the chart's conviction is BUILT from the horizons
//      inside it: every chart bar opened up at each finer resolution. Up asks
//      whether it is HELD by the horizons that contain it.
//  MTF VALUE · "MTF value ladder" (Value ladder · Samanvaya) · default LADDER
//      UP, Samanvaya's own default. Up carries the WHOLE composite - RV leg and
//      breadth - on every higher frame, so it asks whether the dislocation
//      stands on the horizons that contain the chart. Down is breadth only.
//
//  The defaults therefore read THREE HORIZONS: conviction from INSIDE the bar,
//  the chart itself (trace, histogram, Samanvaya's value on this chart), and
//  value from ABOVE it. Every lower frame's window is shorter than the chart's
//  and every higher frame's longer, so a ladder direction is also a SPEED:
//  down is faster than chart conviction, up is slower. The signal rules say
//  what each condition means in both directions (THE SIGNALS).
//
//  FALLBACK. A direction with no frames on this chart - Ladder up on 4h, 45m
//  or monthly, Ladder down on 1m - reads the other direction instead of pausing
//  every signal, and the tape's label shows ↺.
//
//  ═══ THE LADDER ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  A FIXED LADDER OF STANDARD FRAMES, the same on every venue. A frame is used
//  only if the larger of the two holds a whole number of the smaller's bars -
//  a reconstruction that split bars across bars would be inventing data.
//
//      chart   LADDER DOWN                                LADDER UP
//      3m      1m · 3m                                    4h · 1h · 30m · 15m · 3m
//      5m      1m · 5m                                    4h · 1h · 30m · 15m · 5m
//      15m     1m · 3m · 5m · 15m                         4h · 1h · 30m · 15m
//      30m     1m · 3m · 5m · 15m · 30m                   4h · 1h · 30m
//      1h      1m · 3m · 5m · 15m · 30m · 1h              4h · 1h
//      4h      1m · 3m · 5m · 15m · 30m · 1h · 4h         none
//      D       1m · 3m · 5m · 15m · 30m · 1h · 4h · D     W · D
//      W       1h · 4h · D · W                            none
//
//  1m has nothing below it; 4h and weekly nothing above within the ladder's
//  ceiling. A direction with no rungs falls back to the other. Weekly and
//  monthly looking down stop short of the finest frames: TradingView caps the
//  intrabars one request carries, and a 1m rung under a weekly bar would cover
//  only its last few months.
//
//  THE VALUE TAPE KEEPS SAMANVAYA'S OWN RUNGS, on its own input. Up: the same
//  frames as above, plus M · W on a weekly chart. Down: only the THREE frames
//  nearest below the chart (daily reads 30m · 1h · 4h · D), breadth leg only -
//  the RV leg spends 23 data requests and cannot be re-run per rung, and each
//  lower rung is seven full MSF engines running on intrabars.
//
//  SESSION ENDS. 1h and 4h do not divide every session - on NSE the last 1h bar
//  runs 15:15-15:30. Every frame's real bar boundaries are read from that frame
//  itself, and participation weighting mutes a short bar that carried little.
//
//  EQUAL WEIGHT PER RUNG. The standard frames are not evenly spaced, so the tape
//  leans toward wherever the rungs are denser. That is the price of a fixed,
//  familiar ladder, stated rather than hidden. Rungs are not independent
//  evidence: a 5m window sits inside the 4h one. Pramana does the whitened
//  composite; this does structure.
//
//  ═══ HOW A LOWER FRAME IS READ ════════════════════════════════════════════
//  Sampling a lower frame at the chart bar's close would describe only its last
//  few bars - the final twenty minutes of a day, on a 1m rung. So each lower
//  frame runs the full engine on its own history, and the chart bar takes the
//  participation-weighted AVERAGE of that frame's reading across all of its bars
//  inside the chart bar: the bar seen from inside. request.security_lower_tf
//  delivers every intrabar of every closed bar and the intrabars so far on the
//  live one - no repaint. Its depth is capped (about 100,000 intrabars per
//  request on most plans), so the finest rungs reach back less far than the
//  chart; older bars run without them.
//
//  ═══ HOW A HIGHER FRAME IS READ ═══════════════════════════════════════════
//  A higher frame read the usual way is a staircase, or it repaints. So each is
//  RECONSTRUCTED on the chart: from the parent comes only its SETTLED state as
//  of its last closed bar - rolling sums, participation baselines, the sums
//  behind its σ, its last close - and from the chart comes the parent bar now
//  FORMING, aggregated from the chart's own bars. The parent's arithmetic is
//  finished every chart bar, lands on the parent's settled value at its close,
//  and is identical in history and realtime. The Data Window's reconstruction-
//  error row reports how closely it landed, in points.
//
//  ═══ HOW THE TAPES ARE BUILT ══════════════════════════════════════════════
//      conviction  z = raw / 3σ per rung, σ over its own window
//                  100 · tanh( mean of z over the ladder, the chart included )
//      value       z = Samanvaya's unified z per rung (RV ⊕ breadth, blended
//                      in z-space with the leg correlation measured per rung)
//                  100 · softbound( 0.5 · mean of z over the ladder, chart included )
//      momentum    faster − slower, per instrument, in its tape's own points
//                      ladder down   tape − the chart's own reading
//                      ladder up     the chart's own reading − tape
//                  conviction: the chart's reading is chart conviction -
//                      the Conviction trace, whichever is drawn; impulse at
//                      k·σ of its own history over the normalization window,
//                      the k the pane's trigger uses (Dhṛti)
//                  value: the chart's reading is Samanvaya's value on this
//                      chart; impulse at 0.5σ over 200 bars (Samanvaya)
//
//  Both are fused in linear z-space and compressed once, with no second
//  normalization, so agreement across frames is RARER than any one frame's
//  reading - a bright tape means the ladder is stretched, not one horizon. A
//  rung whose history begins later joins when it can calibrate and moves the
//  level, never the scale. The value tape also needs Samanvaya's model built -
//  ten anchor periods past a twelve-period hold-out - before it leaves grey.
//  A momentum tape needs no request of its own: both sides of its difference
//  are already on the chart, the same quantity on the same scale.
//
//  VALUE ON EVERY TIMEFRAME. Samanvaya's model takes one observation per closed
//  anchor period (a day, by default). Each observation is now the SUM of the
//  chart-bar returns inside that period - the period's own return - for the
//  target and every driver alike. It used to be the return of the period's LAST
//  chart bar, which is the period's return only on a daily chart: on a 5m chart
//  the whole macro fit ran on one closing 5-minute return a day, the hedge's
//  measured skill collapsed, and value carried no macro information. The
//  intercept is now spread over the bars a period actually holds. The panel's
//  'macro share' row shows how much of value is relative value on this chart.
//  samanvaya.pine carries the same correction.
//
//  ═══ PRESENTATION ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  The HISTOGRAM is the primary read; the trace is context; the tapes are the
//  frame around both. Every visual answers ONE question on ONE channel:
//
//      column hue         which way the trace is being PUSHED
//      column lightness   whether that push is pressing or fading
//      column saturation  whether the reading is real or an amplified quiet
//      pane background    the standing ▲▼ declaration (opt-in)
//      trace intensity    how far the trace has run, on its rails - dimmed
//                         while a blend's ingredients disagree
//      trace side of 0    Conviction trace: WHO IS IN CONTROL on this chart;
//                         the others: which way price is stretched
//      MTF conviction     who controls across the ladder, and how far
//      MTF value          rich or cheap across the ladder, and how far
//      MTF momentum ×2    which way, and how hard, the faster frames lead
//
//  HUE IS NOT CONTROL. A column is the trace minus its own signal line, so on
//  the Conviction trace a green column with the trace at -45 means sellers
//  are still in control but their grip is easing. Section 9 has the detail.
//
//  GOLD, everywhere in this script, means one thing: READ WITH CAUTION. It is a
//  qualifier - warming up, a setup without evidence, a capped participation
//  weight, a quiet regime, a self-limiting flow, a structural OI jump, a crowded
//  book, a grid row held against its tape, an unaccepted price. It never
//  means up or down. The tapes use no gold: a tape that is still warming is
//  drawn in neutral grey.
//
//  VIEW: CORE shows the instrument and its tapes. FULL adds, on the price
//  chart, WHERE (the volume profile) and the readout: WHAT KIND (open
//  interest) and HOW MUCH (the Conviction-Value Grid).
//
//  ═══ THE READOUT · OPEN INTEREST AND THE CONVICTION-VALUE GRID ═══════════
//  One table on the price chart, a row per layer and a column per question,
//  so every column means the same thing on every row:
//
//             STATE          WHY                          PUSH   CHART  SPAN
//      OI     Long build-up  new longs · book 62 pct      +1.8σ         3d
//      CVG    Watch 1.25     sellers pausing · turning up ↑      ↑ Add  6d
//
//  Each cell is coloured by its own meaning: the state by the side it works,
//  push and chart by their direction, caution gold, the rest neutral. A Trim
//  into a push ↑ reads as a red Trim and a green ↑ - which is the reading.
//
//  CVG - Pragyam's portfolio style, read for this chart. Its 3 × 3 FAMILIES:
//  rows by the conviction tape against the inner zone, columns by the value
//  tape against θ, each named for what it says to do, at its own units - so
//  the word is the weight:
//
//                 CHEAP            FAIR          RICH
//      UP         Buy 3            Add 3         Hold 1.5
//      FAINT      Accumulate 1.5   Wait 1        Trim 0.75
//      DOWN       Watch 1          Reduce 0.5    Exit 0.25
//
//  Every name is held: the action says how much, never whether.
//      STATE  the action, and the units Pragyam sizes from - the 5 × 5 cell
//             shaded by how intensely each tape is drawn.
//      WHY    what separates the 5 × 5 cell from its neighbours: each
//             momentum tape's phase on its own edge. Buyers or sellers
//             RUNNING confirm the row; PAUSING (a pullback) leaves it
//             half-way to the middle. TURNING UP from cheap or DOWN from
//             rich confirms the column; STILL CHEAPENING or RICHENING
//             (widening) leaves it half-way. HELD, in gold: the tape has
//             left the row and the histogram has not confirmed a push that
//             way.
//      PUSH   conviction's own histogram as it runs the rows - the one
//             Pragyam reads, the pane's only on the Conviction trace: ↑↑ an
//             impulse, ↑ a push, TURNING or QUIET no push (the row holds),
//             ↓, ↓↓. A row moves only on a push toward its tape.
//      CHART  where this chart alone would put the name: its conviction and
//             Samanvaya's value on this chart, on the tapes' cuts. ↑ when
//             that family carries more units than the state's - the chart
//             has moved before the ladder; ↓ fewer.
//      SPAN   how long the name has stood in this action.
//  READ THE PUSH AGAINST THE ACTION - a reading, not a rule. Buy, Add and
//  Accumulate are best done on a push ↑; Trim, Reduce and Exit now, or into
//  a push ↑; Hold, Wait and Watch change nothing - the push says which way
//  the row may move next.
//
//  OI - the book on this bar: its character, who is doing it and how full
//  the book is (gold from the 80th percentile: crowded), the size of the
//  change in σ, and how long the character has held.
//
//  Display only: nothing on the pane reads either row. Pragyam reads D · W -
//  on a daily chart set both ladders to Ladder up to match it.
//
//  ═══ THE SIGNALS · THREE FAMILIES ══════════════════════════════════════════
//  INDICATOR SIGNALS, NOT TRADES. A ▲▼ is a DECLARATION about the market at
//  that bar; it stands until the opposite one and has no exit. Every signal
//  needs every one of its layers; nothing is optional:
//
//      CHART        this timeframe: the trace and its histogram, the rails,
//                   conviction's control and pivots, the impulse,
//                   Samanvaya's value on this chart, effort → result
//      CONVICTION   the +82 tape, C
//      VALUE        the −82 tape, V - rich (+) or cheap (−), judged against θ
//
//  1 · DECLARATION  ▲ ▼   Samanvaya's triangle, read through all of Pragati
//      ARM        the trace crosses UP through −θ with the value basket
//                 settled - on the Conviction trace, Samanvaya's value on
//                 this chart does. Falling back below −θ cancels the arm.
//      CONFIRM    within the confirmation window, on one closed bar:
//        VALUE · LADDER    V ≤ −θ inside the dislocation window, V < +θ now
//        PUSH · CHART      the trace's histogram > 0
//        CONV · LADDER     C > 0, or C rising two bars running
//        EFFORT → RESULT   participation absorbed at some point in the
//                          dislocation window (switchable)
//      In one line: cheap across the frames, effort exhausted, and buyers
//      starting to do the work - on this chart and on the ladder.
//      Arm-then-confirm replaced a same-bar rule that lost every ▲▼ whose
//      layers turned one bar apart.
//
//  2 · CONTINUATION  ◆   the pullback reset value without breaking the push
//      CHART        conviction > 0; the trace's histogram went < 0 inside
//                   the pullback window, then crosses +k·σ; participation
//                   NOT absorbed on the bar
//      CONVICTION   C ≥ +inner zone
//      VALUE        V < +θ
//
//  3 · DIVERGENCE  R H   the chart's pivot pattern, confirmed by the tapes -
//                  found on CONVICTION's pivots whichever trace is drawn
//      R bearish    price higher high, conviction lower high (zone gate
//                   on); C also a lower high; V > 0 at the pivot
//      H bearish    price lower high, conviction higher high; C ≤ −inner
//                   zone; V > −θ
//
//  WHAT C MEANS FOLLOWS ITS DIRECTION - the rules were checked for both:
//      LADDER DOWN (default)  C is the inside of the bars, faster than chart
//          conviction. ▲ 'C rising' = the inside of the bars turning first.
//          ◆ 'C ≥ inner zone' = the push BUILT at every finer frame, not one
//          print. R 'C a lower high' = the second high carried less
//          conviction inside the bars too - partly a restatement of chart
//          conviction, the chart's own reading being one of C's rungs. H =
//          the inside of the bars in control.
//      LADDER UP  C is the frames above, slower than chart conviction:
//          control HELD by the larger frames, and R the larger frames
//          failing to confirm.
//      With the defaults the view from above comes from V (value ↑), so every
//      signal spans three horizons: inside the bar, the chart, above it.
//
//  BREADTH STAYS INSIDE VALUE, by decision. It is momentum and overlaps C; the
//  panel's 'layer overlap' row measures by how much instead of assuming it
//  away. It stays because the blend is Samanvaya's measured best, because the
//  value ladder below the chart is breadth only, and because the anchor fix
//  (VALUE ON EVERY TIMEFRAME) gives value its macro information back.
//
//  Every bullish rule mirrors its bearish one. A COOLDOWN applies per
//  direction (▲ and long ◆ share one clock); a triangle takes precedence over
//  a diamond on the same bar.
//
//  NOTHING PAUSES SILENTLY. A tape warming or switched Off, no frames on this
//  chart after FALLBACK, or Samanvaya's model still building pauses every
//  signal, and a gold SIGNALS PAUSED label at the pane's right edge names the
//  layer. A ▲▼ also cannot arm while the basket settles after a rotation.
//
//  FREE PARAMETERS - none fitted for this stack: the trace's equal blend and
//  its ±60 outer rail; θ (1.5); the inner zone
//  reused as C's threshold (C averages several z's, so it reaches the zone less
//  often than the trace); 'rising two bars'; the dislocation window (20); the
//  confirmation window (5); the absorption cut (bottom fifth); V > 0 for R;
//  the cooldown (10); the pullback window (6).
//
//  UNMEASURED AS A STACK. Every figure in EVIDENCE below was measured on the
//  chart layer alone. Samanvaya's figures are the information coefficient of
//  its oscillator LEVEL on weekly data - not of the crossing as an event, and
//  not intraday.
//
//  ═══ HOW TO READ IT ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  ZERO LINE - on the Conviction trace, who is in control on this chart; on
//      the others, balanced - neither stretched up nor down, though a DIM
//      blend near zero is its ingredients cancelling. Context, not an event.
//
//  RAILS - on the Conviction trace, the zones: beyond the inner zone the
//      window's effort is heavily one-sided; beyond the outer it is
//      near-uniform, where trends run and also end. On the others θ is a
//      1.5σ stretch - where a ▲▼ can form as it releases - and ±60 a 3σ one.
//      A setting, never a signal.
//
//  DIVERGENCE - the primary edge in this construction.
//      REGULAR BEARISH   price higher high, conviction lower high    → reversal
//      REGULAR BULLISH   price lower low,   conviction higher low    → reversal
//      HIDDEN  BEARISH   price lower high,  conviction higher high   → continuation
//      HIDDEN  BULLISH   price higher low,  conviction lower low     → continuation
//
//  THE STRONGEST READING is a regular divergence formed in a zone, confirmed by
//  the ladder, followed by a ▲▼ in the same direction - the chart, the ladder's
//  conviction and value across horizons all turning together. Read, not
//  enforced: the R and the triangle are separate families.
//
//  WITH THE PROFILE ON, read the divergence against the structure too: a high in
//  a low-volume node is distribution into thin air; the same high at the point
//  of control was made where the market trades willingly. A reading only.
//
//  ═══ READING THE RAILS ════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//      TAPE          +90   MTF conviction momentum - faster minus slower
//      TAPE          +82   MTF conviction - control across the ladder
//      OUTER DASHED  ±60     divergences    R regular      H hidden
//      INNER DOTTED  ±30|θ   signals        ▲▼ declaration ◆ continuation
//      TAPE          −82   MTF value - rich (green) / cheap (red) across it
//      TAPE          −90   MTF value momentum - faster minus slower
//
//  Bearish events on the upper rail, bullish on the lower. Divergence markers
//  sit on the CONFIRMATION bar: nothing is drawn before it is known and nothing
//  is revised afterwards.
//
//  ═══ WHAT IT MEASURES ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  CONVICTION - how much of a bar's travel became net displacement:
//
//      c = ( C − C[1] ) / TR        bounded −1 … +1
//
//  PARTICIPATION - volume relative to its own recent average, capped:
//
//      w = min( V / EMA(V), cap )    relative TRUE RANGE where there is no volume
//
//  THE RAW SHARE, chosen by the Denominator input:
//
//      AGREEMENT   raw = 100 × Σ ( c · w ) / Σ ( |c| · w )
//      EFFORT      raw = 100 × Σ ( c · w ) / Σ w
//
//  AGREEMENT is Chande's form, the RSI family, on range-normalised closes
//  weighted by relative volume: how CONSISTENTLY bars resolve one way. EFFORT
//  divides by total participation - Wyckoff's law literally - so a heavy bar
//  that goes nowhere dilutes the reading. Every rung of the ladder uses the
//  same form, so the tapes and the pane are always the same quantity.
//
//  SCALING. conviction = 100 · tanh( raw / 3σ ), σ over the normalization
//  window: bounded, smooth, calibrated so the inner zone holds about a third of
//  the time and the outer about four percent (measured: 3.2-4.3%).
//
//  THE TRACE on its default blends this conviction, in σ, with Samanvaya's
//  value, in σ, and bounds the blend once on Samanvaya's scale (THE TRACE).
//
//  ═══ WHAT TO KNOW BEFORE LEANING ON IT ════════════════════════════════════
//  · PIVOTS CONFIRM LATE, BY CONSTRUCTION. Not repainting - the cost of knowing
//    a pivot was a pivot.
//  · SCALING IS RELATIVE. +60 is extreme FOR THIS INSTRUMENT over the window. In
//    a dead range small imbalances are amplified; the panel shows the raw share.
//  · CAPPING PARTICIPATION MATTERS. Expiry days, rebalances and block prints
//    would otherwise dominate a window for its whole length.
//  · THE WINDOW IS THE HORIZON. A 20-bar lookback is a swing instrument on daily
//    bars and a scalping one on 5-minute bars - which is what the tapes are for.
//  · SCALED, NOT CENTRED. Zero keeps meaning "neither side in control", so on a
//    persistently trending instrument one side's zone is visited more often.
//
//  ═══ EVIDENCE ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  Measured on the Conviction trace - the single-frame engine, published as
//  Nishchaya v3 - under the Agreement denominator, which is the default. The
//  tapes, the signal stack and the blended trace are unmeasured; so is any
//  signal read off the blended trace's histogram.
//
//  METHOD. Every signal entered at the NEXT bar's open, bracketed at two ATR of
//  target against one ATR of stop, twenty bars maximum, stop assumed first when a
//  bar spans both. Edge in R over a baseline that applies the identical bracket
//  to every bar in both directions. Fitted on the first 60% of each series,
//  tested on the last 40%, re-tested on eight instruments never used in fitting.
//  26 years of daily data, roughly 730 days of hourly, 60 days of 5- and
//  15-minute, on GC, SI, CL, HG, NG, ES, SPY, QQQ, TLT, 6E and BTC.
//
//  WHAT SURVIVED.
//      · Regular divergence RANKED first: the only element positive on both the
//        primary futures and the held-out instruments in nearly every
//        configuration tested. Ranked, not established.
//      · Participation weighting earns its place. Off is the worst setting in
//        all three signal architectures tested.
//      · The adaptive scaling is calibrated: 3.2-4.3% beyond the outer zone.
//
//  WHAT DID NOT.
//      · The chart-only reversal trigger had no edge (+0.0003R, t=0.01) - the
//        reason no ▲▼ fires on the chart layer alone.
//      · Hidden divergence measured no edge on any universe. Default-off.
//      · Continuation: +0.036R on the primary futures, -0.016R elsewhere.
//      · NOTHING reaches significance once overlapping windows are accounted
//        for. Best of 48 cells was t = 1.9 - what noise alone produces against
//        an expected null maximum near 2.2. The modern bar is t > 3 (Harvey, Liu
//        and Zhu 2016). What the evidence supports is "no demonstrated edge".
//
//  GLYPH INTENSITY FOLLOWS EVIDENCE WEIGHT. Every marker is sized
//  automatically; R, the measured element, and the ▲▼ are drawn solid, the
//  instrument-dependent ◆ and the edgeless H lighter.
//
//  ON OPTIMISING THE INPUTS. 900 randomised parameter sets: fitted edge and
//  unseen edge correlate at about zero. Lookback at or above 20 and the inner
//  zone at or below 40 were the only preferences that held. Do not tune this to
//  a backtest. It will not hold.
//
//  COSTS DECIDE THE TIMEFRAME. At one ATR of stop a round trip costs about 0.02R
//  daily, 0.045R hourly, 0.05R on 15m and 0.09R on 5m, against a best measured
//  edge near 0.05R. Daily is the only timeframe with real headroom - and the
//  reason to test whether the tapes help a lower-timeframe trader pick the
//  signals worth paying for.
//
//  ═══ REPAINT POSTURE ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  Trace, histogram and all four tapes are live readings: they move while the
//  chart bar forms and are final at its close. Higher frames enter only as their
//  last CLOSED bar plus chart bars that exist; lower frames only as intrabars
//  that exist. History and realtime compute the same numbers. Triggers,
//  divergence markers and connectors, the ▲▼ arms and declarations, their
//  background and all alerts are committed on closed bars only. The volume profile's developing
//  session is the one disclosed exception. The value tape inherits Samanvaya's
//  posture: its macro drivers use lookahead_off with a confirmed-bar fallback,
//  and its anchor samples come from CLOSED periods. DRIVER TIMING: on a daily
//  chart many drivers close hours after the chart does - TVC:US10Y about eleven
//  hours after NSE - so a driver whose bar closes more than a third of a bar
//  late is read at its previous close, the value known at the chart's close.
//  The panel's 'driver timing' row shows what was lagged and what lateness was
//  kept - the most the last bar can revise by.
//
//  ═══ DATA REQUESTS ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  Pine allows 40 unique requests. Samanvaya's engine spends 22 (the macro
//  symbols; Y is the chart's own close) on every bar; the rest depend on the
//  two ladder settings.
//  Conviction: up to 7 rungs down, 5 up. Value: up to 3 down, 5 up. They never
//  peak together - the most any chart carries across both is 10 (daily with
//  both down: 7 + 3; 1m with both up: 5 + 5; the defaults peak at 8). Plus 1
//  for the profile's intrabars and 4 for open interest. At most 37 execute on
//  any chart and any combination - close to the ceiling, so nothing more can
//  be requested here. FALLBACK swaps a direction for the other, never adds one.
//  The momentum tapes request nothing: each is a difference of two series the
//  chart already carries.
//
//  ═══ DRAWING BUDGETS ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  Divergence connectors - in the pane on the Conviction trace, on the price
//  chart on the others - are capped at sixty and recycled explicitly, so the
//  profile's level lines cannot evict them. The naked-POC ledger is capped by
//  input. Plot series: 64 allowed, and a series colour costs two - which is why
//  both continuation markers share one plot. alertcondition() counts too.
//  THE PLOT BUDGET IS FULL: 64 of 64 with the momentum tapes, so their
//  readings live in the right-edge labels and the panel, not in the Data
//  Window. Anything new that plots must first free a count. The trace's
//  choice costs none: one trace is drawn, whichever it is.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// NAMING. The line runs Siddhi → Nishchaya → Dhṛti → Pragati. Siddhi claimed
// attainment, a result. Nishchaya named what one window measures: resolve.
// Dhṛti named what the ladder tests: whether that resolve holds - the firmness
// that sustains an action to its end. Pragati names what all of it measures at
// the root - c = ΔC / TR, how much of a bar's travel became progress - and asks
// whether that progress is carried on every timeframe and paid for in value.
// Published as Dhṛti until this rename; the engine is unchanged.
// max_bars_back=2500 is Samanvaya's, carried in with its engine (section 4c).
indicator("Pragati · Multi-Timeframe Conviction Oscillator", shorttitle="Pragati", overlay=false,
     precision=1, max_bars_back=2500, max_lines_count=500, max_labels_count=100, max_boxes_count=500)

// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  INPUTS
// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
var string G_E  = "1 · Conviction engine"
var string G_M  = "2 · Trace and MTF tapes"
var string G_S  = "3 · Value model · Samanvaya"
var string G_T  = "4 · Signals · ▲ ▼ ◆"
var string G_V  = "5 · Divergence · R H"
var string G_Z  = "6 · Zones"
var string G_D  = "7 · Display"
var string G_P  = "8 · Information panel"

// ── 1 · conviction engine: the pane's own measurement ───────────────────────
int    inpLen  = input.int(20, "Lookback", minval=3, maxval=200, group=G_E, tooltip="How many bars of effort the oscillator accounts for. This is the horizon of the whole instrument.\n\nShorter turns faster and produces more signals, most of them noise. Longer produces fewer and better ones but sits at extremes for long stretches during trends.\n\nMatch it to your holding period.", display=display.none)
int    inpSm   = input.int(3, "Smoothing", minval=1, maxval=20, group=G_E, tooltip="Final EMA on the oscillator. Its real job is the divergence engine: pivots detected on a jagged series produce spurious highs and lows and therefore spurious divergences.\n\n1 disables it. Above about 5 the pivots start arriving materially late.", display=display.none)
int    inpSig  = input.int(9, "Signal EMA", minval=1, maxval=50, group=G_E, tooltip="The reference line. The histogram is the gap between the trace and this.\n\nA plain crossing of these two is NOT a signal here - two lines near zero cross endlessly. The ◆ needs the gap to cross a magnitude threshold measured in its own standard deviations, after a genuine pullback; the ▲▼ read only which side of zero the gap is on.", display=display.none)
// The "Raw share" scaling option is GONE, and its removal is a fix rather than a
// simplification. Selecting it left the zones at ±30/±60 on a series that lives
// inside roughly ±15, so nothing ever armed, no gated divergence passed, and the
// reversal trigger could not fire - three of the four signal channels went silent
// with nothing on the pane to say why. Its stated purpose was to sanity-check the
// adaptive reading against absolute conviction, and the DETAIL panel's
// "raw share · σ" row already does exactly that without disabling anything. A
// setting whose only other effect is to break the engine is a trap, not a choice.
int    inpNorm = input.int(200, "Normalization window", minval=30, maxval=1000, group=G_E, tooltip="Sample used for the scaling σ and for the trigger's magnitude threshold.\n\nLonger is stabler and slower to acknowledge that the instrument has changed character. Shorter adapts quickly but makes both the zones and the trigger threshold move under your feet.", display=display.none)
string inpPart = input.string("Auto", "Participation", options=["Auto", "Volume", "True range", "Off"], group=G_E, tooltip="What weights each bar's conviction.\n\nAUTO uses volume where the symbol has it and relative true range where it does not, so index spot works without configuration.\n\nVOLUME is the intended input - it is what makes effort-without-result detectable at all.\n\nTRUE RANGE is the fallback: a bar that covered unusual ground counts for more. Weaker, because a wide range is already partly in the conviction term.\n\nOFF weights every bar equally. Useful as a control - if the reading barely changes when you switch participation off, volume is telling you nothing on this instrument.\n\nUNDER THE AGREEMENT DENOMINATOR, participation only re-weights up-bars against down-bars: if every bar in the window closes the same way, the weights cancel and volume drops out entirely. Under EFFORT it also dilutes bars that went nowhere.", display=display.none)
string inpDen  = input.string("Agreement", "Denominator", options=["Agreement", "Effort"], group=G_E, tooltip="What the signed, participation-weighted result is divided by.\n\nAGREEMENT (default, and the only form the published evidence was measured on): Σ|c|·w. Reads how consistently bars resolve one way. A bar that goes nowhere drops out of both sides, so heavy volume with no progress is INVISIBLE to it.\n\nEFFORT: Σw, total participation. Wyckoff's law literally - heavy bars that go nowhere dilute the reading toward zero, the way Twiggs and Chaikin money flow divide by total volume. Untested: measure it on your own universe before relying on it.\n\nThe zones, triggers and divergence engine run unchanged on either; the adaptive scaling recalibrates automatically. The panel's effort → result row shows the absorption reading under both.", display=display.none)
int    inpVN   = input.int(20, "Participation baseline", minval=5, maxval=200, group=G_E, tooltip="Averaging length for the volume or range baseline. Each bar is weighted by its participation relative to this.", display=display.none)
float  inpCap  = input.float(3.0, "Participation cap", minval=1.0, maxval=10.0, step=0.5, group=G_E, tooltip="Ceiling on the participation weight.\n\nExpiry days, index rebalances and block prints generate volume many multiples of normal. Uncapped, a single print would dominate the entire window for its full length and the oscillator would be reporting that one bar.", display=display.none)

// ── 2 · the trace, and which ladder each tape reads ─────────────────────────
string inpTrace = input.string("Conviction × Value", "Trace", options=["Conviction × Value", "Conviction", "Value"], group=G_M, tooltip="WHAT THE PANE DRAWS. The histogram is always this trace minus its own signal EMA.\n\nCONVICTION × VALUE (default) - how far the move is STRETCHED on this chart, in one-sided effort and in price against fair value: conviction in σ and Samanvaya's value in σ, blended equally, their measured correlation restoring the blend's variance, and bounded once on Samanvaya's scale - Samanvaya's own recipe for its two legs, one level up. Read against θ (±43 at the default) and ±60, a 3σ stretch. It DIMS while its two ingredients point opposite ways - accumulation or distribution, not quiet - and while the value basket settles.\n\nCONVICTION - the single-frame engine alone, bit-identical to Nishchaya v3: every number in the evidence was measured on it, and with it the script reads exactly as it did before this setting. Read against the conviction zones.\n\nVALUE - Samanvaya's value on this chart alone, read against θ.\n\nWHAT FOLLOWS THE TRACE: the columns and their tiers, the impulse gate, the ◆ pullback and resume, the ▲▼ push and - when the trace carries value - the ▲▼ arm, the rails and the panel's headline. WHAT STAYS ON CONVICTION: the conviction ladder's chart rung and momentum tape, control for a ◆, the divergence pivots (off the Conviction trace their connectors move to the price chart), the quiet regime, and the Conviction-Value Grid, which reads conviction's histogram as Pragyam does.\n\nThe blend is unmeasured.", display=display.none)
string inpTape = input.string("Ladder down", "MTF conviction ladder", options=["Ladder down", "Ladder up", "Off"], group=G_M, tooltip="Which ladder of timeframes feeds the MTF CONVICTION tape (+82) - Pragati's own - and its MOMENTUM tape (+90). The MTF VALUE tapes have their own setting just below.\n\nLADDER DOWN (default) - every standard frame BELOW the chart: 15m reads 1m · 3m · 5m · 15m, daily reads 1m through 4h, then D. Each lower frame is its conviction averaged across its own bars inside the chart bar, weighted by participation. Every lower frame's window is SHORTER than the chart's, so this is a FASTER read than chart conviction: whether the chart's conviction is BUILT from the bars inside it - it sees a turn inside the bars before the chart does.\n\nLADDER UP - the chart and the standard frames ABOVE it, up to 4h: 5m reads 4h · 1h · 30m · 15m · 5m, daily reads W · D. A SLOWER read: whether the conviction is HELD by the frames that contain the chart.\n\nThe signal rules are the same in both directions; what each condition MEANS follows the direction, and the header's SIGNALS section spells out both.\n\nFALLBACK - if the chosen direction has no frames on this chart (Ladder up on 4h or weekly, Ladder down on 1m), the other direction is read and the tape's label shows ↺. Nothing pauses silently.\n\nOFF - no conviction tapes. Every signal needs both level tapes, so with this Off no signal can fire.\n\nUnmeasured.", display=display.none)
bool   inpMomC = input.bool(true, "    Conviction momentum tape  (+90)", group=G_M, tooltip="MTF CONVICTION MOMENTUM (+90) - Dhṛti's momentum tape, on the conviction ladder above: the faster view of conviction minus the slower, in the tape's own points.\n\nLADDER DOWN - the bar's insides minus the bar. Bright against chart conviction's side is the inside of the bars turning before the chart does (TURNING INSIDE); with it, BUILDING INSIDE.\nLADDER UP - the chart minus the frames that contain it. Bright in the conviction tape's hue is the chart RUNNING AHEAD; against it, a PULLBACK in progress.\n\nShaded on the pane's own three column tiers: near-solid while the pull grows past k·σ of its own history, moderate while it grows below it, pale while it shrinks.\n\nContext only - no signal reads it, so switching it off changes nothing that fires. It requests no data. Unmeasured.", display=display.none)
string inpVLad = input.string("Ladder up", "MTF value ladder", options=["Ladder up", "Ladder down", "Off"], group=G_M, tooltip="Which ladder of timeframes feeds the MTF VALUE tape (−82) and its MOMENTUM tape (−90) - Samanvaya's MTF bias and momentum, with Samanvaya's own rung choice.\n\nLADDER UP (default, as in Samanvaya) - the chart and the standard frames above it, 4h the intraday ceiling: 5m reads 4h · 1h · 30m · 15m · 5m, daily reads W · D, weekly reads M · W. Every higher rung is the WHOLE composite: the RV leg exactly and the breadth leg at its last closed bar. A SLOWER read: where the frames that contain the chart say price stands.\n\nLADDER DOWN - the three standard frames nearest below the chart, breadth leg only.\n\nWith the defaults the stack reads THREE HORIZONS: inside the bar (conviction ↓), the chart itself, and the frames above it (value ↑).\n\nFALLBACK - if the chosen direction has no frames on this chart (Ladder up on 4h, 45m or monthly), the other direction is read and the label shows ↺.\n\nOFF - no value tapes; Samanvaya's chart-frame engine still runs. Every signal needs both level tapes, so with this Off no signal can fire.\n\nColours as set: GREEN = rich, RED = cheap.", display=display.none)
bool   inpMomV = input.bool(true, "    Value momentum tape  (−90)", group=G_M, tooltip="MTF VALUE MOMENTUM (−90) - Samanvaya's momentum tape, on the value ladder above: the faster view of value minus the slower. Coloured as the value tape: GREEN toward rich, RED toward cheap.\n\nWIDENING when it agrees with the value tape - the faster frames are stretching the dislocation. REVERTING when it opposes it - the fast end has already turned back toward fair value. On Ladder down the value rungs are breadth only, so there it is partly breadth against the composite, not only fast against slow.\n\nShaded on three tiers as Samanvaya shades it: near-solid while the pull grows past 0.5σ of its own 200-bar history, moderate while it grows below it, pale while it shrinks.\n\nContext only - no signal reads it, so switching it off changes nothing that fires. It requests no data. Unmeasured.", display=display.none)

// ── 3 · value model: Samanvaya's own inputs, the ones that move its composite ─
string hedgeMode = input.string("Auto (measured)", "Macro hedge", options=["Auto (measured)", "Off", "Full"], group=G_S, tooltip="How much of the macro hedge to apply in the RV leg.\n\nAUTO scales it by the hedge's own rolling out-of-sample R², so the instrument decides. Across 24 targets this beat a full hedge on both mean and median.\n\nOFF removes the macro layer - the RV leg becomes Y's own cumulative return.\n\nFULL forces the complete hedge regardless of measured skill. Correct when you know the relationship holds.", display=display.none)
string anchorSel = input.string("Daily (works on every timeframe)", "Model clock", options=["Daily (works on every timeframe)", "Weekly (stronger, needs daily+ charts)"], group=G_S, tooltip="Clock on which the RV leg's driver selection and hedge ratios are estimated, then applied to the chart.\n\nDAILY works everywhere. WEEKLY measured better (mean IC +0.060 vs +0.047) but needs a daily-or-higher chart: on a 5-minute chart there are too few weekly boundaries to build anything.", display=display.none)
float  legMix    = input.float(0.5, "Leg mix · RV ← → breadth", minval=0.0, maxval=1.0, step=0.05, group=G_S, tooltip="Weight on the RELATIVE VALUE leg. 0.5 is the equal integration Samanvaya measured best. 1.0 is the RV leg alone, 0.0 the breadth leg alone. With the value ladder on Ladder down the rungs are breadth only whatever this is set to; the chart rung always uses the mix.\n\nBREADTH STAYS IN BY DESIGN. The breadth leg is momentum and overlaps the conviction tape - the panel's 'layer overlap' row shows by how much. It stays because the equal blend is Samanvaya's measured best (+0.0691 against +0.0637 for the RV leg alone), the only measured part of the value side; because below the chart the value ladder is breadth only, so removing it would leave Ladder down with no ladder; and because the anchor fix gives the RV leg its macro information back on every timeframe, so value is no longer momentum alone. Set 1.0 to see value without momentum.", display=display.none)
float  entryThresh = input.float(1.5, "θ · value threshold", minval=0.5, maxval=4.0, step=0.1, group=G_S, tooltip="Samanvaya\'s entry threshold θ, in |z|. It is the level Samanvaya\'s value reading must cross back through for a ▲▼, the dislocation level the MTF value tape is judged against by ▲▼, ◆ and H, and the knee of the value tape\'s shading - faint inside θ, a clear step brighter at it, solid toward ±70.\n\nLiterature standard for a mean-reversion entry is 1.5 to 2.5.", display=display.none)
bool   inpCustom = input.bool(false, "Add a custom macro driver", group=G_S, tooltip="Adds your own symbol as a ninth candidate factor in the RV leg. Never the target's own volatility index - it would hedge the target against itself.", display=display.none)
string usy_sym   = input.symbol("", "    Custom driver symbol", group=G_S, tooltip="Leave empty unless you have a specific economic reason.", display=display.none)

// ── 4 · signals ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
bool   inpState = input.bool(true, "Declarations  ▲ ▼", group=G_T, tooltip="The ▲▼ DECLARATIONS - Samanvaya's triangle, read through everything Pragati knows. A ▲ declares BUY, a ▼ declares SELL; a declaration stands until the opposite one. It is a statement about the market at that bar, not a position, and it has no exit.\n\nHOW A ▲ IS MADE (▼ mirrors):\n  1 · ARM      the trace crosses back UP through −θ with the value basket settled - the stretch releasing. On the Conviction trace it is Samanvaya's value reading on this chart that crosses - price leaving a dislocation. A fall back below −θ cancels the arm; the next crossing re-arms it.\n  2 · CONFIRM  inside the confirmation window, on one closed bar, all of:\n     VALUE · LADDER       the value tape was cheap past θ inside the dislocation window, and is not rich past θ now\n     PUSH · CHART         the trace's histogram is above zero\n     CONVICTION · LADDER  the conviction tape is above zero or has risen two bars running\n     EFFORT → RESULT      participation was ABSORBED at some point in the dislocation window - effort that stopped producing result, the exhaustion the turn comes out of (switchable below)\n\nArm-then-confirm replaced a same-bar rule that lost every ▲ whose layers turned one bar apart. Unmeasured as a stack: neither the crossing as an event nor the confirmations has been tested.", display=display.none)
int    inpConfirm = input.int(5, "Confirmation window", minval=1, maxval=50, group=G_T, tooltip="For ▲▼: how many bars, counting the crossing bar, the other layers have to confirm Samanvaya's crossing.\n\nValue on this chart and effort usually turn a bar or two apart. Five bars absorbs that stagger while the turn is still the same event. 1 restores the old same-bar rule.\n\nA chosen number, not a fitted one.", display=display.none)
int    inpDisloc = input.int(20, "Dislocation window", minval=3, maxval=200, group=G_T, tooltip="For ▲▼: how far back the MTF value tape may have been past θ for the turn to count as a dislocation every horizon saw.\n\nThe chart's own value reading turns first; the ladder mean, which includes slower frames, is usually already easing by the time the chart crosses back through θ. This window lets the ladder's dislocation be recent rather than simultaneous.", display=display.none)
bool   inpCon  = input.bool(true, "Continuation  ◆", group=G_T, tooltip="CONTINUATION ◆ - control held, value reset, push resumed.\n\n◆ LONG (short mirrors) needs, on one closed bar:\n  CHART                this chart's conviction is above zero - control held - and the trace's histogram went below zero inside the pullback window and now crosses +k·σ\n  CONVICTION · LADDER  the conviction tape is past the inner zone on the buyers' side. With Ladder down (default) the push is BUILT inside the bars at every finer frame, not carried by one print; with Ladder up it is HELD by the frames above\n  VALUE · LADDER       the value tape is not rich past θ - the pullback left price fair or cheap; with the default Ladder up, on the frames above the chart\n  EFFORT → RESULT      participation is NOT absorbed on the bar - the push is producing result (switchable below)\n\nIn one line: the pullback reset value without breaking the push. The chart part alone measured +0.036R on metals and energy and -0.016R elsewhere; the stack is unmeasured.", display=display.none)
float  inpK    = input.float(0.5, "Impulse threshold (σ of histogram)", minval=0.0, maxval=3.0, step=0.1, group=G_T, tooltip="The histogram must cross ± this many standard deviations of its own distribution, rather than merely crossing zero. Used by the ◆, and it sets the brightest histogram tier.\n\nIn the earlier trigger design, 0 - the plain signal-line crossover - was the worst value of this setting out of sample across a 900-configuration search; that is why it is not 0. It has not been re-measured for the ◆ as it now stands.\n\nRaise it for fewer and stronger ◆. Above about 1.5 only violent expansions qualify.", display=display.none)
int    inpPull = input.int(6, "Pullback window", minval=2, maxval=50, group=G_T, tooltip="For continuation signals: how recently the histogram must have crossed to the wrong side for the move to count as a genuine pullback rather than an uninterrupted drift.", display=display.none)
bool   inpEff = input.bool(true, "Effort → result in signals", group=G_T, tooltip="The indicator's own thesis - is the move paid for - as a signal layer.\n\n▲▼ require that participation was ABSORBED (effort → result in the bottom fifth of its history) at some point in the dislocation window: heavy effort that stopped producing result, the exhaustion a turn comes out of.\n◆ requires that participation is NOT absorbed on the signal bar: the resuming push is producing result.\n\nA HYPOTHESIS, not a measured result. Switch it off to see the stack without it; comparing the two is how it should be judged.", display=display.none)
int    inpCool = input.int(10, "Cooldown (bars)", minval=0, maxval=200, group=G_T, tooltip="Minimum bars between signals in the same direction - ▲ and long ◆ share one clock, ▼ and short ◆ the other.\n\nWithout this, one choppy stretch produces a cluster of markers that all describe the same event. Raise it if the rails still look busy on your timeframe.", display=display.none)

// ── 5 · divergence ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
bool   inpDiv  = input.bool(true,  "Regular divergence  R", group=G_V, tooltip="DIVERGENCE FAMILY · REGULAR - the move is not confirmed by the chart OR by the ladder, at a price every horizon calls stretched.\n\nR BEARISH (bullish mirrors), judged at the confirming pivot:\n  CHART        price made a higher high while conviction made a lower high - on conviction's own pivots, whichever trace the pane draws; with the zone gate on, the earlier conviction high was above the inner zone.\n  CONVICTION   the ladder also made a lower high - its reading at this pivot is below its reading at the previous one. The whole ladder is failing to confirm, not one frame.\n  VALUE        the ladder is on the RICH side (> 0) at the pivot - the high was made above fair value across horizons.\n\nChart-only regular divergence was the one element positive almost everywhere it was measured; the tape conditions are unmeasured.", display=display.none)
bool   inpHid  = input.bool(false, "Hidden divergence  H", group=G_V, tooltip="DIVERGENCE FAMILY · HIDDEN - a pullback where the underlying control never left.\n\nH BEARISH (bullish mirrors), judged at the confirming pivot:\n  CHART        price made a lower high while conviction made a higher high - on its own pivots, whichever trace is drawn.\n  CONVICTION   the conviction tape is past the inner zone on the sellers' side at the pivot - inside the bars with Ladder down, the frames above with Ladder up.\n  VALUE        the value tape is NOT cheap past θ at the pivot - the bounce restored value inside that control.\n\nThe same thesis as the ◆, found at a pivot. Chart-only hidden divergence measured no edge on any universe; default off.", display=display.none)
int    inpPL   = input.int(5, "Pivot left", minval=1, maxval=30, group=G_V, tooltip="Bars of confirmation on the left of a pivot.", display=display.none)
int    inpPR   = input.int(5, "Pivot right", minval=1, maxval=30, group=G_V, tooltip="Bars of confirmation on the right of a pivot.\n\nTHIS IS THE LAG. A divergence cannot be known until this many bars have passed after the turn. Not repainting - the price of confirmation.", display=display.none)
int    inpGapM = input.int(5,  "Min pivot separation", minval=2, maxval=100, group=G_V, tooltip="Two pivots closer than this are the same swing and their comparison is meaningless.", display=display.none)
int    inpGapX = input.int(60, "Max pivot separation", minval=10, maxval=400, group=G_V, tooltip="Two pivots further apart than this belong to different market episodes. Comparing them produces impressive-looking lines that mean nothing.", display=display.none)
bool   inpGate = input.bool(true, "Zone gate on regular divergence", group=G_V, tooltip="Only accept a regular bearish divergence whose earlier pivot formed above the inner zone, and a regular bullish one whose earlier pivot formed below it.\n\nThe measurement is equivocal: the gate improves divergence quality on gold, silver and crude, and slightly REDUCES it on the eight held-out instruments, where ungated divergence scored better. It is kept on by default because the primary futures are what this indicator is tuned for.", display=display.none)
bool   inpLine = input.bool(true, "Draw divergence connectors", group=G_V, tooltip="The line joining a divergence's two pivots - in the pane, between the two conviction pivots, on the Conviction trace; on the other traces on the PRICE chart, between the two swing extremes, because the drawn trace is not the series the pivots were found on. Switch off to keep only the rail markers.\n\nCapped at sixty and recycled explicitly, so the profile's level lines and these cannot evict each other from a shared drawing budget.", display=display.none)

// ── 6 · zones ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
float  inpZ1   = input.float(30.0, "Inner zone", minval=5.0,  maxval=95.0, step=5.0, group=G_Z, tooltip="Where the window's effort has become heavily one-sided. Under adaptive scaling this is occupied roughly a third of the time.\n\nThis level gates divergences, sets the conviction tape's control threshold for ◆ and H, and - on the Conviction trace - carries the SIGNAL rail. On the other traces the inner rail is θ.", display=display.none)
float  inpZ2   = input.float(60.0, "Outer zone", minval=10.0, maxval=99.0, step=5.0, group=G_Z, tooltip="Near-uniform conviction, roughly four percent occupancy under adaptive scaling. Trends live here and so do their endings.\n\nOn the Conviction trace this level carries the DIVERGENCE rail; on the other traces the outer rail is ±60, a 3σ stretch.", display=display.none)

// ── 7 · display ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
string inpView = input.string("Full", "View", options=["Full", "Core"], group=G_D, tooltip="CORE is the instrument: histogram, trace, zones, markers and the ladder tapes. The volume profile, the readout (open interest and the grid) and the gold cast stand down - and the profile stops being COMPUTED, not just hidden.\n\nFULL adds the context layers on the price chart: WHERE participation happened (profile) and the readout - WHAT KIND it was (open interest) and HOW MUCH Pragyam's grid would hold (the Conviction-Value Grid).\n\nFull is the default. Core is the lighter view when you want the pane alone. Nothing in the engine or the signals changes either way.", display=display.none)
string inpPlace = input.string("Threshold rails", "Marker placement", options=["Threshold rails", "On the oscillator"], group=G_D, tooltip="THRESHOLD RAILS stamps every event on a fixed level - divergences on the outer dashed lines, triggers on the inner dotted lines - with bearish above and bullish below, so the markers form readable rows.\n\nON THE OSCILLATOR places each marker at the oscillator's value when the event fired.", display=display.none)
bool   inpHist = input.bool(true, "Histogram", group=G_D, display=display.none)
float  inpHGain = input.float(2.0, "    Histogram display gain", minval=1.0, maxval=10.0, step=0.5, group=G_D, tooltip="DISPLAY ONLY. Multiplies the plotted columns and the impulse-threshold band by this factor so the histogram - the primary read - is tall enough to see on a ±100 pane. Its native range is roughly ±20.\n\nNothing in the engine reads the scaled value. Triggers, the impulse gate, the gradient shading and the information panel all work on the native histogram, and the Data Window carries a 'Histogram (native)' row alongside the plotted one.\n\nThe rails belong to the TRACE, not to the histogram. At any gain above 1 a column touching a zone line means nothing - read the columns against zero and against the threshold band only.\n\n1.0 restores the true scale.", display=display.none)
string inpHLogic = input.string("Expansion + slope", "    Histogram shading", options=["Expansion + slope", "Expansion only"], group=G_D, tooltip="DISPLAY ONLY. How each column's shade is decided.\n\nHUE is the direction of the PUSH - which side of its own signal line the trace is on. It is NOT who is in control; that is the trace's side of zero. A green column below a trace at -45 means sellers still control but their grip is easing.\n\nEXPANSION + SLOPE (default) - three tiers per side:\n  PRESSING      column growing away from zero. Moderate below the impulse threshold, near-solid past it - bright means the trigger would accept it.\n  DECELERATING  column shrinking, but the trace is still moving with it. Mid shade - a pause in the push, not a reversal of it.\n  TURNING       column shrinking and the trace has flattened or turned against it. Pale - the trace itself is giving ground, often a few bars before the column crosses zero.\n\nWHY THE SLOPE ONLY SPLITS SHRINKING COLUMNS. With the signal an EMA of the trace, Δhist = (1−α)·Δosc − α·hist[1]. A growing column above zero therefore REQUIRES a rising trace, so slope adds nothing there. A shrinking column is ambiguous - the trace may be rising slower than the signal catches up, or it may have turned - and the slope is what tells the two apart.\n\nA trace move smaller than 0.1σ of its own bar-to-bar change counts as flat, so a wobbling trace reads as turning rather than flickering between tiers.\n\nEXPANSION ONLY - two tiers: growing is saturated, shrinking is pale.\n\nNone of this has been measured for edge. It is a reading aid.", display=display.none)
bool   inpQuiet = input.bool(true, "    Quiet-regime desaturation", group=G_D, tooltip="DISPLAY ONLY. Adaptive scaling divides by 3σ of the raw share, so when that σ collapses a small imbalance scales into a large reading - the pane looks dramatic and the chart does not.\n\nWhen the raw share's σ sits in the bottom fifth of its own history (4× the normalization window), the columns lose about half their colour and the panel's raw-share row reads QUIET. The tiers still read; the hue just stops shouting.\n\nThis is the check the panel has always asked you to make by hand, moved onto the chart. It is conviction's regime, so it applies while the trace carries conviction - not on the Value trace.", display=display.none)
bool   inpTraceZone = input.bool(true, "Trace intensity by zone", group=G_D, tooltip="DISPLAY ONLY. The trace stays secondary but grades with distance from zero: muted inside the inner rail, clearer past it, full past the outer rail. A blended trace is also dimmed while its ingredients point opposite ways or the value basket settles. Zone position reads off the line itself rather than from the dotted rails.\n\nIntensity, not a new colour: past the outer zone is where trends run AND where they end, and a colour that implied either would claim more than the evidence supports.", display=display.none)
bool   inpThr  = input.bool(false, "Show impulse threshold", group=G_D, tooltip="Draws the ± k·σ band the histogram must cross for a trigger to fire. Useful once, while calibrating the threshold to an instrument; clutter afterwards.", display=display.none)
bool   inpFill = input.bool(true, "Shade", group=G_D, display=display.none)
bool   inpStateBg = input.bool(false, "Declaration background", group=G_D, tooltip="DISPLAY ONLY. A faint pane tint for the standing declaration: green after a ▲ until the next ▼, red after a ▼ until the next ▲. Off by default - the triangles already mark each declaration, and a declaration has no end, so the tint can stand for a long time.\n\nNo tint while the stack is paused.", display=display.none)
string inpTheme = input.string("Auto", "Palette", options=["Auto", "Light", "Dark"], group=G_D, display=display.none)

// ── 8 · information panel ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
bool   inpPanel = input.bool(false, "Information panel", group=G_P, tooltip="The oscillator's own panel, in the indicator pane. Off removes it entirely.\n\nThe readout - open interest and the Conviction-Value Grid - is a separate table with its own switches, in the '10 · Open interest' and '11 · Conviction-Value Grid' groups — it sits on the PRICE chart rather than in this pane, so the two never collide and either can be run without the other.", display=display.none)
string inpTbl  = input.string("Detailed", "    Detail", options=["Compact", "Detailed"], group=G_P, tooltip="COMPACT is the headline, the signal state, both tapes, the impulse and the trace's ingredients. DETAILED adds the raw share and its σ, this bar's conviction, the participation weight, and — when the profile is on — its data mode and where price sits in it.", display=display.none)

int DIV_LINES = 60      // divergence connectors retained, recycled explicitly

// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  PALETTE
// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
float bgLum    = color.r(chart.bg_color) * 0.299 + color.g(chart.bg_color) * 0.587 + color.b(chart.bg_color) * 0.114
bool  darkMode = inpTheme == "Dark" or (inpTheme == "Auto" and bgLum < 128)

color C_BULL  = darkMode ? #3FB980 : #08724A
color C_BEAR  = darkMode ? #E4776A : #A8443A
color C_NEUT  = darkMode ? #8A97A0 : #69757E
color C_GOLD  = darkMode ? #CFC257 : #7A6B15      // READ WITH CAUTION - never a direction
color C_SOFT  = darkMode ? #A8B3B9 : #525C64
color C_INK   = darkMode ? #0D1215 : #FFFFFF
color C_PANEL = darkMode ? color.new(#0D1215, 6) : color.new(#FFFFFF, 4)
color C_GRID  = darkMode ? color.new(#8A97A0, 70) : color.new(#7A868F, 74)

// profile palette — PVOI's, unchanged
color C_POC    = darkMode ? #FFB020 : #B26A00      // point of control — warm anchor
color C_VA     = darkMode ? #5B8FD6 : #2C5C99      // value-area boundaries + band, cool
color C_VWAP   = darkMode ? #F06BA8 : #A32468      // volume-weighted average, rose
color C_NODE   = darkMode ? #6E7C88 : #8A949B      // monochrome out-of-value tails · neutral grey
color C_HVN    = darkMode ? #8F7BD8 : #5E4B9E      // High-Volume Node · dense violet
color C_LVN    = darkMode ? #C6BBEA : #8F7DBB      // Low-Volume Node  · pale  violet
color C_VIOLET = darkMode ? #A87ABF : #6A3F84      // Initial Balance

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  1 · CONVICTION AND PARTICIPATION
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
float tr   = ta.tr(true)
float disp = close - nz(close[1], close)
float conv = tr > 1e-12 ? disp / tr : 0.0          // bounded −1 … +1

bool  volOK  = not na(volume) and volume > 0
// A hollow bar (holiday, half session, thin overnight print) used to feed na into
// this EMA, which killed it for at least inpVN bars and silently dropped the whole
// series back to range weighting. Measured on real data, volume weighting was only
// live on 73% of bars under "Auto" - 31% on 1h futures, 50% on silver daily, where
// the resulting oscillator correlated only 0.58 with the intended one. Carrying the
// last good print keeps the baseline alive; a genuinely volume-less symbol still
// leaves volLast na and still falls through to true range, as documented.
var float volLast = na
if volOK
    volLast := volume
float volAvg = ta.ema(volLast, inpVN)
float trAvg  = ta.ema(tr, inpVN)

float wVol = volOK and not na(volAvg) and volAvg > 1e-12 ? volume / volAvg : na
float wRng = not na(trAvg) and trAvg > 1e-12 ? tr / trAvg : 1.0

float wRaw = inpPart == "Off"        ? 1.0 :
             inpPart == "True range" ? wRng :
             inpPart == "Volume"     ? nz(wVol, 1.0) :
                                       nz(wVol, wRng)
float w = math.min(math.max(nz(wRaw, 1.0), 0.0), inpCap)

// Which branch ACTUALLY produced w on this bar - reported by the panel. It used
// to be inferred from volOK alone, which read "volume" in True-range mode and
// "true range" in Volume mode on a hollow bar, where the weight is really a flat
// 1.0. A panel that guesses at the branch is an echo, not a report.
string wSrc = inpPart == "Off"        ? "off · equal weight" :
              inpPart == "True range" ? "true range" :
              inpPart == "Volume"     ? (not na(wVol) ? "volume" : "no print · flat") :
              not na(wVol)            ? "volume" : "true range"

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  2 · THE CHART'S OWN CONVICTION           the fastest rung of the ladder
//
//  Ratio of sums, so a dead bar contributes nothing to either side rather than
//  an undefined ratio; then scaled against its own dispersion. This is the
//  published Nishchaya v3 oscillator exactly, and it IS the pane's trace.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
f_tanh(float x) =>
    float xc = math.max(-10.0, math.min(10.0, x))
    float e  = math.exp(2.0 * xc)
    (e - 1.0) / (e + 1.0)

float num  = ta.sma(conv * w, inpLen)
float denA = ta.sma(math.abs(conv) * w, inpLen)          // agreement: Σ|c|·w
float denE = ta.sma(w, inpLen)                           // effort:    Σw
float den  = inpDen == "Effort" ? denE : denA
float raw  = den > 1e-12 ? 100.0 * num / den : 0.0

// EFFORT → RESULT (display only). The share of the window's participation that
// became directional displacement at all, and where that share sits in its own
// history. Low = effort being absorbed. Computed under both denominators.
float effShare = denE > 1e-12 ? denA / denE : na
float effPct   = ta.percentrank(effShare, inpNorm)

float rawSd  = ta.stdev(raw, inpNorm)
float zChart = na(rawSd) or rawSd < 1e-9 ? 0.0 : raw / (3.0 * rawSd)     // the tanh argument
float scaled = 100.0 * f_tanh(zChart)
float fast   = inpSm > 1 ? ta.ema(scaled, inpSm) : scaled            // v3's oscillator

// Warm-up, stage one: raw is pinned to 0 until its window and the participation
// baseline fill; zChart until rawSd has a window free of those zeros.
var int nRaw = 0
if bar_index >= inpLen + inpVN
    nRaw += 1
bool sdOK = nRaw > inpNorm

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  3 · THE LADDER                          fixed, standard, the same everywhere
//
//  UP    every frame of 4h · 1h · 30m · 15m · 5m ABOVE the chart that holds a
//        whole number of chart bars, then the chart. Daily: W · D. Up to five.
//  DOWN  every frame of 1m · 3m · 5m · 15m · 30m · 1h · 4h BELOW the chart that
//        the chart holds a whole number of, then the chart. Daily: all seven.
//        Weekly: D · 4h · 1h. Monthly: W · D. Up to seven.
//  FALLBACK  a direction with no frames on this chart reads the other; the
//        tape's label shows ↺.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
var array<int> LAD_UP = array.from(240, 60, 30, 15, 5)
var array<int> LAD_DN = array.from(1, 3, 5, 15, 30, 60, 240)

int  ladSec   = timeframe.in_seconds()
int  ladTm    = int(math.round(ladSec / 60.0))
bool ladMin   = timeframe.isintraday and ladSec >= 60 and ladSec % 60 == 0
bool ladDaily = timeframe.isdaily and timeframe.multiplier == 1
// FALLBACK. A direction with no frames on this chart uses the other instead of
// pausing every signal; the tape's label shows ↺. Availability mirrors the rung
// loops below exactly: every frame above an intraday chart divides 240, and 1m
// divides every chart above it.
bool ladUpAv  = ladDaily or (ladMin and ladTm < 240 and 240 % ladTm == 0)
bool ladDnAv  = (timeframe.ismonthly and timeframe.multiplier == 1) or (timeframe.isweekly and timeframe.multiplier == 1) or ladDaily or (ladMin and ladTm > 1)
bool cWantUp  = inpTape == "Ladder up"
bool cWantDn  = inpTape == "Ladder down"
bool ladUp    = (cWantUp and ladUpAv) or (cWantDn and not ladDnAv and ladUpAv)
bool ladDn    = (cWantDn and ladDnAv) or (cWantUp and not ladUpAv and ladDnAv)
bool ladFallback = (cWantUp and ladDn) or (cWantDn and ladUp)

// Up: parents, top → down.
string lad1 = ""
string lad2 = ""
string lad3 = ""
string lad4 = ""
string lad5 = ""
// Down: children, finest → coarsest.
string low1 = ""
string low2 = ""
string low3 = ""
string low4 = ""
string low5 = ""
string low6 = ""
string low7 = ""
if ladUp
    if ladDaily
        lad1 := "W"
    else if ladMin and ladTm < 240
        int n = 0
        for f in LAD_UP
            if f > ladTm and f % ladTm == 0
                n += 1
                string sf = str.tostring(f)
                if n == 1
                    lad1 := sf
                else if n == 2
                    lad2 := sf
                else if n == 3
                    lad3 := sf
                else if n == 4
                    lad4 := sf
                else
                    lad5 := sf
if ladDn
    if timeframe.ismonthly and timeframe.multiplier == 1
        low1 := "D"
        low2 := "W"
    else if timeframe.isweekly and timeframe.multiplier == 1
        low1 := "60"
        low2 := "240"
        low3 := "D"
    else if ladDaily or ladMin
        int n = 0
        for f in LAD_DN
            if ladDaily or (f < ladTm and ladTm % f == 0)
                n += 1
                string sf = str.tostring(f)
                if n == 1
                    low1 := sf
                else if n == 2
                    low2 := sf
                else if n == 3
                    low3 := sf
                else if n == 4
                    low4 := sf
                else if n == 5
                    low5 := sf
                else if n == 6
                    low6 := sf
                else
                    low7 := sf
bool multiUp = lad1 != ""
bool multiDn = low1 != ""
bool multi   = multiUp or multiDn

f_tfLbl(string tf) =>
    string out = tf
    if tf != "D" and tf != "W" and tf != "M" and tf != ""
        int n = int(str.tonumber(tf))
        out := n % 60 == 0 ? str.tostring(n / 60) + "h" : tf + "m"
    out

f_join(string acc, string tf) => tf == "" ? acc : acc + f_tfLbl(tf) + "·"
string ladChartLbl = timeframe.isintraday ? f_tfLbl(str.tostring(ladTm)) : timeframe.isdaily ? "D" : timeframe.isweekly ? "W" : timeframe.period
string ladLbl = not multi ? "chart only" :
     multiUp ? f_join(f_join(f_join(f_join(f_join("", lad1), lad2), lad3), lad4), lad5) + ladChartLbl :
     f_join(f_join(f_join(f_join(f_join(f_join(f_join("", low1), low2), low3), low4), low5), low6), low7) + ladChartLbl

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  4 · PARENTS, RECONSTRUCTED ON THE CHART              (ladder up)
//
//  The parent supplies only what it knows for certain: its state as of its last
//  CLOSED bar, plus the open time of the bar now forming. Every sum is over one
//  bar fewer than its window, so the chart can complete it with the forming bar.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
type Parent
    float cPrev      // last settled close
    float vLast      // last good volume print, carried
    float vAvg       // EMA of volume at the settled bar
    float trAvg      // EMA of true range at the settled bar
    float sNum       // Σ c·w     over the last (lookback − 1) settled bars
    float sDa        // Σ |c|·w
    float sDe        // Σ w
    float sR         // Σ raw     over the last (normalization − 1) settled bars
    float sR2        // Σ raw²
    float zS         // the settled bar's own z, for the reconstruction check
    bool  rdy        // the parent's own normalization window is clean
    int   t          // open time of the parent bar now forming

f_parentSettled() =>
    float pTr  = ta.tr(true)
    float pCv  = pTr > 1e-12 ? (close - nz(close[1], close)) / pTr : 0.0
    bool  pVok = not na(volume) and volume > 0
    var float pVl = na
    if pVok
        pVl := volume
    float pVa  = ta.ema(pVl, inpVN)
    float pTa  = ta.ema(pTr, inpVN)
    float pWv  = pVok and not na(pVa) and pVa > 1e-12 ? volume / pVa : na
    float pWr  = not na(pTa) and pTa > 1e-12 ? pTr / pTa : 1.0
    float pWx  = inpPart == "Off" ? 1.0 : inpPart == "True range" ? pWr : inpPart == "Volume" ? nz(pWv, 1.0) : nz(pWv, pWr)
    float pW   = math.min(math.max(nz(pWx, 1.0), 0.0), inpCap)
    float pNum = ta.sma(pCv * pW, inpLen)
    float pDa  = ta.sma(math.abs(pCv) * pW, inpLen)
    float pDe  = ta.sma(pW, inpLen)
    float pDen = inpDen == "Effort" ? pDe : pDa
    float pRaw = pDen > 1e-12 ? 100.0 * pNum / pDen : 0.0
    float pSd  = ta.stdev(pRaw, inpNorm)
    float pZ   = na(pSd) or pSd < 1e-9 ? 0.0 : pRaw / (3.0 * pSd)
    var int pN = 0
    if bar_index >= inpLen + inpVN
        pN += 1
    float sNum = math.sum(pCv * pW, inpLen - 1)
    float sDa  = math.sum(math.abs(pCv) * pW, inpLen - 1)
    float sDe  = math.sum(pW, inpLen - 1)
    float sR   = math.sum(pRaw, inpNorm - 1)
    float sR2  = math.sum(pRaw * pRaw, inpNorm - 1)
    bool  rdy  = pN > inpNorm and not na(sNum) and not na(sR) and not na(pVa) and not na(pTa)
    Parent.new(close, pVl, pVa, pTa, sNum, sDa, sDe, sR, sR2, pZ, rdy, time)

// Evaluated on parent bar i with lookahead_on, this hands every chart bar inside
// bar i the state of bar i−1 - closed, final, known - and the open time of bar
// i, which is known the moment it opens. Nothing from bar i's future leaks.
f_parent() =>
    Parent x = f_parentSettled()
    Parent p = x[1]
    if not na(p)
        p := p.copy()
        p.t := time
    p

// The forming parent bar, aggregated from chart bars since it opened. Each call
// site keeps its own running bar.
f_agg(int t) =>
    var int   at = na
    var float ah = na
    var float al = na
    var float av = 0.0
    if na(at) or t != at
        at := t
        ah := high
        al := low
        av := nz(volume)
    else
        ah := math.max(ah, high)
        al := math.min(al, low)
        av += nz(volume)
    [ah, al, close, av]

// The parent's arithmetic, finished with the forming bar included. Mirrors
// section 1-2 line for line; returns the parent's live z, or na until the
// parent can calibrate.
f_develop(Parent p, float aH, float aL, float aC, float aV) =>
    float z = na
    if not na(p) and p.rdy
        float cp  = p.cPrev
        float trD = math.max(aH, cp) - math.min(aL, cp)
        float cD  = trD > 1e-12 ? (aC - cp) / trD : 0.0
        bool  vOk = aV > 0
        float vl  = vOk ? aV : p.vLast
        float al  = 2.0 / (inpVN + 1)
        float vA  = na(vl) ? p.vAvg : al * vl + (1.0 - al) * p.vAvg
        float tA  = al * trD + (1.0 - al) * p.trAvg
        float wv  = vOk and not na(vA) and vA > 1e-12 ? aV / vA : na
        float wr  = not na(tA) and tA > 1e-12 ? trD / tA : 1.0
        float wx  = inpPart == "Off" ? 1.0 : inpPart == "True range" ? wr : inpPart == "Volume" ? nz(wv, 1.0) : nz(wv, wr)
        float wD  = math.min(math.max(nz(wx, 1.0), 0.0), inpCap)
        float nm  = (p.sNum + cD * wD) / inpLen
        float dA  = (p.sDa + math.abs(cD) * wD) / inpLen
        float dE  = (p.sDe + wD) / inpLen
        float dn  = inpDen == "Effort" ? dE : dA
        float rw  = dn > 1e-12 ? 100.0 * nm / dn : 0.0
        float mu  = (p.sR + rw) / inpNorm
        float vr  = (p.sR2 + rw * rw) / inpNorm - mu * mu
        float sd  = math.sqrt(math.max(vr, 0.0))
        z := sd < 1e-9 ? 0.0 : rw / (3.0 * sd)
    z

Parent p1 = na
Parent p2 = na
Parent p3 = na
Parent p4 = na
Parent p5 = na
if multiUp
    p1 := request.security(syminfo.tickerid, lad1, f_parent(), lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_on)
    if lad2 != ""
        p2 := request.security(syminfo.tickerid, lad2, f_parent(), lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_on)
    if lad3 != ""
        p3 := request.security(syminfo.tickerid, lad3, f_parent(), lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_on)
    if lad4 != ""
        p4 := request.security(syminfo.tickerid, lad4, f_parent(), lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_on)
    if lad5 != ""
        p5 := request.security(syminfo.tickerid, lad5, f_parent(), lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_on)

// Fields are read only inside a guard: a field of an na object is a runtime
// error, and a ternary is not a guaranteed guard.
f_pt(Parent p) =>
    int t = na
    if not na(p)
        t := p.t
    t
int t1 = f_pt(p1)
int t2 = f_pt(p2)
int t3 = f_pt(p3)
int t4 = f_pt(p4)
int t5 = f_pt(p5)
[aH1, aL1, aC1, aV1] = f_agg(t1)
[aH2, aL2, aC2, aV2] = f_agg(t2)
[aH3, aL3, aC3, aV3] = f_agg(t3)
[aH4, aL4, aC4, aV4] = f_agg(t4)
[aH5, aL5, aC5, aV5] = f_agg(t5)
float z1 = f_develop(p1, aH1, aL1, aC1, aV1)
float z2 = f_develop(p2, aH2, aL2, aC2, aV2)
float z3 = f_develop(p3, aH3, aL3, aC3, aV3)
float z4 = f_develop(p4, aH4, aL4, aC4, aV4)
float z5 = f_develop(p5, aH5, aL5, aC5, aV5)

// RECONSTRUCTION CHECK (display only). On the first chart bar of each new parent
// bar, the last live reading of the bar that just closed is compared with the
// value the parent itself settled on, in trace points. Carried between closes.
f_recErr(Parent p, int t, float zLive) =>
    float e = na
    if not na(p) and not na(t) and t != nz(t[1], t) and not na(zLive[1]) and not na(p.zS)
        e := 100.0 * math.abs(f_tanh(zLive[1]) - f_tanh(p.zS))
    e
float e1 = f_recErr(p1, t1, z1)
float e2 = f_recErr(p2, t2, z2)
float e3 = f_recErr(p3, t3, z3)
float e4 = f_recErr(p4, t4, z4)
float e5 = f_recErr(p5, t5, z5)
var float recErr = na
if not na(e1) or not na(e2) or not na(e3) or not na(e4) or not na(e5)
    recErr := math.max(nz(e1), nz(e2), nz(e3), nz(e4), nz(e5))

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  4b · CHILDREN, READ FROM INSIDE THE CHART BAR           (ladder down)
//
//  Sampling a lower frame at the chart bar's close would describe only its last
//  few bars - the final twenty minutes of a day, on a 1m rung. So each lower
//  frame runs the full engine on its own continuous history, and the chart bar
//  takes the participation-weighted AVERAGE of that frame's z across all of its
//  bars inside the chart bar: the bar seen from inside, at that resolution. A
//  heavy intrabar counts for more than a quiet one, as it should.
//
//  request.security_lower_tf delivers every intrabar of every closed chart bar
//  in history and the intrabars so far on the live one, so the reading moves
//  while the chart bar forms and is final at its close - no repaint.
//
//  DEPTH. TradingView caps intrabars per request (about 100,000 on most plans).
//  A 1m rung on a daily chart is 375 intrabars a day on NSE, so it reaches back
//  roughly a year; older bars run without it. A frame joins the ladder when its
//  own normalization is clean and shifts the trace's level, not its scale.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
f_child() =>
    float qTr  = ta.tr(true)
    float qCv  = qTr > 1e-12 ? (close - nz(close[1], close)) / qTr : 0.0
    bool  qVok = not na(volume) and volume > 0
    var float qVl = na
    if qVok
        qVl := volume
    float qVa  = ta.ema(qVl, inpVN)
    float qTa  = ta.ema(qTr, inpVN)
    float qWv  = qVok and not na(qVa) and qVa > 1e-12 ? volume / qVa : na
    float qWr  = not na(qTa) and qTa > 1e-12 ? qTr / qTa : 1.0
    float qWx  = inpPart == "Off" ? 1.0 : inpPart == "True range" ? qWr : inpPart == "Volume" ? nz(qWv, 1.0) : nz(qWv, qWr)
    float qW   = math.min(math.max(nz(qWx, 1.0), 0.0), inpCap)
    float qNum = ta.sma(qCv * qW, inpLen)
    float qDa  = ta.sma(math.abs(qCv) * qW, inpLen)
    float qDe  = ta.sma(qW, inpLen)
    float qDen = inpDen == "Effort" ? qDe : qDa
    float qRaw = qDen > 1e-12 ? 100.0 * qNum / qDen : 0.0
    float qSd  = ta.stdev(qRaw, inpNorm)
    var int qN = 0
    if bar_index >= inpLen + inpVN
        qN += 1
    float qZ   = qN > inpNorm ? (na(qSd) or qSd < 1e-9 ? 0.0 : qRaw / (3.0 * qSd)) : na
    [qZ, qW]

// Participation-weighted mean of a child's z over the intrabars of this chart
// bar; na when the frame has no calibrated intrabars here.
f_inside(array<float> zs, array<float> ws) =>
    float sz = 0.0
    float sw = 0.0
    if not na(zs) and not na(ws)
        int m = math.min(array.size(zs), array.size(ws))
        if m > 0
            for i = 0 to m - 1
                float zi = array.get(zs, i)
                if not na(zi)
                    float wi = math.max(nz(array.get(ws, i), 1.0), 1e-6)
                    sz += zi * wi
                    sw += wi
    sw > 0.0 ? sz / sw : na

float y1 = na
float y2 = na
float y3 = na
float y4 = na
float y5 = na
float y6 = na
float y7 = na
if multiDn
    [qz1, qw1] = request.security_lower_tf(syminfo.tickerid, low1, f_child())
    y1 := f_inside(qz1, qw1)
    if low2 != ""
        [qz2, qw2] = request.security_lower_tf(syminfo.tickerid, low2, f_child())
        y2 := f_inside(qz2, qw2)
    if low3 != ""
        [qz3, qw3] = request.security_lower_tf(syminfo.tickerid, low3, f_child())
        y3 := f_inside(qz3, qw3)
    if low4 != ""
        [qz4, qw4] = request.security_lower_tf(syminfo.tickerid, low4, f_child())
        y4 := f_inside(qz4, qw4)
    if low5 != ""
        [qz5, qw5] = request.security_lower_tf(syminfo.tickerid, low5, f_child())
        y5 := f_inside(qz5, qw5)
    if low6 != ""
        [qz6, qw6] = request.security_lower_tf(syminfo.tickerid, low6, f_child())
        y6 := f_inside(qz6, qw6)
    if low7 != ""
        [qz7, qw7] = request.security_lower_tf(syminfo.tickerid, low7, f_child())
        y7 := f_inside(qz7, qw7)

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  4c · THE VALUE LADDER · Samanvaya's engine, carried whole     display-only
//
//  Everything from here to section 5 is Samanvaya's composite: the RV leg (Y
//  hedged against a stepwise-selected macro basket on an anchored clock, the
//  hedge applied in proportion to its measured skill, the spread scored over a
//  timescale ensemble) and the breadth leg (seven MSF views of Y's own price),
//  blended in z-space. The MTF VALUE tape needs the composite on the chart AND
//  on every rung, so the engine has to run here; a lighter copy would be a
//  different reading wearing Samanvaya's name.
//
//  Ported line for line. Only what feeds the ladder was kept: the table, the
//  pane, the OU / ADF diagnostics, the signals and Siddhi's line stayed behind.
//  Names that collided with Pragati's were renamed (confirmed → svConfirmed,
//  f_tanh → f_svTanh, b1..b3 → gb1..gb3).
//  "The pane" in the comments below means Samanvaya's unified reading, which
//  is not drawn here: it is the chart rung of the value ladder.
//
//  Nothing here touches Pragati's trace or histogram. The signals read three
//  things from it: the value tape (tpBias), Samanvaya's value on this chart
//  (unified_osc) and its basket gate (basketWarm, enoughData).
//
//  KEPT IN STEP WITH samanvaya.pine, whose audit this engine shares:
//    · the anchor sample is each period's OWN return (section 4 below), the
//      intercept is spread over the bars a period holds (section 9), and hedge
//      skill is measured one period at a time (section 10);
//    · DRIVER TIMING - a driver whose bar closes more than a third of a chart
//      bar after the chart's is read at its previous close (section 1);
//    · Y is the chart's own close, not a request; the custom driver faces the
//      same self-screen as every constituent; breadth is defined once.
//  The value rungs keep rolling window sums instead of re-summing (same
//  numbers). What samanvaya.pine has and this port does not need: its table,
//  pane, stationarity diagnostics, exits and markers.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  STUDY-FIXED CONSTANTS.  Each was an input; each was swept over its full range
//  across 24 real targets. The bracket is how far the WHOLE range moved mean
//  information coefficient, in standard errors across targets (SE = 0.0135).
// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
string anchorTF   = anchorSel == "Weekly (stronger, needs daily+ charts)" ? "W" : "D"
bool   selStep    = true   // stepwise vs absolute-ρ selection        [0.1 SE]
int    corrLen    = 250    // estimation sample, anchor bars          [0.4 SE, best]
int    holdOut    = 12     // selection hold-out, anchor bars         [0.4 SE, best]
float  hystMarg   = 0.10   // selection hysteresis                    [0.1 SE, best]
float  ridgeLam   = 0.05   // ridge shrinkage                         [0.1 SE]
float  minRho     = 0.0    // economic floor; Fisher does the work    [0.0 SE]
bool   sigCorrect = true   // Šidák over 9 candidates                 [0.0 SE]
int    inpNumVars = 3      // maximum model order                     [0.1 SE]
int    minObs     = 30     // minimum usable anchor sample
float  gain       = 0.5    // display scaling; monotone, cannot move rank IC [0.0 SE]
bool   useDrift   = true   // high-pass. neutral in the sweep [0.5 SE]; kept
int    driftLen   = 400    // because with the hedge off the RV leg is a raw
                           // cumulative return and would otherwise drift [0.7 SE]
int    skillWin   = 104    // anchor bars over which hedge skill is measured

// RV leg timescale ensemble. No window dominates — best and worst member are
// 0.0053 IC apart — so the grid is averaged rather than chosen. The members are
// ~0.73 correlated, so their mean has sd √((1+4ρ)/5) = 0.887 and is divided by
// it to keep θ meaning what it means at a single window.
int   Z1 = 8
int   Z2 = 13
int   Z3 = 21
int   Z4 = 34
int   Z5 = 55
                           // (ENS_SCALE is declared with the ensemble below,
                           //  where its derivation is documented)

// Breadth leg grid — Tattva's module default, which also measured best of the
// six configurations swept (+0.0691; whole spread 0.0060 IC, inside the SE).
int   B1 = 10
int   B2 = 14
int   B3 = 20
int   B4 = 28
int   B5 = 40
float rocFrac    = 0.70
float regimeSens = 8.0     // Tattva SWAYAM_REGIME_SENSITIVITY
float baseWeight = 0.0     // Tattva SWAYAM_BASE_WEIGHT — sets the (-5,5) bound
float clipZ      = 3.0
int   varCorrLen = 200     // window for measuring ρ between the two legs

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  1. DATA  ·  strict non-repainting fetch of Y and the 21-instrument universe
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
bool svConfirmed = barstate.isconfirmed

// DRIVER TIMING. A driver's bar is matched to the chart bar by date, and on a
// daily chart many drivers close hours AFTER Y - TVC:US10Y about eleven hours
// after NSE. In history such a bar carries information from after Y's close;
// live, at Y's close, it is still forming - so history would read better than
// realtime and the last bar would revise. A driver whose bar closes more than a
// third of a chart bar after the chart's is read at its PREVIOUS close, the
// value known when Y closed. The third is derived: lagging a driver L of a bar
// late keeps L of genuine overlap with Y's return; keeping it keeps 1 − L, of
// which L is future; keeping wins exactly while (1 − L) − L > L. Measured, not
// assumed, so a driver already aligned to an earlier bar is never lagged twice.
// The tuple costs no extra request.
float DRIVER_LATE = 1.0 / 3.0

f_req(simple string sym) =>
    [cNow, cPrev, tClose] = request.security(sym, "", [close, close[1], time_close], lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_off)
    float span = timeframe.in_seconds() * 1000.0
    float lf   = na(tClose) or na(time_close) or span <= 0 ? 0.0 : math.max(0.0, (tClose - time_close) / span)
    float v    = lf > DRIVER_LATE ? cPrev : cNow
    [barstate.isconfirmed ? v : nz(v[1], v), lf]

// Y is the chart's own close under the same confirmed-bar discipline - it used
// to be fetched through a request of the chart's own symbol, which bought
// nothing and spent one of the forty.
float src_y = svConfirmed ? close : nz(close[1], close)

[d_JP10Y, lf_JP10Y]   = f_req('TVC:JP10Y')
[d_JP02Y, lf_JP02Y]   = f_req('TVC:JP02Y')
[d_CN10Y, lf_CN10Y]   = f_req('TVC:CN10Y')
[d_CN02Y, lf_CN02Y]   = f_req('TVC:CN02Y')
[d_EU10Y, lf_EU10Y]   = f_req('TVC:EU10Y')
[d_EU02Y, lf_EU02Y]   = f_req('TVC:EU02Y')
[d_GB10Y, lf_GB10Y]   = f_req('TVC:GB10Y')
[d_GB02Y, lf_GB02Y]   = f_req('TVC:GB02Y')
[d_US10Y, lf_US10Y]   = f_req('TVC:US10Y')
[d_US02Y, lf_US02Y]   = f_req('TVC:US02Y')
[d_US30Y, lf_US30Y]   = f_req('TVC:US30Y')
[d_IN10Y, lf_IN10Y]   = f_req('TVC:IN10Y')
[d_IN02Y, lf_IN02Y]   = f_req('TVC:IN02Y')
[d_USDINR, lf_USDINR] = f_req('FX_IDC:USDINR')
[d_EURINR, lf_EURINR] = f_req('FX_IDC:EURINR')
[d_GBPINR, lf_GBPINR] = f_req('FX_IDC:GBPINR')
[d_JPYINR, lf_JPYINR] = f_req('FX_IDC:JPYINR')
[d_GOLD, lf_GOLD]     = f_req('TVC:GOLD')
[d_SILVER, lf_SILVER] = f_req('TVC:SILVER')
[d_USOIL, lf_USOIL]   = f_req('TVC:USOIL')
[d_DXY, lf_DXY]       = f_req('TVC:DXY')
// An empty symbol is not a valid request.security argument and the custom slot
// now ships empty. Substituting the chart symbol keeps the call legal; the name
// screen then drops it, so the column is constant and can never be selected.
string custSym = usy_sym == "" ? syminfo.tickerid : usy_sym
[d_CUSTOM, lf_CUSTOM] = f_req(custSym)

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  2. NATURAL UNITS  ·  the reason the model can travel between timeframes
//
//  A yield and a price do not move in the same unit. Yields are measured in
//  PERCENTAGE POINTS — log(4.28/4.20) is meaningless the moment a yield
//  approaches or crosses zero, which JP and EU spent years doing. Prices are
//  measured in LOGARITHMS, whose differences are returns.
//
//  Crucially there is NO rolling standardization here. A rolling z-score is a
//  window-dependent transform, so a beta fitted against it is only valid on the
//  window that produced it and cannot be moved from the anchor clock to the
//  chart clock. Natural units are frequency-stable, which is what makes the
//  whole anchored design work. It also keeps every value O(1)–O(10), which is
//  better conditioned than the raw levels ever were.
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
f_lg(series float x) => na(x) or x <= 0 ? na : math.log(x)
f_df(series float x) => na(x) or na(x[1]) ? na : x - x[1]

// levels representation: yields as-is (%), prices as logs
float L_JP10Y  = d_JP10Y
float L_JP02Y  = d_JP02Y
float L_CN10Y  = d_CN10Y
float L_CN02Y  = d_CN02Y
float L_EU10Y  = d_EU10Y
float L_EU02Y  = d_EU02Y
float L_GB10Y  = d_GB10Y
float L_GB02Y  = d_GB02Y
float L_US10Y  = d_US10Y
float L_US02Y  = d_US02Y
float L_US30Y  = d_US30Y
float L_IN10Y  = d_IN10Y
float L_IN02Y  = d_IN02Y
float L_USDINR = f_lg(d_USDINR)
float L_EURINR = f_lg(d_EURINR)
float L_GBPINR = f_lg(d_GBPINR)
float L_JPYINR = f_lg(d_JPYINR)
float L_GOLD   = f_lg(d_GOLD)
float L_SILVER = f_lg(d_SILVER)
float L_USOIL  = f_lg(d_USOIL)
float L_DXY    = f_lg(d_DXY)
float L_CUSTOM = f_lg(d_CUSTOM)
float L_Y      = f_lg(src_y)

// Differenced once: the model runs on returns. (A levels engine existed and was
// removed - it passed ADF 2% of the time - and with it the switch between them.)
float v_JP10Y  = f_df(L_JP10Y)
float v_JP02Y  = f_df(L_JP02Y)
float v_CN10Y  = f_df(L_CN10Y)
float v_CN02Y  = f_df(L_CN02Y)
float v_EU10Y  = f_df(L_EU10Y)
float v_EU02Y  = f_df(L_EU02Y)
float v_GB10Y  = f_df(L_GB10Y)
float v_GB02Y  = f_df(L_GB02Y)
float v_US10Y  = f_df(L_US10Y)
float v_US02Y  = f_df(L_US02Y)
float v_US30Y  = f_df(L_US30Y)
float v_IN10Y  = f_df(L_IN10Y)
float v_IN02Y  = f_df(L_IN02Y)
float v_USDINR = f_df(L_USDINR)
float v_EURINR = f_df(L_EURINR)
float v_GBPINR = f_df(L_GBPINR)
float v_JPYINR = f_df(L_JPYINR)
float v_GOLD   = f_df(L_GOLD)
float v_SILVER = f_df(L_SILVER)
float v_USOIL  = f_df(L_USOIL)
float v_DXY    = f_df(L_DXY)
float v_CUSTOM = f_df(L_CUSTOM)
float yFit     = f_df(L_Y)

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  3. FACTOR CONSTRUCTION
//
//  Pooling is na-safe: a symbol your data plan does not carry drops out of its
//  factor instead of poisoning it. Because differencing is linear, the pool of
//  the constituents' returns is exactly the return of the pooled level.
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
f_pool(float a, float b, float c, float d, float e, float f) =>
    float s = 0.0
    int   n = 0
    if not na(a)
        s := s + a
        n := n + 1
    if not na(b)
        s := s + b
        n := n + 1
    if not na(c)
        s := s + c
        n := n + 1
    if not na(d)
        s := s + d
        n := n + 1
    if not na(e)
        s := s + e
        n := n + 1
    if not na(f)
        s := s + f
        n := n + 1
    n > 0 ? s / n : na

f_sub(float a, float b) => na(a) or na(b) ? na : a - b

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  SELF-CONTAINMENT SCREEN  ·  do not hedge the target against itself
//
//  Chart TVC:GOLD and the "Precious metals" factor IS half gold; chart TVC:DXY
//  and the "Dollar" factor IS the chart symbol exactly. Measured on weekly
//  returns, the hedge then removes the target's own variance rather than a
//  macro exposure:
//        GOLD   vs Precious   ρ 0.91  →  82% of gold's own variance removed
//        SILVER vs Precious   ρ 0.98  →  96%
//        WTI    vs Energy     ρ 1.00  →  100%   (residual identically zero)
//        DXY    vs Dollar     ρ 1.00  →  100%
//        USDINR vs INR carry  ρ 0.72  →  51%
//
//  THE SCREEN RUNS ON CONSTITUENTS, NOT FACTORS, and that is the whole point:
//  at FACTOR level the two cases are provably inseparable. Charting gold gives
//  ρ = 0.91 against Precious, and charting TLT gives ρ = 0.91 against US rates
//  — identical numbers, opposite meanings, one self-hedging and one a genuine
//  duration relationship. No factor-level threshold can tell them apart.
//  At CONSTITUENT level they separate: self and alias pairs measure 1.000 while
//  the highest genuine constituent pair in the study was TLT against the US 30Y
//  at 0.986, so 0.99 sits in the gap.
//
//  Two screens, because neither alone suffices:
//    NAME  exact ticker match. Deterministic, no false positives, but blind to
//          aliases — most people chart gold as OANDA:XAUUSD or GC1!, not
//          TVC:GOLD.
//    |ρ|   catches those aliases at 0.99, measured over 200 CHART bars.
//          It is dormant until that window fills, so on a chart with fewer than
//          200 bars only the NAME screen is active. That is the deterministic
//          one, so the exact-ticker case is still covered; an ALIAS on a very
//          short chart is the residual gap. The window is deliberately long
//          rather than short: the verdict latches, so a false positive is
//          permanent, and TLT against the US 30Y already measures 0.986 — a
//          shorter window could push a genuine pair over 0.99 in a quiet
//          stretch and strip a real driver for good.
//  The verdict LATCHES: once a constituent is identified as the target it stays
//  excluded, so the basket cannot flicker as a rolling correlation wanders.
//
//  A dropped constituent leaves its pool via the na-safe path, so "Precious"
//  on a gold chart becomes silver alone — ρ 0.80 to gold, a real driver rather
//  than a mirror. A factor whose every constituent is dropped goes constant,
//  and a constant column has zero variance and can never be selected.
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
var string TK_SELF = ticker.standard(syminfo.tickerid)

f_isSelf(series float v, simple string sym) =>
    float c = ta.correlation(yFit, v, 200)
    bool nameHit = sym == TK_SELF or sym == syminfo.tickerid or sym == syminfo.ticker
    var bool latch = false
    latch := latch or nameHit or (not na(c) and math.abs(c) > 0.99)
    latch

// isSelf is SERIES, not simple: f_isSelf carries a `var bool` latch, and a
// latched value is by definition series-qualified. Declaring the parameter
// simple is what the compiler rejects.
f_drop(series float v, series bool isSelf) => isSelf ? na : v

bool s_JP10Y  = f_isSelf(v_JP10Y,  'TVC:JP10Y')
bool s_JP02Y  = f_isSelf(v_JP02Y,  'TVC:JP02Y')
bool s_CN10Y  = f_isSelf(v_CN10Y,  'TVC:CN10Y')
bool s_CN02Y  = f_isSelf(v_CN02Y,  'TVC:CN02Y')
bool s_EU10Y  = f_isSelf(v_EU10Y,  'TVC:EU10Y')
bool s_EU02Y  = f_isSelf(v_EU02Y,  'TVC:EU02Y')
bool s_GB10Y  = f_isSelf(v_GB10Y,  'TVC:GB10Y')
bool s_GB02Y  = f_isSelf(v_GB02Y,  'TVC:GB02Y')
bool s_US10Y  = f_isSelf(v_US10Y,  'TVC:US10Y')
bool s_US02Y  = f_isSelf(v_US02Y,  'TVC:US02Y')
bool s_US30Y  = f_isSelf(v_US30Y,  'TVC:US30Y')
bool s_IN10Y  = f_isSelf(v_IN10Y,  'TVC:IN10Y')
bool s_IN02Y  = f_isSelf(v_IN02Y,  'TVC:IN02Y')
bool s_USDINR = f_isSelf(v_USDINR, 'FX_IDC:USDINR')
bool s_EURINR = f_isSelf(v_EURINR, 'FX_IDC:EURINR')
bool s_GBPINR = f_isSelf(v_GBPINR, 'FX_IDC:GBPINR')
bool s_JPYINR = f_isSelf(v_JPYINR, 'FX_IDC:JPYINR')
bool s_GOLD   = f_isSelf(v_GOLD,   'TVC:GOLD')
bool s_SILVER = f_isSelf(v_SILVER, 'TVC:SILVER')
bool s_USOIL  = f_isSelf(v_USOIL,  'TVC:USOIL')
bool s_DXY    = f_isSelf(v_DXY,    'TVC:DXY')
// The custom driver faces the same two screens as every built-in constituent.
bool s_CUSTOM = f_isSelf(v_CUSTOM, custSym)

float k_JP10Y  = f_drop(v_JP10Y,  s_JP10Y)
float k_JP02Y  = f_drop(v_JP02Y,  s_JP02Y)
float k_CN10Y  = f_drop(v_CN10Y,  s_CN10Y)
float k_CN02Y  = f_drop(v_CN02Y,  s_CN02Y)
float k_EU10Y  = f_drop(v_EU10Y,  s_EU10Y)
float k_EU02Y  = f_drop(v_EU02Y,  s_EU02Y)
float k_GB10Y  = f_drop(v_GB10Y,  s_GB10Y)
float k_GB02Y  = f_drop(v_GB02Y,  s_GB02Y)
float k_US10Y  = f_drop(v_US10Y,  s_US10Y)
float k_US02Y  = f_drop(v_US02Y,  s_US02Y)
float k_US30Y  = f_drop(v_US30Y,  s_US30Y)
float k_IN10Y  = f_drop(v_IN10Y,  s_IN10Y)
float k_IN02Y  = f_drop(v_IN02Y,  s_IN02Y)
float k_USDINR = f_drop(v_USDINR, s_USDINR)
float k_EURINR = f_drop(v_EURINR, s_EURINR)
float k_GBPINR = f_drop(v_GBPINR, s_GBPINR)
float k_JPYINR = f_drop(v_JPYINR, s_JPYINR)
float k_GOLD   = f_drop(v_GOLD,   s_GOLD)
float k_SILVER = f_drop(v_SILVER, s_SILVER)
float k_USOIL  = f_drop(v_USOIL,  s_USOIL)
float k_DXY    = f_drop(v_DXY,    s_DXY)


float fac1 = f_pool(k_JP10Y, k_CN10Y, k_EU10Y, k_GB10Y, k_US10Y, k_IN10Y)
float fac2 = f_pool(f_sub(k_JP10Y, k_JP02Y), f_sub(k_CN10Y, k_CN02Y), f_sub(k_EU10Y, k_EU02Y),
     f_sub(k_GB10Y, k_GB02Y), f_sub(k_US10Y, k_US02Y), f_sub(k_IN10Y, k_IN02Y))
float fac3 = f_pool(k_US02Y, k_US10Y, k_US30Y, na, na, na)
float fac4 = f_sub(k_US30Y, k_US02Y)
float fac5 = k_DXY
float fac6 = k_USOIL
float fac7 = f_pool(k_GOLD, k_SILVER, na, na, na, na)
float fac8 = f_pool(k_USDINR, k_EURINR, k_GBPINR, k_JPYINR, na, na)
float fac9 = f_drop(v_CUSTOM, s_CUSTOM)

bool customOk = inpCustom and usy_sym != "" and usy_sym != ticker.standard(syminfo.tickerid) and usy_sym != syminfo.tickerid and usy_sym != syminfo.ticker

// What the driver-timing rule did on this bar, for the panel: how many drivers
// were read a bar late, and the largest lateness KEPT, in hours - the most the
// last bar can still revise by.
f_lag(float lf) => lf > DRIVER_LATE ? 1 : 0
f_kept(float lf) => lf > DRIVER_LATE ? 0.0 : lf
int   drvLagged = f_lag(lf_JP10Y) + f_lag(lf_JP02Y) + f_lag(lf_CN10Y) + f_lag(lf_CN02Y) + f_lag(lf_EU10Y) +
     f_lag(lf_EU02Y) + f_lag(lf_GB10Y) + f_lag(lf_GB02Y) + f_lag(lf_US10Y) + f_lag(lf_US02Y) + f_lag(lf_US30Y) +
     f_lag(lf_IN10Y) + f_lag(lf_IN02Y) + f_lag(lf_USDINR) + f_lag(lf_EURINR) + f_lag(lf_GBPINR) + f_lag(lf_JPYINR) +
     f_lag(lf_GOLD) + f_lag(lf_SILVER) + f_lag(lf_USOIL) + f_lag(lf_DXY) + (customOk ? f_lag(lf_CUSTOM) : 0)
float drvKeptH  = math.max(f_kept(lf_JP10Y), f_kept(lf_JP02Y), f_kept(lf_CN10Y), f_kept(lf_CN02Y), f_kept(lf_EU10Y),
     f_kept(lf_EU02Y), f_kept(lf_GB10Y), f_kept(lf_GB02Y), f_kept(lf_US10Y), f_kept(lf_US02Y), f_kept(lf_US30Y),
     f_kept(lf_IN10Y), f_kept(lf_IN02Y), f_kept(lf_USDINR), f_kept(lf_EURINR), f_kept(lf_GBPINR), f_kept(lf_JPYINR),
     f_kept(lf_GOLD), f_kept(lf_SILVER), f_kept(lf_USOIL), f_kept(lf_DXY), customOk ? f_kept(lf_CUSTOM) : 0.0) *
     timeframe.in_seconds() / 3600.0

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  4. THE ANCHOR SAMPLE
//
//  One observation is taken per CLOSED anchor bar and pushed into a circular
//  buffer of length corrLen, carrying Y in column 0 and the nine factors in
//  columns 1-9. Incremental sums maintain the full 10x10 cross-product matrix in
//  O(1) per anchor bar, so the entire correlation matrix is available every bar
//  without a single ta.* call and without re-scanning the window.
//
//  Values are stored as deviations from a fixed per-series base captured at the
//  first insertion. Correlations and regression slopes are shift-invariant, so
//  this costs nothing — but it turns Σx² ≈ 21.2 with a variance of 0.0004 (log
//  DXY over a decade) into a well-conditioned sum, recovering roughly five
//  significant digits that naive centring throws away.
//
//  The sample is read `holdOut` anchor periods in arrears via a small FIFO, so
//  the coefficients applied to any period were fitted on data that ended holdOut
//  periods earlier - which is what makes the skill measure in section 10
//  genuinely out-of-sample. A queue rather than a history offset because holdOut
//  is counted in ANCHOR periods: 12 daily periods is 900 chart bars on a
//  5-minute chart, far past any sane max_bars_back.
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
int  anchorT   = time(anchorTF)
bool newAnchor = not na(anchorT) and not na(anchorT[1]) and anchorT != anchorT[1]

var matrix<float> BUF  = matrix.new<float>(corrLen, 10, 0.0)
var matrix<float> MS   = matrix.new<float>(10, 10, 0.0)
var array<float>  VS   = array.new_float(10, 0.0)
var array<float>  BASE = array.new_float(10, na)
var array<float>  CUR  = array.new_float(10, na)
var array<float>  INS  = array.new_float(10, na)
var array<float>  QQ   = array.new_float(0)
var int nObs = 0
var int wptr = 0

// ANCHOR-PERIOD RETURNS. An observation is the anchor period's OWN return: the
// sum of the chart-bar returns inside it, for the target and every factor. The
// port used to store the return of the period's LAST chart bar, which is the
// period's return only on a daily chart - on a 5m chart the whole macro fit ran
// on one closing 5-minute return a day, the hedge's measured skill collapsed,
// and value carried no macro information. Accumulated on every bar below.
var array<float> ACC = array.new_float(10, 0.0)
var int accYn    = 0      // bars with a target return in the open period
var int accBars  = 0      // chart bars in the open period
var int anchN    = 0      // closed periods seen
var int anchBars = 0      // chart bars across them

if newAnchor
    // the period that just CLOSED ended on the previous chart bar - never the
    // forming one, so nothing here can repaint
    array.set(CUR, 0, accYn > 0 ? array.get(ACC, 0) : na)
    for i = 1 to 8
        array.set(CUR, i, array.get(ACC, i))
    array.set(CUR, 9, customOk ? array.get(ACC, 9) : na)
    anchN    += 1
    anchBars += accBars

    // MISSING DATA IS PER-COLUMN, NEVER PER-ROW. Discarding a whole observation
    // because one factor is absent would empty the sample entirely whenever a
    // single symbol is outside the user's data plan — or simply whenever the
    // custom driver is switched off, which blanks column 9 by design. A missing
    // return is a zero: there was no change to observe. A factor that is NEVER
    // available stays constant, so its variance is zero, its correlation is zero
    // by the guard in f_corr, and it can never be selected. The column
    // disqualifies itself instead of taking the sample down.
    for i = 1 to 9
        if na(array.get(CUR, i))
            array.set(CUR, i, 0.0)

    // Y is the one column with no substitute: no dependent variable, no
    // observation.
    if not na(array.get(CUR, 0))
        for i = 0 to 9
            array.push(QQ, array.get(CUR, i))

        if array.size(QQ) >= (holdOut + 1) * 10
            for i = 0 to 9
                array.set(INS, i, array.shift(QQ))

            // base-shift against the first value each column ever showed
            for i = 0 to 9
                float vv = array.get(INS, i)
                if na(array.get(BASE, i)) and not na(vv)
                    array.set(BASE, i, vv)
                float bb = array.get(BASE, i)
                array.set(INS, i, na(vv) or na(bb) ? 0.0 : vv - bb)

            if nObs >= corrLen
                for i = 0 to 9
                    float oi = matrix.get(BUF, wptr, i)
                    array.set(VS, i, array.get(VS, i) - oi)
                    for j = 0 to 9
                        matrix.set(MS, i, j, matrix.get(MS, i, j) - oi * matrix.get(BUF, wptr, j))
                nObs := nObs - 1

            for i = 0 to 9
                float ci = array.get(INS, i)
                matrix.set(BUF, wptr, i, ci)
                array.set(VS, i, array.get(VS, i) + ci)
            for i = 0 to 9
                for j = 0 to 9
                    matrix.set(MS, i, j, matrix.get(MS, i, j) + matrix.get(BUF, wptr, i) * matrix.get(BUF, wptr, j))

            nObs := nObs + 1
            wptr := (wptr + 1) % corrLen

// open a new period on its first bar, then add THIS bar to the open period
if newAnchor
    for i = 0 to 9
        array.set(ACC, i, 0.0)
    accYn   := 0
    accBars := 0
array.set(ACC, 0, array.get(ACC, 0) + nz(yFit))
array.set(ACC, 1, array.get(ACC, 1) + nz(fac1))
array.set(ACC, 2, array.get(ACC, 2) + nz(fac2))
array.set(ACC, 3, array.get(ACC, 3) + nz(fac3))
array.set(ACC, 4, array.get(ACC, 4) + nz(fac4))
array.set(ACC, 5, array.get(ACC, 5) + nz(fac5))
array.set(ACC, 6, array.get(ACC, 6) + nz(fac6))
array.set(ACC, 7, array.get(ACC, 7) + nz(fac7))
array.set(ACC, 8, array.get(ACC, 8) + nz(fac8))
array.set(ACC, 9, array.get(ACC, 9) + nz(fac9))
if not na(yFit)
    accYn += 1
accBars += 1

float nF = nObs

// ── moment accessors ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
f_mean(i, n, vs, base) => n > 0 ? array.get(vs, i) / n + nz(array.get(base, i)) : na

f_sd(i, n, vs, ms) =>
    float s = array.get(vs, i)
    float v = n > 1 ? (matrix.get(ms, i, i) - s * s / n) / n : na
    na(v) ? na : math.sqrt(math.max(v, 0.0))

f_corr(i, j, n, vs, ms) =>
    float sx  = array.get(vs, i)
    float sy  = array.get(vs, j)
    float cNum = n * matrix.get(ms, i, j) - sx * sy
    float dx  = n * matrix.get(ms, i, i) - sx * sx
    float dy  = n * matrix.get(ms, j, j) - sy * sy
    float cDen = math.sqrt(math.max(dx, 0.0) * math.max(dy, 0.0))
    n > 2 and cDen > 1e-12 ? math.max(-1.0, math.min(1.0, cNum / cDen)) : 0.0

// partial correlation of x and y controlling for z, from pairwise correlations
f_part(rxy, rxz, ryz) =>
    float d = math.sqrt(math.max(1e-12, (1.0 - rxz * rxz) * (1.0 - ryz * ryz)))
    math.max(-1.0, math.min(1.0, (rxy - rxz * ryz) / d))

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  5. SIGNIFICANCE FLOOR SIZED TO THE SAMPLE ACTUALLY AVAILABLE
//
//  Fisher (1921): z = artanh(r) is approximately normal with SE = 1/√(n−3−k)
//  for a partial correlation with k controls. Inverting gives the smallest |ρ|
//  distinguishable from zero at level α:  |ρ|* = tanh(z_α / √(n−3−k)).
//
//  Šidák over the 9 candidates because we keep the best of nine, not a
//  pre-specified one: α′ = 1 − (1−0.05)^(1/9) ≈ 0.005683, two-sided z = 2.7655.
//
//     n = 200 → 0.195     n = 60 → 0.354     n = 40 → 0.431     n = 30 → 0.495
//
//  This is the whole answer to a short-history instrument. A fixed 0.10 floor
//  on 40 observations admits pure noise and calls it a macro driver.
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
f_svTanh(float x) =>
    float e = math.exp(math.min(20.0, 2.0 * math.abs(x)))
    float t = (e - 1.0) / (e + 1.0)
    x < 0 ? -t : t

float zCrit = sigCorrect ? 2.7655 : 1.9600

f_critR(n, k, z) =>
    float df = n - 3.0 - k
    df > 0.0 ? f_svTanh(z / math.sqrt(df)) : 1.0

float critR2 = f_critR(nF, 1, zCrit)
float critR3 = f_critR(nF, 2, zCrit)
float floor2 = math.max(minRho, critR2)
float floor3 = math.max(minRho, critR3)
bool  enoughData = nObs >= minObs

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  6. SELECTION  ·  closed-form stepwise partial correlation
//
//  Every score comes off the anchor correlation matrix, so this is pure
//  arithmetic — safe inside loops, and identical on every chart timeframe.
//  A near-duplicate of an already-chosen driver has partial correlation ≈0 by
//  construction and cannot be picked.
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
f_argmax(scores, exA, exB) =>
    int   best  = 0
    float bestv = -1.0
    for i = 0 to array.size(scores) - 1
        if i != exA and i != exB
            float v = math.abs(array.get(scores, i))
            if v > bestv
                bestv := v
                best  := i
    best

// HYSTERESIS. Candidates inside the margin are a statistical tie; swapping
// between them on noise rotates the basket for no informational gain.
f_hold(prev, rawIdx, scores, marg, exA, exB) =>
    int out = rawIdx
    if not na(prev) and prev != rawIdx and prev != exA and prev != exB
        float sInc = math.abs(array.get(scores, prev))
        float sCha = math.abs(array.get(scores, rawIdx))
        out := sCha > sInc * (1.0 + marg) ? rawIdx : prev
    out

// step 1 · marginal correlation with Y
var array<float> S1 = array.new_float(9, 0.0)
for i = 0 to 8
    array.set(S1, i, f_corr(0, i + 1, nF, VS, MS))

var int hIdx1 = na
int raw1 = f_argmax(S1, -1, -1)
hIdx1 := f_hold(hIdx1, raw1, S1, hystMarg, -1, -1)
int idx1 = hIdx1

// step 2 · partial correlation given driver 1
var array<float> S2 = array.new_float(9, 0.0)
float ry1 = array.get(S1, idx1)
for i = 0 to 8
    float rk1 = f_corr(i + 1, idx1 + 1, nF, VS, MS)
    array.set(S2, i, i == idx1 ? 0.0 : f_part(array.get(S1, i), ry1, rk1))

var int hIdx2 = na
int raw2 = selStep ? f_argmax(S2, idx1, -1) : f_argmax(S1, idx1, -1)
hIdx2 := f_hold(hIdx2, raw2, selStep ? S2 : S1, hystMarg, idx1, -1)
int idx2 = hIdx2

// step 3 · partial correlation given drivers 1 and 2
var array<float> S3 = array.new_float(9, 0.0)
float r12  = f_corr(idx1 + 1, idx2 + 1, nF, VS, MS)
float ry2  = array.get(S1, idx2)
float ry21 = f_part(ry2, ry1, r12)
for i = 0 to 8
    float rk1  = f_corr(i + 1, idx1 + 1, nF, VS, MS)
    float rk2  = f_corr(i + 1, idx2 + 1, nF, VS, MS)
    float rk21 = f_part(rk2, rk1, r12)
    float ryk1 = f_part(array.get(S1, i), ry1, rk1)
    array.set(S3, i, i == idx1 or i == idx2 ? 0.0 : f_part(ryk1, ry21, rk21))

var int hIdx3 = na
int raw3 = selStep ? f_argmax(S3, idx1, idx2) : f_argmax(S1, idx1, idx2)
hIdx3 := f_hold(hIdx3, raw3, selStep ? S3 : S1, hystMarg, idx1, idx2)
int idx3 = hIdx3

// ── admission ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
float q2 = math.abs(selStep ? array.get(S2, idx2) : array.get(S1, idx2))
float q3 = math.abs(selStep ? array.get(S3, idx3) : array.get(S1, idx3))

int kEff = 1
kEff := inpNumVars >= 2 and q2 >= floor2 ? 2 : 1
kEff := inpNumVars >= 3 and q2 >= floor2 and q3 >= floor3 ? 3 : kEff

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  7. ROTATION DETECTION
//  Set membership, not order: swapping ranks 2 and 3 reorders Gram-Schmidt but
//  spans the same subspace, so the fit is unchanged and it is not a rotation.
//  Carried in because Samanvaya's ▲▼ are only tradable once the basket has
//  been stable for a representative window.
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
f_inSet(v, a, b, c) => v == a or v == b or v == c

int pv1 = nz(idx1[1], idx1)
int pv2 = nz(idx2[1], idx2)
int pv3 = nz(idx3[1], idx3)
bool sameSet = f_inSet(idx1, pv1, pv2, pv3) and f_inSet(idx2, pv1, pv2, pv3) and f_inSet(idx3, pv1, pv2, pv3)
bool rotated = not barstate.isfirst and (not sameSet or kEff != nz(kEff[1], kEff))

var int barsSinceRot = 0
barsSinceRot := rotated ? 0 : barsSinceRot + 1
bool basketWarm = barsSinceRot >= Z3

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  8. REGRESSION  ·  Gram-Schmidt in correlation space, with ridge
//
//  Working in correlation space means var(u₁) = 1 exactly, so the ridge penalty
//  reads directly as a fraction and the orthogonalization needs nothing but the
//  correlation matrix. Coefficients are then converted back to natural units so
//  they can be applied to chart-timeframe data.
//
//  λ SCALES WITH 1/n. The optimal ridge penalty grows as the noise-to-signal
//  ratio of the estimate grows, and that ratio is ~1/n. A 50-observation
//  history therefore gets four times the shrinkage of a 200-observation one.
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
float lamEff = ridgeLam * math.max(1.0, 200.0 / math.max(nF, 1.0))

float r13 = f_corr(idx1 + 1, idx3 + 1, nF, VS, MS)
float r23 = f_corr(idx2 + 1, idx3 + 1, nF, VS, MS)
float ry3 = array.get(S1, idx3)

float a21 = r12
float vu2 = math.max(0.0, 1.0 - r12 * r12)
float a31 = r13
float a32 = vu2 > 1e-10 ? (r23 - r13 * r12) / vu2 : 0.0
float vu3 = math.max(0.0, 1.0 - r13 * r13 - a32 * a32 * vu2)

float c1 = ry1
float c2 = ry2 - a21 * ry1
float c3 = ry3 - a31 * ry1 - a32 * c2

float gb1 = kEff >= 1 ? c1 / (1.0 + lamEff) : 0.0
float gb2 = kEff >= 2 ? c2 / (vu2 + lamEff) : 0.0
float gb3 = kEff >= 3 ? c3 / (vu3 + lamEff) : 0.0

// back-substitution to per-driver weights on the standardized axes:
//   u₃ = z₃ − a₃₂z₂ − (a₃₁ − a₃₂a₂₁)z₁ ,  u₂ = z₂ − a₂₁z₁
float w3 = gb3
float w2 = gb2 - a32 * gb3
float w1 = gb1 - a21 * gb2 - (a31 - a21 * a32) * gb3

float sdY = f_sd(0, nF, VS, MS)
float sd1 = f_sd(idx1 + 1, nF, VS, MS)
float sd2 = f_sd(idx2 + 1, nF, VS, MS)
float sd3 = f_sd(idx3 + 1, nF, VS, MS)

// natural-unit hedge ratios: move in Y per one unit of the driver
float h1 = not na(sd1) and sd1 > 1e-12 and not na(sdY) ? w1 * sdY / sd1 : 0.0
float h2 = not na(sd2) and sd2 > 1e-12 and not na(sdY) ? w2 * sdY / sd2 : 0.0
float h3 = not na(sd3) and sd3 > 1e-12 and not na(sdY) ? w3 * sdY / sd3 : 0.0

float mY  = f_mean(0, nF, VS, BASE)
float mX1 = f_mean(idx1 + 1, nF, VS, BASE)
float mX2 = f_mean(idx2 + 1, nF, VS, BASE)
float mX3 = f_mean(idx3 + 1, nF, VS, BASE)
float icept = na(mY) ? na : mY - h1 * mX1 - h2 * mX2 - h3 * mX3

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  9. APPLYING THE ANCHORED MODEL TO CHART-TIMEFRAME DATA
//
//  The intercept is the one quantity that does not transfer unchanged: it is a
//  drift per anchor bar, so it is scaled by the ratio of chart bar to anchor
//  bar — a weekly alpha spread across the bars that make up the week.
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
float secChart  = timeframe.in_seconds(timeframe.period)
float secAnchor = timeframe.in_seconds(anchorTF)
float tfRatio   = secAnchor > 0 ? math.min(1.0, secChart / secAnchor) : 1.0
// The intercept is a drift per anchor PERIOD. It is spread over the chart bars a
// period actually holds - measured, so a 6¼-hour session is not treated as 24
// hours - with the clock ratio only until the first period has closed.
float barsPerAnchor = anchN > 0 ? float(anchBars) / anchN : (tfRatio > 0 ? 1.0 / tfRatio : 1.0)
float iceptUse  = na(icept) ? na : icept / math.max(1.0, barsPerAnchor)

float f1c = idx1 == 0 ? fac1 : idx1 == 1 ? fac2 : idx1 == 2 ? fac3 : idx1 == 3 ? fac4 : idx1 == 4 ? fac5 : idx1 == 5 ? fac6 : idx1 == 6 ? fac7 : idx1 == 7 ? fac8 : fac9
float f2c = idx2 == 0 ? fac1 : idx2 == 1 ? fac2 : idx2 == 2 ? fac3 : idx2 == 3 ? fac4 : idx2 == 4 ? fac5 : idx2 == 5 ? fac6 : idx2 == 6 ? fac7 : idx2 == 7 ? fac8 : fac9
float f3c = idx3 == 0 ? fac1 : idx3 == 1 ? fac2 : idx3 == 2 ? fac3 : idx3 == 3 ? fac4 : idx3 == 4 ? fac5 : idx3 == 5 ? fac6 : idx3 == 6 ? fac7 : idx3 == 7 ? fac8 : fac9

float y_hat    = na(iceptUse) or na(yFit) ? na : iceptUse + h1 * nz(f1c) + h2 * nz(f2c) + h3 * nz(f3c)
float residFul = na(y_hat) or na(yFit) ? na : yFit - y_hat

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  10. THE HEDGE WEIGHS ITSELF
//
//  This is the one genuinely new mechanism, and it exists because the study
//  rejected the alternative. A full macro hedge predicted WORSE than no hedge
//  at every frequency tested, and the correlation between how much the basket
//  explained and whether hedging helped was +0.11 — nothing. So the hedge is
//  applied in proportion to how well it has actually been working:
//
//      s = clip( 1 − Σε² / Σr² , 0 , 1 )     over the last skillWin anchor bars
//      ε_used = (1−s)·r_Y + s·(r_Y − ŷ)
//
//  s = 0 collapses the spread to Y's own cumulative return, which is the
//  benchmark that won the comparison. s = 1 is the full hedge, which is right
//  when the basket has earned it. TLT measured 0.97, INFY 0.04.
//
//  Both sums run on the ANCHOR clock and use the residual as it stood at each
//  closed anchor bar — coefficients that were genuinely in force at the time —
//  so this is measured skill, never a look-back-fitted number.
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
var array<float> SKQ = array.new_float(0)
var float SK_e = 0.0
var float SK_y = 0.0
var int   SK_n = 0

// Each skill observation is one anchor PERIOD: the residual and the target
// return summed over its chart bars, each with the coefficients in force then.
var float skRes = 0.0
var float skY   = 0.0
var int   skN   = 0

if newAnchor and skN > 0
    float ee = skRes * skRes
    float yy = skY * skY
    array.push(SKQ, ee)
    array.push(SKQ, yy)
    SK_e := SK_e + ee
    SK_y := SK_y + yy
    SK_n := SK_n + 1
    if SK_n > skillWin
        SK_e := SK_e - array.shift(SKQ)
        SK_y := SK_y - array.shift(SKQ)
        SK_n := SK_n - 1
if newAnchor
    skRes := 0.0
    skY   := 0.0
    skN   := 0
if not na(residFul) and not na(yFit)
    skRes += residFul
    skY   += yFit
    skN   += 1

float skillR2 = SK_n >= 20 and SK_y > 1e-18 ? 1.0 - SK_e / SK_y : na
float sAuto   = na(skillR2) ? 0.0 : math.max(0.0, math.min(1.0, skillR2))
float hedgeW  = hedgeMode == "Off" ? 0.0 : hedgeMode == "Full" ? 1.0 : sAuto

// ε_used interpolates between the raw return and the fully hedged residual.
float resid = na(yFit) ? na : na(residFul) ? yFit : yFit - hedgeW * (yFit - residFul)

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  11. THE SPREAD  ·  running sum of the residual return, i.e. the log P&L of
//  the book as actually hedged. At hedgeW = 0 this is simply Y's own path.
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
bool  modelBuilt = nObs >= 10
bool  modelReady = modelBuilt and not na(resid)
float spread     = ta.cum(modelReady ? resid : 0.0)

// ── ENSEMBLE MEMBERS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// One member per timescale: sample z of the spread over that window, then the
// high-pass. The drift correction is applied PER MEMBER before averaging, not
// after: each window has its own drift to remove, and averaging first would
// leave the fast members carrying the slow members' trend.
f_member(series float sp, simple int zl, simple int dl) =>
    float sd = ta.stdev(sp, zl)
    float m  = ta.sma(sp, zl)
    float z  = sd > 1e-14 ? (sp - m) / sd : 0.0
    float e  = ta.ema(z, dl)          // evaluated unconditionally, masked after
    useDrift ? z - e : z

float m1 = f_member(spread, Z1, driftLen)
float m2 = f_member(spread, Z2, driftLen)
float m3 = f_member(spread, Z3, driftLen)
float m4 = f_member(spread, Z4, driftLen)
float m5 = f_member(spread, Z5, driftLen)

// AVERAGING SHRINKS THE SCALE, AND θ HAS TO KEEP MEANING THE SAME THING.
// The five members are views of ONE spread, so they are highly correlated —
// measured mean pairwise ρ = 0.734 — and the mean of k correlated unit-variance
// series has sd √((1+(k−1)ρ)/k) = √((1+4·0.734)/5) = 0.887, which is exactly the
// ratio measured on real data (0.887 mean; 0.862-0.900 across ten instruments
// as different as TLT, INFY, COPPER and FTSE — a 4% spread, so a constant is
// safe here). Left uncorrected the ensemble would sit inside θ far more often:
// bars beyond |z|>2 fell from 10.2% to 5.8%, halving the entry rate silently
// while looking like a pure refactor. Dividing restores single-window
// semantics, and being a monotone rescale it cannot change rank information.
float ENS_SCALE = 0.887
float z_hp    = (m1 + m2 + m3 + m4 + m5) / 5.0 / ENS_SCALE
float z_score = z_hp

f_softBound(float x) =>
    x / (1.0 + math.abs(x))

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  LEG 2 · BREADTH  ·  the price-only half of Tattva's Swayam self-ensemble
//
//  Seven causal views of Y's OWN price: five timescales running the full Market
//  Strength Factor, plus the fastest as momentum-only and the slowest as
//  flow-only. Ported line-for-line from engines/swayam/kernel.py with the
//  primitives byte-matched to pandas — zscore_clipped's shift(1) and ddof=1,
//  ATR as ewm(alpha=1/14) ≡ ta.atr, close.diff(5).diff(5) expanded to
//  close − 2·close[5] + close[10], and the √n that cancels in the MSF combine.
//
//  The eight MACRO-anchored members of the full 15-member ensemble are absent
//  by necessity, not choice: they select from ~100 external series per bar,
//  which no Pine script can fetch. This is the price-action half, exactly.
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
f_sig(float x, float s) => 2.0 / (1.0 + math.exp(-x / s)) - 1.0
// Locals renamed from m1/sd1 to zcM/zcSd: in the standalone Swayam port those
// names were free, but this file defines a top-level ensemble member m1 and a
// driver deviation sd1, and a function local silently shadowing a global is a
// hazard whether or not the compiler allows it.
f_zc(float src, int win, float clp) =>
    float zcM  = ta.sma(src, win)[1]
    float zcSd = ta.stdev(src, win, false)[1]
    float mm = na(zcM) ? 0.0 : zcM
    float z = (na(zcSd) or zcSd == 0.0) ? na : (src - mm) / zcSd
    na(z) ? 0.0 : math.max(-clp, math.min(clp, z))
f_msf(int _len, int _roc, int _mask) =>
    float roc_raw = (close - close[_roc]) / close[_roc]
    float momentum_norm = f_sig(f_zc(roc_raw, _len, clipZ), 1.5)
    float intrabar_dir = (high + low) / 2.0 - open
    float vol_ma = ta.sma(volume, _len)
    float vol_ratio = (na(vol_ma) or vol_ma == 0.0) ? 1.0 : volume / vol_ma
    float vw_direction = ta.sma(intrabar_dir * vol_ratio, _len)
    float vw_impact = ta.sma((close - close[5]) * vol_ratio, _len)
    float micro_norm = f_sig(f_zc(vw_direction - vw_impact, _len, clipZ), 1.5)
    float trend_slow = ta.sma(close, _len)
    float trend_diff_z = f_zc(ta.sma(close, 5) - trend_slow, _len, clipZ)
    float mom_accel_z = f_zc(close - 2.0 * close[5] + close[10], _len, clipZ)
    float atrv = ta.atr(14)
    float vol_adj_mom_z = f_zc((close - close[5]) / atrv, _len, clipZ)
    float mean_rev_z = f_zc(close - trend_slow, _len, clipZ)
    float composite_trend_z = (trend_diff_z + mom_accel_z + vol_adj_mom_z + mean_rev_z) / 2.0 
    float composite_trend_norm = f_sig(composite_trend_z, 1.5)
    float typical_price = (high + low + close) / 3.0
    float mf = typical_price * volume
    float mf_pos = close > close[1] ? mf : 0.0
    float mf_neg = close < close[1] ? mf : 0.0
    float mf_pos_smooth = ta.sma(mf_pos, _len)
    float mf_neg_smooth = ta.sma(mf_neg, _len)
    float mf_total = mf_pos_smooth + mf_neg_smooth
    float accum_ratio = (na(mf_total) or mf_total == 0.0) ? 0.5 : mf_pos_smooth / mf_total
    float accum_norm = 2.0 * (accum_ratio - 0.5)
    float pctc = (close - close[1]) / close[1]
    float regime_signal = pctc > 0.0033 ? 1.0 : pctc < -0.0033 ? -1.0 : 0.0
    float regime_count = ta.cum(regime_signal)
    float regime_norm = f_sig(f_zc(regime_count - ta.sma(regime_count, _len), _len, clipZ), 1.5)
    float structure = (micro_norm + composite_trend_norm) / math.sqrt(2.0)
    float flow = (accum_norm + regime_norm) / math.sqrt(2.0)
    float msum = _mask == 0 ? (momentum_norm + structure + flow) : _mask == 1 ? momentum_norm : _mask == 2 ? structure : flow
    f_sig(msum, 1.0)

f_unified_msf(float _msf, float _rs, float _bw) =>
    float msf_cl = math.pow(math.abs(_msf), _rs)
    float msf_w_ad = msf_cl / (msf_cl + 0.001) 
    float msf_w_final = 0.5 * _bw + 0.5 * msf_w_ad
    float mmr_w_final = 0.5 * (1.0 - _bw)
    float msf_w_norm = msf_w_final / (msf_w_final + mmr_w_final)
    math.max(-1.0, math.min(1.0, msf_w_norm * _msf))

int rb1 = int(math.max(5.0, math.round(rocFrac * B1)))
int rb2 = int(math.max(5.0, math.round(rocFrac * B2)))
int rb3 = int(math.max(5.0, math.round(rocFrac * B3)))
int rb4 = int(math.max(5.0, math.round(rocFrac * B4)))
int rb5 = int(math.max(5.0, math.round(rocFrac * B5)))

// ONE DEFINITION of the breadth leg, run by the chart and by every value rung,
// so a rung's breadth is the same quantity the chart blends: five timescales at
// full MSF, the fastest as momentum only and the slowest as flow only.
//
// VOLUME DEGENERACY. Flow is the only MSF component that needs real volume, so
// a flow-ONLY member is dropped on instruments that do not report it (index
// levels, several FX and futures feeds). Streaming approximation of Tattva's
// full-sample (volume>0).mean() >= 0.5 test.
f_breadthRaw() =>
    float br0 = f_unified_msf(f_msf(B1, rb1, 0), regimeSens, baseWeight)
    float br1 = f_unified_msf(f_msf(B2, rb2, 0), regimeSens, baseWeight)
    float br2 = f_unified_msf(f_msf(B3, rb3, 0), regimeSens, baseWeight)
    float br3 = f_unified_msf(f_msf(B4, rb4, 0), regimeSens, baseWeight)
    float br4 = f_unified_msf(f_msf(B5, rb5, 0), regimeSens, baseWeight)
    float br5 = f_unified_msf(f_msf(B1, rb1, 1), regimeSens, baseWeight)   // fastest · momentum
    float br6 = f_unified_msf(f_msf(B5, rb5, 3), regimeSens, baseWeight)   // slowest · flow
    float bVp = ta.cum((na(volume) or volume == 0.0) ? 0.0 : 1.0)
    bool  bVo = (bVp / (bar_index + 1)) >= 0.5
    [(br0 + br1 + br2 + br3 + br4 + br5 + (bVo ? br6 : 0.0)) * 10.0 / (bVo ? 7.0 : 6.0), bVo]

// A value rung's breadth, held na until the slowest member has a full window.
f_breadth() =>
    [bZ, bVo] = f_breadthRaw()
    bar_index > 2 * B5 + 20 ? bZ : na

[breadth_z, volOk] = f_breadthRaw()

// Each member is bounded by (baseWeight+1)/2, so at baseWeight = 0 every one
// lies in (-0.5, 0.5) and their mean ×10 in (-5, 5) — measured range over
// 145,411 member-bars was -4.375 .. +4.481. In these units the mean has
// sd ≈ 0.97, i.e. already a unit-variance score, so it blends against the RV
// z directly with no rescale.
float rv_z      = z_score

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  INTEGRATION  ·  blend in z-space, restore variance by measurement, bound once
//
//  For legs with weights w and (1−w), unit variance and correlation ρ, the mix
//  has sd √(w² + (1−w)² + 2w(1−w)ρ). Dividing by that restores amplitude for ANY
//  mix and degenerates correctly at the endpoints: at w = 0.5, ρ = 0 it is the
//  familiar √2 = 1.414, at w = 0.5, ρ = 0.71 (measured here) it is 1.081, and at
//  w = 0 or 1 it is exactly 1 because a single leg needs no restoration.
//  The soft-bound is applied ONCE, after the blend — never to a leg first.
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
float rho_legs = ta.correlation(rv_z, breadth_z, varCorrLen)
float rho_c    = math.max(-0.90, math.min(0.99, nz(rho_legs, 0.71)))
float wRV      = legMix
float wBR      = 1.0 - legMix
float mixVar   = wRV * wRV + wBR * wBR + 2.0 * wRV * wBR * rho_c
float varCorr  = mixVar > 1e-9 ? 1.0 / math.sqrt(mixVar) : 1.0

float unified_z   = (wRV * rv_z + wBR * breadth_z) * varCorr
// Samanvaya's pane reading, on its ±100 scale - the value trace its ▲▼ read.
float unified_osc = math.max(-100.0, math.min(100.0, f_softBound(unified_z * gain) * 100.0))

// The value tape's grading knee: θ entry on Samanvaya's own ±100 scale.
float osc_entry = f_softBound(entryThresh * gain) * 100.0

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  MTF LADDER · the engine behind the value tape                 display-only
//
//  Driven by its own "MTF value ladder" input, default Ladder up as in
//  Samanvaya, independent of the conviction ladder. Rung choice is Samanvaya's:
//  up to five frames above the chart (weekly adds M), or the THREE nearest below
//  it, breadth only. Verified line for line against samanvaya.pine; the one
//  addition is FALLBACK - a direction with no frames here reads the other.
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
var array<int> TP_UP = array.from(240, 60, 30, 15, 5)
var array<int> TP_DN = array.from(240, 60, 30, 15, 5, 3, 1)      // nearest first

int  tpSec   = timeframe.in_seconds()
int  tpTm    = int(math.round(tpSec / 60.0))
bool tpMin   = timeframe.isintraday and tpSec >= 60 and tpSec % 60 == 0
bool tpDay   = timeframe.isdaily   and timeframe.multiplier == 1
bool tpWeek  = timeframe.isweekly  and timeframe.multiplier == 1
bool tpMonth = timeframe.ismonthly and timeframe.multiplier == 1
bool tpUpAv  = tpDay or tpWeek or (tpMin and tpTm < 240 and 240 % tpTm == 0)
bool tpDnAv  = tpMonth or tpWeek or tpDay or (tpMin and tpTm > 1)
bool vWantUp = inpVLad == "Ladder up"
bool vWantDn = inpVLad == "Ladder down"
bool tpUp    = (vWantUp and tpUpAv) or (vWantDn and not tpDnAv and tpUpAv)
bool tpDn    = (vWantDn and tpDnAv) or (vWantUp and not tpUpAv and tpDnAv)
bool tpFallback = (vWantUp and tpDn) or (vWantDn and tpUp)

// Up: the frames above the chart, top → down. Down: the three nearest below.
string up1 = ""
string up2 = ""
string up3 = ""
string up4 = ""
string up5 = ""
string dn1 = ""
string dn2 = ""
string dn3 = ""
if tpUp
    if tpDay
        up1 := "W"
    else if tpWeek
        up1 := "M"
    else if tpMin and tpTm < 240
        int nU = 0
        for f in TP_UP
            if f > tpTm and f % tpTm == 0
                nU += 1
                string sf = str.tostring(f)
                if nU == 1
                    up1 := sf
                else if nU == 2
                    up2 := sf
                else if nU == 3
                    up3 := sf
                else if nU == 4
                    up4 := sf
                else
                    up5 := sf
if tpDn
    if tpMonth
        dn1 := "W"
        dn2 := "D"
    else if tpWeek
        dn1 := "D"
        dn2 := "240"
        dn3 := "60"
    else if tpDay
        dn1 := "240"
        dn2 := "60"
        dn3 := "30"
    else if tpMin
        int nD = 0
        for f in TP_DN
            if nD < 3 and f < tpTm and tpTm % f == 0
                nD += 1
                string sf = str.tostring(f)
                if nD == 1
                    dn1 := sf
                else if nD == 2
                    dn2 := sf
                else
                    dn3 := sf
bool tpMultiUp = up1 != ""
bool tpMultiDn = dn1 != ""
bool tpOn      = tpMultiUp or tpMultiDn

f_tpLbl(string t) =>
    string o = t
    if t != "" and t != "D" and t != "W" and t != "M"
        int n = int(str.tonumber(t))
        o := n % 60 == 0 ? str.tostring(n / 60) + "h" : t + "m"
    o
f_tpJoin(string acc, string t) => t == "" ? acc : acc + f_tpLbl(t) + "·"
string tpChartLbl = tpMin ? f_tpLbl(str.tostring(tpTm)) : tpDay ? "D" : tpWeek ? "W" : tpMonth ? "M" : timeframe.period
string tpLadLbl = tpMultiUp ? f_tpJoin(f_tpJoin(f_tpJoin(f_tpJoin(f_tpJoin("", up1), up2), up3), up4), up5) + tpChartLbl :
     f_tpJoin(f_tpJoin(f_tpJoin("", dn3), dn2), dn1) + tpChartLbl

// ── the breadth leg on a rung: f_breadth(), defined with the chart's leg ─────

// A higher frame hands over breadth at its last CLOSED bar, plus the open time
// of the bar now forming - known the moment it opens, so nothing leaks.
f_tpUpReq() =>
    float bZ = f_breadth()
    [bZ[1], time]

// A lower frame's breadth, averaged across its bars inside this chart bar.
f_tpInside(array<float> a) =>
    float s = 0.0
    int   k = 0
    if not na(a)
        for v in a
            if not na(v)
                s += v
                k += 1
    k > 0 ? s / k : na

// ── the RV leg on a higher frame, rebuilt from this chart's spread ───────────
// A higher frame's spread is this spread sampled at that frame's closes. Each
// rung keeps its settled samples and the settled drift EMA of each of the five
// ensemble members; every chart bar it finishes the ensemble with the current
// spread as the forming bar's close - the same arithmetic as section 12, in
// that frame's bars.
type TpRung
    array<float> smp      // settled samples, newest last
    array<float> ema      // per member: settled drift EMA
    array<float> s1       // per member: Σ of its last (window − 1) settled samples
    array<float> s2       // per member: Σ of their squares

f_tpZl(int k) => k == 0 ? Z1 : k == 1 ? Z2 : k == 2 ? Z3 : k == 3 ? Z4 : Z5

// A member's z with the forming value as the window's last observation. The
// window sums are kept rolling, so this is O(1) rather than a loop over up to
// 55 samples per member, per rung, per bar - the same numbers.
f_tpZ(TpRung r, int k, float cur) =>
    int   zl  = f_tpZl(k)
    float out = na
    if not na(cur) and array.size(r.smp) >= zl - 1
        float a1  = array.get(r.s1, k) + cur
        float a2  = array.get(r.s2, k) + cur * cur
        float mu  = a1 / zl
        float sdv = math.sqrt(math.max(a2 / zl - mu * mu, 0.0))
        out := sdv > 1e-14 ? (cur - mu) / sdv : 0.0
    out

// Freeze a closed parent bar: each member's final z enters its drift EMA, then
// the close enters the sample.
f_tpSettle(TpRung r, float cClosed, float al) =>
    for k = 0 to 4
        float zk = f_tpZ(r, k, cClosed)
        if not na(zk)
            float e0 = array.get(r.ema, k)
            array.set(r.ema, k, na(e0) ? zk : al * zk + (1.0 - al) * e0)
    array.push(r.smp, cClosed)
    int ns = array.size(r.smp)
    for k = 0 to 4
        int wn = f_tpZl(k) - 1
        array.set(r.s1, k, array.get(r.s1, k) + cClosed)
        array.set(r.s2, k, array.get(r.s2, k) + cClosed * cClosed)
        if ns > wn
            float vOut = array.get(r.smp, ns - 1 - wn)
            array.set(r.s1, k, array.get(r.s1, k) - vOut)
            array.set(r.s2, k, array.get(r.s2, k) - vOut * vOut)
    if array.size(r.smp) > Z5
        array.shift(r.smp)

// The forming parent bar: members finished with the current spread, the drift
// EMA advanced one step in arrears, exactly as ta.ema would at that close.
f_tpLive(TpRung r, float cur, float al) =>
    float acc = 0.0
    bool  okk = true
    for k = 0 to 4
        float zk = f_tpZ(r, k, cur)
        if na(zk)
            okk := false
        else
            float e0 = array.get(r.ema, k)
            float ek = na(e0) ? zk : al * zk + (1.0 - al) * e0
            acc += useDrift ? zk - ek : zk
    okk ? acc / 5.0 / ENS_SCALE : na

float tpAl = 2.0 / (driftLen + 1)

// A new parent bar opened on this chart bar, so the previous chart bar closed
// the last one: settle its spread, then read the forming bar live.
f_tpStep(TpRung r, int t) =>
    if not na(t) and not na(t[1]) and t != t[1] and modelBuilt[1]
        f_tpSettle(r, spread[1], tpAl)
    modelBuilt ? f_tpLive(r, spread, tpAl) : na

// Each rung blended as the pane blends: z-space, measured ρ, variance restored.
f_tpBlend(float rz, float bz, float rho) =>
    float out = na
    if not na(rz) and not na(bz)
        float rc = math.max(-0.90, math.min(0.99, nz(rho, 0.71)))
        float mv = wRV * wRV + wBR * wBR + 2.0 * wRV * wBR * rc
        out := (wRV * rz + wBR * bz) * (mv > 1e-9 ? 1.0 / math.sqrt(mv) : 1.0)
    else if not na(rz) and wRV > 0.0
        out := rz
    else if not na(bz) and wBR > 0.0
        out := bz
    out

// ── requests: up to five parents, up to three children ───────────────────────
float tpB1 = na
float tpB2 = na
float tpB3 = na
float tpB4 = na
float tpB5 = na
int   tpT1 = na
int   tpT2 = na
int   tpT3 = na
int   tpT4 = na
int   tpT5 = na
if tpMultiUp
    [ub1, ut1] = request.security(syminfo.tickerid, up1, f_tpUpReq(), lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_on)
    tpB1 := ub1
    tpT1 := ut1
    if up2 != ""
        [ub2, ut2] = request.security(syminfo.tickerid, up2, f_tpUpReq(), lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_on)
        tpB2 := ub2
        tpT2 := ut2
    if up3 != ""
        [ub3, ut3] = request.security(syminfo.tickerid, up3, f_tpUpReq(), lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_on)
        tpB3 := ub3
        tpT3 := ut3
    if up4 != ""
        [ub4, ut4] = request.security(syminfo.tickerid, up4, f_tpUpReq(), lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_on)
        tpB4 := ub4
        tpT4 := ut4
    if up5 != ""
        [ub5, ut5] = request.security(syminfo.tickerid, up5, f_tpUpReq(), lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_on)
        tpB5 := ub5
        tpT5 := ut5

float tpD1 = na
float tpD2 = na
float tpD3 = na
if tpMultiDn
    tpD1 := f_tpInside(request.security_lower_tf(syminfo.tickerid, dn1, f_breadth()))
    if dn2 != ""
        tpD2 := f_tpInside(request.security_lower_tf(syminfo.tickerid, dn2, f_breadth()))
    if dn3 != ""
        tpD3 := f_tpInside(request.security_lower_tf(syminfo.tickerid, dn3, f_breadth()))

// ── higher rungs: the whole composite at each frame ──────────────────────────
var TpRung tpR1 = TpRung.new(array.new_float(0), array.new_float(5, na), array.new_float(5, 0.0), array.new_float(5, 0.0))
var TpRung tpR2 = TpRung.new(array.new_float(0), array.new_float(5, na), array.new_float(5, 0.0), array.new_float(5, 0.0))
var TpRung tpR3 = TpRung.new(array.new_float(0), array.new_float(5, na), array.new_float(5, 0.0), array.new_float(5, 0.0))
var TpRung tpR4 = TpRung.new(array.new_float(0), array.new_float(5, na), array.new_float(5, 0.0), array.new_float(5, 0.0))
var TpRung tpR5 = TpRung.new(array.new_float(0), array.new_float(5, na), array.new_float(5, 0.0), array.new_float(5, 0.0))
float tpRv1 = f_tpStep(tpR1, tpT1)
float tpRv2 = f_tpStep(tpR2, tpT2)
float tpRv3 = f_tpStep(tpR3, tpT3)
float tpRv4 = f_tpStep(tpR4, tpT4)
float tpRv5 = f_tpStep(tpR5, tpT5)
float tpU1 = f_tpBlend(tpRv1, tpB1, ta.correlation(tpRv1, tpB1, varCorrLen))
float tpU2 = f_tpBlend(tpRv2, tpB2, ta.correlation(tpRv2, tpB2, varCorrLen))
float tpU3 = f_tpBlend(tpRv3, tpB3, ta.correlation(tpRv3, tpB3, varCorrLen))
float tpU4 = f_tpBlend(tpRv4, tpB4, ta.correlation(tpRv4, tpB4, varCorrLen))
float tpU5 = f_tpBlend(tpRv5, tpB5, ta.correlation(tpRv5, tpB5, varCorrLen))

// ── the value bias ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Samanvaya's MTF bias exactly: the ladder mean of z, NOT variance-restored -
// frames that disagree pull it toward zero, so a bright tape means the
// dislocation stands across horizons, not on one. Positive = RICH, negative =
// CHEAP, in Samanvaya's polarity. Only the tape's colours are flipped (below).
f_tpA(float z) => na(z) ? 0.0 : z
f_tpC(float z) => na(z) ? 0 : 1
float tpSum = 0.0
int   tpCnt = 0
if tpOn and modelBuilt
    tpSum := unified_z + f_tpA(tpU1) + f_tpA(tpU2) + f_tpA(tpU3) + f_tpA(tpU4) + f_tpA(tpU5) +
         f_tpA(tpD1) + f_tpA(tpD2) + f_tpA(tpD3)
    tpCnt := 1 + f_tpC(tpU1) + f_tpC(tpU2) + f_tpC(tpU3) + f_tpC(tpU4) + f_tpC(tpU5) +
         f_tpC(tpD1) + f_tpC(tpD2) + f_tpC(tpD3)
bool  tpReady = tpOn and modelBuilt and tpCnt > 1
float tpBiasZ = tpCnt > 0 ? tpSum / tpCnt : 0.0
float tpBias  = math.max(-100.0, math.min(100.0, f_softBound(tpBiasZ * gain) * 100.0))


// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  5 · THE PANE AND THE TAPES
//
//  PANE        the TRACE chosen by 'Trace' - conviction × value by default,
//              or either ingredient alone - and its histogram, the trace
//              minus its signal EMA. On the Conviction trace it is the
//              single-frame engine (Nishchaya v3), bit-identical.
//  MTF CONV.   every rung's z - the chart's included - averaged in linear space
//              and compressed ONCE, with no second normalization. Each rung is
//              calibrated on its own, so agreement across frames is RARER than
//              any one frame's reading. A rung whose history starts later joins
//              when it can calibrate and moves the level, never the scale.
//  MTF VALUE   Samanvaya's MTF bias, computed in section 4c (tpBias).
//  MTF MOM.    each ladder's faster view minus its slower, in its tape's own
//              points - Dhṛti's on conviction, Samanvaya's on value. Computed
//              after the pane, because it needs chart conviction. Context only.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
f_acc(float z) => na(z) ? 0.0 : z
f_cnt(float z) => na(z) ? 0 : 1
float zSum = 0.0
int   zCnt = 0
if multi and sdOK
    zSum := zChart + f_acc(z1) + f_acc(z2) + f_acc(z3) + f_acc(z4) + f_acc(z5) +
         f_acc(y1) + f_acc(y2) + f_acc(y3) + f_acc(y4) + f_acc(y5) + f_acc(y6) + f_acc(y7)
    zCnt := 1 + f_cnt(z1) + f_cnt(z2) + f_cnt(z3) + f_cnt(z4) + f_cnt(z5) +
         f_cnt(y1) + f_cnt(y2) + f_cnt(y3) + f_cnt(y4) + f_cnt(y5) + f_cnt(y6) + f_cnt(y7)
float zLad      = zCnt > 0 ? zSum / zCnt : 0.0
float ladScaled = 100.0 * f_tanh(zLad)
float ladAgree  = inpSm > 1 ? ta.ema(ladScaled, inpSm) : ladScaled

// The conviction tape is ready once the chart rung is calibrated and a second
// rung has held for a full normalization window - the same warm-up the pane
// itself needs.
var int nLad = 0
if multi and sdOK and zCnt > 1
    nLad += 1
bool  ladReady  = nLad > inpNorm + inpSm

// ── the trace: what the pane draws ────────────────────────────────────────
// CONVICTION × VALUE blends the two one level up, the way Samanvaya blends its
// own legs: each ingredient in σ, weighted equally, the mix's variance
// restored with their measured correlation, bounded ONCE on Samanvaya's scale
// - so θ lands on the trace exactly where it lands on the value tape.
// CONVICTION is the single-frame engine alone, bit-identical to Nishchaya v3;
// VALUE is Samanvaya's value on this chart alone - the same series.
bool  trConv = inpTrace == "Conviction"
bool  trVal  = inpTrace == "Value"
bool  trCV   = not trConv and not trVal
bool  trHasC = not trVal
bool  trHasV = not trConv
float zcS    = inpSm > 1 ? ta.ema(3.0 * zChart, inpSm) : 3.0 * zChart     // conviction in σ, smoothed
float rhoCV  = ta.correlation(zcS, unified_z, varCorrLen)
float trZ    = (zcS + unified_z) / math.sqrt(2.0 * (1.0 + math.max(-0.90, math.min(0.99, nz(rhoCV, 0.0)))))
float trace  = trConv ? fast : trVal ? unified_osc : math.max(-100.0, math.min(100.0, f_softBound(trZ * gain) * 100.0))

// The rails the trace is read against: the conviction zones on its own scale;
// θ and ±60 - a 3σ stretch, twice the default θ - on Samanvaya's.
float trIn  = trConv ? inpZ1 : 100.0 * (0.5 * entryThresh) / (1.0 + 0.5 * entryThresh)
float trOut = trConv ? inpZ2 : 60.0

// ── the pane: the trace and its histogram ─────────────────────────────────
float osc  = trace
float sig  = ta.ema(osc, inpSig)
float hist = osc - sig

// Conviction itself, whichever trace is drawn - the ladder's chart rung, the
// divergence pivots, control for a ◆, and the grid's histogram, as Pragyam
// reads it. On the Conviction trace these ARE the trace and its histogram.
float cvOsc  = fast
float cvHist = cvOsc - ta.ema(cvOsc, inpSig)

// Warm-up. Conviction is clean once its histogram's σ window is - v3's own
// rule. The trace is ready once each ingredient it carries is - conviction
// calibrated, the value model built - plus, for the blend, a clean window for
// their correlation, then the histogram's own σ window. On the Conviction
// trace the two are the same count.
var int nOsc = 0
if sdOK
    nOsc += 1
bool cvReady = nOsc > inpNorm + inpSm + inpSig       // conviction's histSd window is clean
var int nTr = 0
if (not trHasC or sdOK) and (not trHasV or modelBuilt)
    nTr += 1
bool ready = nTr > (trHasC ? inpSm : 0) + (trCV ? varCorrLen : 0) + inpNorm + inpSig

// A blend DIMS while its ingredients point opposite ways - they cancel, so a
// trace near zero is not quiet - and, carrying value, while the basket settles
// after a rotation.
bool  trSplit = trCV and ready and (zcS > 0) != (unified_z > 0)
bool  trDim   = trHasV and ready and (trSplit or not basketWarm)

// ── the momentum tapes: faster − slower, on each ladder ────────────────────
// ONE construction for both instruments, as Dhṛti and Samanvaya each built
// theirs. When a ladder looks DOWN its tape is the faster view - the bar seen
// from inside - so momentum = tape − chart; when it looks UP the chart is the
// faster view, so momentum = chart − tape. The two sides are the same quantity
// on the same scale - chart conviction and the conviction tape are 100·tanh of z, the
// chart's value and the value tape 100·softbound of z - so the gap reads in
// the tape's own points. The direction is the one actually read, fallback
// included. Context only: no signal reads either, and neither requests data.
//
// CONVICTION (Dhṛti). Impulse at k·σ of its own history over the normalization
// window - the k the pane's trigger uses. σ is measured only once the ladder
// and the pane are both warm, so warm-up readings never set its scale.
float ladMom      = multiUp ? cvOsc - ladAgree : ladAgree - cvOsc
bool  ladMomReady = multi and ladReady and cvReady
float ladMomSd    = ta.stdev(ladMomReady ? ladMom : na, inpNorm)
float ladMomThr   = inpK * nz(ladMomSd, 0.0)
// Dhṛti's reading of it. Down: against conviction's side is the inside of the
// bars turning before the chart does. Up: with the conviction tape is the
// chart running ahead of the frames above; against it, a pullback inside them.
string ladMomTag  = multiUp ? ((ladMom > 0) == (ladAgree > 0) ? "running ahead" : "pullback") :
     ((ladMom > 0) == (cvOsc > 0) ? "building inside" : "turning inside")

// VALUE (Samanvaya), carried exactly: impulse at 0.5σ over Samanvaya's 200-bar
// window. REVERTING when it opposes the value tape - the fast end has turned
// back toward fair value; WIDENING when it agrees.
float tpMom   = tpMultiUp ? unified_osc - tpBias : tpBias - unified_osc
float tpMomSd = ta.stdev(tpMom, varCorrLen)
float tpThr   = 0.5 * nz(tpMomSd, 0.0)
bool  tpRev   = (tpMom > 0) != (tpBias > 0)

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  6 · THE MAGNITUDE GATE
//
//  The histogram must cross ± k·σ of its OWN distribution. ta.crossover(hist, 0)
//  fires whenever two lines touch, which near zero is constantly.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
float histSd = ta.stdev(hist, inpNorm)
float thr    = inpK * nz(histSd, 0.0)

bool confirmed = barstate.isconfirmed

bool impUp = ta.crossover (hist,  thr)
bool impDn = ta.crossunder(hist, -thr)

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  6b · THE SIGNAL STACK · what every signal reads
//
//  Every signal in the pane - ▲▼ declaration, ◆ continuation, R H divergence - is ONE
//  thesis stated on several layers, and needs all of them on the same closed bar:
//
//      CHART        this timeframe's own conviction: trace, histogram, zones,
//                   pivots, impulse gate
//      CONVICTION   the +82 tape, C: who controls across its ladder
//      VALUE        the −82 tape, V: rich (+) or cheap (−) across its ladder,
//                   knee at θ (osc_entry)
//
//  No tape is optional and none is a filter laid on afterwards: each family
//  asks each tape the question its thesis depends on, and a signal that cannot
//  ask - a tape warming, off, or with no rungs on this chart - does not fire.
//  The panel says which layer is missing. The thresholds are the tapes' own:
//  the trace's zones for conviction, θ for value. No new number is introduced.
//
//  "Rising two bars running" is spelled out rather than ta.rising(x, 2), which
//  is "above both of the last two values" and is satisfied by one jump - the
//  one-tick noise it is meant to exclude.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
bool  mtfCOk  = multi and ladReady
bool  mtfVOk  = tpReady
bool  stackOk = ready and mtfCOk and mtfVOk
float mC      = ladAgree
float mV      = tpBias
bool  mCUp    = ladAgree > nz(ladAgree[1], ladAgree) and nz(ladAgree[1]) > nz(ladAgree[2], nz(ladAgree[1]))
bool  mCDn    = ladAgree < nz(ladAgree[1], ladAgree) and nz(ladAgree[1]) < nz(ladAgree[2], nz(ladAgree[1]))
float mVLoW   = nz(ta.lowest (tpBias, inpDisloc), tpBias)  // cheapest the ladder got in the dislocation window
float mVHiW   = nz(ta.highest(tpBias, inpDisloc), tpBias)  // richest

// EFFORT → RESULT - the indicator's own thesis, as a signal layer. ABSORBED is
// the share of participation that became displacement sitting in the bottom
// fifth of its own history: heavy effort, little result.
bool  effAbs  = not na(effPct) and effPct <= 20.0
bool  absSeen = nz(ta.highest(effAbs ? 1.0 : 0.0, inpDisloc)) > 0.0

// LAYER OVERLAP - how much of each tape the trace already knows. Shown, not
// used: the signals require every layer, and this says how independent they are.
float rhoC = ta.correlation(ladAgree, osc, inpNorm)
float rhoV = ta.correlation(tpBias,   osc, inpNorm)

// WHY THE STACK CANNOT JUDGE, when it cannot - drawn on the pane and in the panel.
int    svNeed   = holdOut + 10
string stackWhy = not ready ? (trHasV and not modelBuilt ? "value model building · " + str.tostring(math.min(anchN, svNeed)) + "/" + str.tostring(svNeed) + " periods" :
                  trConv ? "chart warming" : "trace warming") :
     not mtfCOk ? (inpTape == "Off" ? "conviction tape off" : multi ? "conviction warming" : "no conviction frames on this chart") :
     not mtfVOk ? (inpVLad == "Off" ? "value tape off" : not tpOn ? "no value frames on this chart" :
                  not modelBuilt ? "value model building · " + str.tostring(math.min(anchN, svNeed)) + "/" + str.tostring(svNeed) + " periods" : "value warming") : ""

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  7 · DIVERGENCE ENGINE
//
//  Pivots are found on CONVICTION, whichever trace the pane draws: it is where
//  the divergence evidence was measured. Price is sampled at the true extreme
//  of the swing, not at whatever bar conviction happened to peak on. Measured
//  over GC/SI/CL on four timeframes
//  this raised divergence edge from +0.035R to +0.060R and lifted the number of
//  datasets it helped from 7/12 to 9/12; on a held-out set of eight other
//  instruments it improved 17/32 to 20/32.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
float pvH = ta.pivothigh(cvOsc, inpPL, inpPR)
float pvL = ta.pivotlow (cvOsc, inpPL, inpPR)

// the swing's own extreme, over the same window the pivot was confirmed across
float swingHi = ta.highest(high, inpPL + inpPR + 1)
float swingLo = ta.lowest (low,  inpPL + inpPR + 1)

var float lastHV = na
var int   lastHB = na
var float lastHP = na
var float lastHC = na          // MTF conviction at the last trace high
var float lastLV = na
var int   lastLB = na
var float lastLP = na
var float lastLC = na          // MTF conviction at the last trace low

// Both tapes as they stood AT the pivot, not at the confirmation bar.
float cPiv = ladAgree[inpPR]
float vPiv = tpBias[inpPR]

// THE DIVERGENCE FAMILY - the chart's pivot pattern, confirmed by both tapes.
//   REGULAR  the ladder fails to confirm too (conviction makes the same lower
//            high / higher low as chart conviction) at a price on the stretched side
//            of fair value (value > 0 at a top, < 0 at a bottom).
//   HIDDEN   the ladder is in control on the continuation side past the inner
//            zone, and value is not stretched past θ against it - the ◆ thesis
//            found at a pivot.
bool rBearT = stackOk and not na(lastHC) and cPiv < lastHC and vPiv > 0.0
bool rBullT = stackOk and not na(lastLC) and cPiv > lastLC and vPiv < 0.0
bool hBearT = stackOk and cPiv <= -inpZ1 and vPiv > -osc_entry
bool hBullT = stackOk and cPiv >=  inpZ1 and vPiv <  osc_entry

bool divBear = false
bool divBull = false
bool hidBear = false
bool hidBull = false

// Capped and recycled explicitly: the profile recreates its level lines on every
// last bar, so an uncapped connector pool would be what Pine evicted under
// pressure — silently, with nothing to say the pane is showing fewer divergences
// than it found.
var array<line> divLn = array.new<line>(0)
// On the Conviction trace the connector joins the two conviction pivots in the
// pane. On the others the drawn trace is not the series the pivots were found
// on, so it joins the two swing extremes on the PRICE chart instead.
f_connect(int b1, float v1, int b2, float v2, float q1, float q2, color c) =>
    if inpLine
        line ln = na
        if trConv
            ln := line.new(b1, v1, b2, v2, xloc.bar_index, extend.none, c, line.style_solid, 2)
        else
            ln := line.new(b1, q1, b2, q2, xloc.bar_index, extend.none, c, line.style_solid, 2, force_overlay = true)
        array.push(divLn, ln)
        if array.size(divLn) > DIV_LINES
            line.delete(array.shift(divLn))

if not na(pvH) and cvReady and confirmed
    int   b   = bar_index - inpPR
    float p   = swingHi
    int   gap = na(lastHB) ? 0 : b - lastHB
    if not na(lastHV) and gap >= inpGapM and gap <= inpGapX
        if inpDiv and pvH < lastHV and p > lastHP and (not inpGate or lastHV >= inpZ1) and rBearT
            divBear := true
            f_connect(lastHB, lastHV, b, pvH, lastHP, p, C_BEAR)
        if inpHid and pvH > lastHV and p < lastHP and hBearT
            hidBear := true
            f_connect(lastHB, lastHV, b, pvH, lastHP, p, color.new(C_BEAR, 45))
    lastHV := pvH
    lastHB := b
    lastHP := p
    lastHC := cPiv

if not na(pvL) and cvReady and confirmed
    int   b   = bar_index - inpPR
    float p   = swingLo
    int   gap = na(lastLB) ? 0 : b - lastLB
    if not na(lastLV) and gap >= inpGapM and gap <= inpGapX
        if inpDiv and pvL > lastLV and p < lastLP and (not inpGate or lastLV <= -inpZ1) and rBullT
            divBull := true
            f_connect(lastLB, lastLV, b, pvL, lastLP, p, C_BULL)
        if inpHid and pvL < lastLV and p > lastLP and hBullT
            hidBull := true
            f_connect(lastLB, lastLV, b, pvL, lastLP, p, color.new(C_BULL, 45))
    lastLV := pvL
    lastLB := b
    lastLP := p
    lastLC := cPiv

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  8 · SIGNALS · ▲▼ DECLARATIONS AND ◆ CONTINUATION
//
//  Indicator signals, not trades. A ▲ DECLARES buy and a ▼ declares sell; a
//  declaration stands until the opposite one and has no exit. The ◆ marks a
//  push resuming after a pullback. Nothing here opens, sizes or closes a
//  position. The header's SIGNALS section has every rule and its reasoning.
//
//  ARM, THEN CONFIRM. The arm's crossing is a one-bar event; the other layers
//  are states that turn a bar or two either side of it. Requiring all of them
//  on the crossing bar lost every ▲▼ whose layers were one bar apart, so the
//  crossing now ARMS a setup for the confirmation window and the ▲▼ fires on
//  the first closed bar inside it where every other layer agrees. A return into
//  the dislocation cancels the arm; the next crossing re-arms it.
//
//  Cooldown per direction: ▲ and long ◆ share one clock, ▼ and short ◆ the
//  other. A triangle takes precedence over a diamond on the same bar.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
var int lastLongBar  = na
var int lastShortBar = na
bool coolLong  = na(lastLongBar)  or (bar_index - lastLongBar)  >= inpCool
bool coolShort = na(lastShortBar) or (bar_index - lastShortBar) >= inpCool

// a genuine pullback: the histogram actually crossed to the other side
bool pulledUp = ta.lowest (hist, inpPull) <  0.0
bool pulledDn = ta.highest(hist, inpPull) >  0.0

// ── ARM · Samanvaya's trigger, on the trace when it carries value ───────────
// The blended or Value trace crossing back through θ - the stretch releasing;
// on the Conviction trace, Samanvaya's value on this chart, as it always was.
// On the Value trace the two are one series. Samanvaya's gate either way.
bool  svTradable = basketWarm and enoughData
float armSrc     = trConv ? unified_osc : osc
bool  svXLong    = ta.crossover (armSrc, -osc_entry)
bool  svXShort   = ta.crossunder(armSrc,  osc_entry)

var int armBuyBar  = na
var int armSellBar = na
if confirmed and svTradable and svXLong
    armBuyBar  := bar_index
    armSellBar := na
if confirmed and svTradable and svXShort
    armSellBar := bar_index
    armBuyBar  := na
// back inside the dislocation: the turn failed - wait for the next crossing
if confirmed and armSrc <= -osc_entry
    armBuyBar  := na
if confirmed and armSrc >=  osc_entry
    armSellBar := na
bool armedBuy  = not na(armBuyBar)  and bar_index - armBuyBar  <= inpConfirm - 1
bool armedSell = not na(armSellBar) and bar_index - armSellBar <= inpConfirm - 1

// ── CONFIRM · the value ladder, this chart's effort, the conviction ladder ────
bool tBuy  = mVLoW <= -osc_entry and mV <  osc_entry and hist > 0.0 and (mC > 0.0 or mCUp) and (not inpEff or absSeen)
bool tSell = mVHiW >=  osc_entry and mV > -osc_entry and hist < 0.0 and (mC < 0.0 or mCDn) and (not inpEff or absSeen)

bool buySig  = inpState and confirmed and stackOk and svTradable and armedBuy  and tBuy  and coolLong
bool sellSig = inpState and confirmed and stackOk and svTradable and armedSell and tSell and coolShort
if buySig
    armBuyBar  := na
if sellSig
    armSellBar := na

// ── ◆ · the push resumes inside conviction, with value room and real result ───
bool tConL = mC >=  inpZ1 and mV <  osc_entry and (not inpEff or not effAbs)
bool tConS = mC <= -inpZ1 and mV > -osc_entry and (not inpEff or not effAbs)

bool conLong  = inpCon and confirmed and stackOk and not buySig  and
     cvOsc > 0.0 and pulledUp and impUp and coolLong and tConL
bool conShort = inpCon and confirmed and stackOk and not sellSig and
     cvOsc < 0.0 and pulledDn and impDn and coolShort and tConS

if buySig or conLong
    lastLongBar := bar_index
if sellSig or conShort
    lastShortBar := bar_index

// ── the standing declaration ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
// +1 after a ▲, −1 after a ▼, standing until the opposite one: a declaration
// about the market at that bar, deliberately without an exit.
var int decl    = 0
var int declBar = na
if buySig
    decl    := 1
    declBar := bar_index
if sellSig
    decl    := -1
    declBar := bar_index

color oscCol = not ready ? C_NEUT : osc >= 0 ? C_BULL : C_BEAR

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  VOLUME PROFILE · MARKET-STRUCTURE OVERLAY   (ported from PVOI v2.1)
//  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  A PRICE-CHART overlay: session volume profile (histogram + POC/VAH/VAL value
//  area), plus session VWAP, Initial Balance, naked POCs, HVN/LVN nodes,
//  prior-session levels and developing-level trails. Pragati is an oscillator
//  pane, so every element is drawn with force_overlay = true.
//
//  DISPLAY-ONLY AND FULLY DETACHED. It touches NOTHING — not the oscillator,
//  the divergence engine, the triggers, or the signals. Everything is
//  namespaced vp*/C_* so it cannot collide with the oscillator's own state.
//  Removing this whole block leaves the oscillator intact.
//
//  Sorted-key bins, O(1) POC, value-area walk outward from the POC, optional
//  intrabar distribution and inferred buy/sell delta. Session anchor = D intraday,
//  W on daily, M above. request.security_lower_tf history depth is plan-dependent;
//  the profile falls back to a uniform bar-range spread when it is unavailable.
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
var string G_VP = "9 · Volume profile · price chart"
bool   inpVpMaster    = input.bool(true,  "▸ Volume profile overlay (master)", group=G_VP, tooltip="ON by default. Off removes every chart-overlay element below AND stops the profile being computed — the accumulation loop, the row inserts, the value-area walk and the naked-POC ledger all stand down, so this is a real off switch rather than a curtain over work that keeps running.\n\nThe oscillator pane is unaffected either way; nothing in the engine reads the profile.\n\nONE COST CANNOT BE SWITCHED OFF: Pine requires request.security_lower_tf() at global scope, so the lower-timeframe fetch happens whether or not the profile uses it. Nothing else does.", display=display.none)
bool   inpVpProfile   = input.bool(true,  "Enable profile compute (POC/VAH/VAL)", group=G_VP, tooltip="Builds the session volume profile. Off: histogram, value area, nodes, naked POCs and prior-session levels go dark (VWAP and Initial Balance are independent of it).", display=display.none)
bool   inpVpUseVol    = input.bool(true,  "Volume-weight the profile", group=G_VP, tooltip="Off degrades the profile to a time-at-price (TPO) distribution — for spot FX / synthetic volume.", display=display.none)
float  inpVpBinsPerAtr= input.float(4.0,  "Profile bins per ATR", minval=1.0, maxval=20.0, step=0.5, group=G_VP, tooltip="Bin width = ATR / this. Frozen at session start so keys stay consistent within a profile.", display=display.none)
string inpVpBinMode   = input.string("ATR adaptive", "Profile bin grid", options=["ATR adaptive", "Fixed ticks"], group=G_VP, display=display.none)
int    inpVpBinTicks  = input.int(20,     "    Bin size (ticks) · Fixed mode", minval=1, maxval=5000, group=G_VP, display=display.none)
float  inpVpVaPct     = input.float(0.70, "Value area fraction", minval=0.50, maxval=0.95, step=0.01, group=G_VP, display=display.none)
bool   inpVpIntrabar  = input.bool(true,  "Build profile from intrabar data", group=G_VP, tooltip="request.security_lower_tf() distributes volume by ACTUAL traded price. More accurate, slower, plan-dependent history. Falls back automatically.", display=display.none)
bool   inpVpDelta     = input.bool(true,  "Split profile into inferred buy/sell", group=G_VP, tooltip="Inferred, not measured order flow — display only.", display=display.none)
bool   inpVpHistg     = input.bool(true,  "    Histogram", group=G_VP, display=display.none)
string inpVpStyle     = input.string("Monochrome", "    Histogram style", options=["Monochrome", "Delta split"], group=G_VP, tooltip="MONOCHROME (default): one box per row, amber POC row inside blue value-area rows, grey out-of-value tails. Hue marks identity only, never direction.\n\nDELTA SPLIT: each row divides into green (inferred buy) and red (inferred sell) volume; the POC row is an amber outline.", display=display.none)
bool   vpMono         = inpVpStyle == "Monochrome"
int    inpVpWidth     = input.int(30,     "    Profile width (bars)", minval=8, maxval=150, group=G_VP, display=display.none)
bool   inpVpNodes     = input.bool(true,  "    HVN / LVN markers", group=G_VP, display=display.none)
bool   inpVpNaked     = input.bool(true,  "    Naked POC (untouched)", group=G_VP, display=display.none)
int    inpVpNakedMax  = input.int(5,      "        Naked POC · max kept", minval=1, maxval=20, group=G_VP, tooltip="How many untouched POCs the ledger carries. The oldest is dropped when the ledger is full.\n\nThe original ceiling was twelve, and on a long chart twelve amber dotted lines stop being a reference and become the chart. A level you cannot count is not a level. Five is enough to see the unfinished business above and below without burying the price action; raise it only if you are actually trading to the older ones.", display=display.none)
int    inpVpNakedAge  = input.int(250,    "        Naked POC · max age (bars)", minval=50, maxval=1000, step=25, group=G_VP, tooltip="A naked POC older than this is dropped whether or not price ever traded through it, and no line starts further back than this. A point of control from four hundred bars ago describes a market that no longer exists.", display=display.none)
bool   inpVpPrev      = input.bool(true,  "    Prior session value", group=G_VP, tooltip="Last session's pPOC, pVAH and pVAL — three dotted lines, drawn only across the session in front of you. These are the reference the market is currently accepting or rejecting, which is why they are not stacked back through history.", display=display.none)
bool   inpVpVwap      = input.bool(true,  "    Session VWAP", group=G_VP, display=display.none)
bool   inpVpIb        = input.bool(true,  "    Initial Balance (IBH/IBL)", group=G_VP, display=display.none)
int    inpVpIbBars    = input.int(12,     "    Initial Balance span (bars)", minval=3, maxval=60, group=G_VP, display=display.none)
bool   inpVpVa        = input.bool(true,  "    Value area (VAH/VAL)", group=G_VP, display=display.none)
bool   inpVpPoc       = input.bool(true,  "    POC line", group=G_VP, display=display.none)
bool   inpVpTrail     = input.bool(true,  "    Developing level trails", group=G_VP, display=display.none)

// ── gates, resolved once ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
bool   coreView   = inpView == "Core"
bool   vpChartOn  = inpVpMaster and not coreView
// THE MASTER NOW GATES COMPUTE AS WELL AS DRAWING. Before this it only silenced
// the chart objects while the accumulation loop, the row inserts and the value-
// area walk all still ran on every bar. A switch that turns the picture off and
// leaves the work on is not an off switch.
bool   vpCtxOn    = inpVpProfile and vpChartOn
bool   vpIsIntra  = timeframe.isintraday
int    vpTfSec    = timeframe.in_seconds()
// AUTO-TPO FALLBACK. On a symbol reporting no volume every row received zero, so
// pocVol never exceeded zero and the `pocVol > 0` guard suppressed the histogram,
// the POC and the value area entirely - the profile simply did not appear, with
// nothing to explain why, and the panel still read "Volume" because it reported the
// user's INTENT rather than what happened. Index spot and most TVC symbols are
// exactly that case. The share of bars carrying real volume decides it now.
float  vpVolShare = ta.cum((na(volume) or volume == 0.0) ? 0.0 : 1.0) / (bar_index + 1)
bool   vpVolUsable= vpVolShare >= 0.5
bool   vpVolOff   = not inpVpUseVol or not vpVolUsable
bool   vpVolOk    = not vpVolOff          // what ACTUALLY happened, not what was asked
bool   vpTpoAuto  = inpVpUseVol and not vpVolUsable
string vpAnchorTf = vpIsIntra ? "D" : timeframe.isdaily ? "W" : "M"
// The first bar opens a session too. Without it the first partial session had no
// frozen bin width and no session start, so it drew no profile and its Initial
// Balance stretched across the whole of it. The flip is evaluated on its own line
// first so ta.change runs on every bar under v6's lazy `or`.
bool   vpSessFlip = ta.change(time(vpAnchorTf)) != 0
bool   vpNewSess  = barstate.isfirst or vpSessFlip
bool   vpHistOn   = vpChartOn and inpVpHistg and vpCtxOn
bool   vpVaOn     = vpChartOn and inpVpVa    and vpCtxOn
bool   vpPocOn    = vpChartOn and inpVpPoc   and vpCtxOn
bool   vpNodesOn  = vpHistOn  and inpVpNodes
bool   vpNakedOn  = vpChartOn and inpVpNaked and vpCtxOn
bool   vpPrevOn   = vpChartOn and inpVpPrev  and vpCtxOn
bool   vpTrailOn  = vpChartOn and inpVpTrail and vpCtxOn
bool   vpVwapOn   = vpChartOn and inpVpVwap
bool   vpIbOn     = vpChartOn and inpVpIb

// ATR floor. Floored to mintick so the session-start bin freeze never freezes to
// na and blanks the first session. ta.* must be evaluated on EVERY bar, never
// inside the session branch that consumes it.
float vpATR = math.max(nz(ta.atr(20), syminfo.mintick), syminfo.mintick)

// ── helpers ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
f_vpLocate(array<int> keys, int k) =>
    int  lo    = 0
    int  hi    = array.size(keys) - 1
    int  pos   = 0
    bool found = false
    while lo <= hi
        int mid = (lo + hi) / 2
        int mk  = array.get(keys, mid)
        if mk == k
            pos   := mid
            found := true
            break
        else if mk < k
            lo := mid + 1
        else
            hi := mid - 1
    if not found
        pos := lo
    [pos, found]

f_vpAdd(array<int> keys, array<float> vols, array<float> buys, array<float> sells, int k, float v, float bv, float sv) =>
    [pos, found] = f_vpLocate(keys, k)
    float nv = v
    if found
        nv := array.get(vols, pos) + v
        array.set(vols,  pos, nv)
        array.set(buys,  pos, array.get(buys,  pos) + bv)
        array.set(sells, pos, array.get(sells, pos) + sv)
    else
        array.insert(keys,  pos, k)
        array.insert(vols,  pos, v)
        array.insert(buys,  pos, bv)
        array.insert(sells, pos, sv)
    nv

// ── profile state ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
var array<int>   vpKey  = array.new<int>()
var array<float> vpVol  = array.new<float>()
var array<float> vpBuy  = array.new<float>()
var array<float> vpSell = array.new<float>()
var float vpBin   = na
var float vpTotal = 0.0
var int   pocKey  = na
var float pocVol  = 0.0
var float poc     = na
var float vah     = na
var float vaLow   = na
var float prevPoc = na
var float prevVah = na
var float prevVal = na
var bool  vpIntraOk = false

// intrabar distribution (falls back automatically when unavailable)
// On a chart at or below one minute there is no lower timeframe worth spreading
// by, and on a SECONDS chart "1" is HIGHER than the chart - a runtime error. The
// request then asks for the chart's own timeframe (harmless) and intrabar
// distribution stands down.
string vpLtf      = vpTfSec >= 3600 ? "5" : vpTfSec > 60 ? "1" : timeframe.period
bool   vpIntraUse = inpVpIntrabar and vpTfSec > 60
[vpIbHi, vpIbLo, vpIbVol, vpIbClose] = request.security_lower_tf(syminfo.tickerid, vpLtf, [high, low, volume, close])

// inferred delta (DISPLAY ONLY) — gap-aware close-location share to buyers
float vpTrHi  = math.max(high, nz(close[1], high))
float vpTrLo  = math.min(low,  nz(close[1], low))
float vpTrRng = math.max(vpTrHi - vpTrLo, syminfo.mintick)
float vpShare = inpVpDelta ? math.min(1.0, math.max(0.0, (close - vpTrLo) / vpTrRng)) : 0.5
float vpBarV  = vpVolOff ? 1.0 : math.max(nz(volume), 0.0)
float vpBarB  = vpBarV * vpShare
float vpBarS  = vpBarV - vpBarB

if vpCtxOn
    if vpNewSess
        prevPoc := poc
        prevVah := vah
        prevVal := vaLow
        array.clear(vpKey)
        array.clear(vpVol)
        array.clear(vpBuy)
        array.clear(vpSell)
        vpTotal := 0.0
        pocKey  := na
        pocVol  := 0.0
        vpBin   := inpVpBinMode == "Fixed ticks" ? math.max(syminfo.mintick, inpVpBinTicks * syminfo.mintick) : math.max(syminfo.mintick, vpATR / inpVpBinsPerAtr)

    if not na(vpBin) and vpBin > 0
        bool usedIntrabar = false
        if vpIntraUse and not na(vpIbHi) and not na(vpIbLo) and not na(vpIbVol) and not na(vpIbClose)
            int m = array.size(vpIbHi)
            if m > 0 and m == array.size(vpIbLo) and m == array.size(vpIbVol) and m == array.size(vpIbClose)
                usedIntrabar := true
                float prevC = nz(close[1], na)
                for j = 0 to m - 1
                    float ih = array.get(vpIbHi, j)
                    float il = array.get(vpIbLo,  j)
                    // TPO: each CHART bar is one unit of time, split across its
                    // intrabars - otherwise an intrabar-built bar counted m times
                    // what a fallback bar counted in the same session.
                    float iv = vpVolOff ? 1.0 / m : math.max(nz(array.get(vpIbVol, j)), 0.0)
                    float ic = array.get(vpIbClose, j)
                    float bvn = iv * 0.5
                    float svn = iv * 0.5
                    if inpVpDelta and not na(ic) and not na(prevC)
                        if ic > prevC
                            bvn := iv
                            svn := 0.0
                        else if ic < prevC
                            bvn := 0.0
                            svn := iv
                    if not na(ic)
                        prevC := ic
                    if not na(ih) and not na(il)
                        int kl = int(math.floor(il / vpBin))
                        int kh = int(math.floor(ih / vpBin))
                        int nb = math.max(1, math.min(kh - kl + 1, 200))
                        float per  = iv  / nb
                        float perB = bvn / nb
                        float perS = svn / nb
                        for k = kl to kl + nb - 1
                            float tot = f_vpAdd(vpKey, vpVol, vpBuy, vpSell, k, per, perB, perS)
                            vpTotal += per
                            if tot > pocVol
                                pocVol := tot
                                pocKey := k
        if not usedIntrabar
            int kl = int(math.floor(low  / vpBin))
            int kh = int(math.floor(high / vpBin))
            int nb = math.max(1, math.min(kh - kl + 1, 200))
            float per  = vpBarV / nb
            float perB = vpBarB / nb
            float perS = vpBarS / nb
            for k = kl to kl + nb - 1
                float tot = f_vpAdd(vpKey, vpVol, vpBuy, vpSell, k, per, perB, perS)
                vpTotal += per
                if tot > pocVol
                    pocVol := tot
                    pocKey := k

        vpIntraOk := usedIntrabar

    int nLev = array.size(vpKey)
    if nLev > 0 and not na(pocKey) and not na(vpBin)
        [pi, pfound] = f_vpLocate(vpKey, pocKey)
        if pfound
            float target = vpTotal * inpVpVaPct
            int   lo    = pi
            int   hi    = pi
            float acc   = array.get(vpVol, pi)
            int   guard = 0
            while acc < target and (lo > 0 or hi < nLev - 1) and guard < nLev + 2
                guard += 1
                float vd = lo > 0        ? array.get(vpVol, lo - 1) : -1.0
                float vu = hi < nLev - 1 ? array.get(vpVol, hi + 1) : -1.0
                if vu >= vd
                    hi  += 1
                    acc += vu
                else
                    lo  -= 1
                    acc += vd
            poc   := (pocKey + 0.5) * vpBin
            vah   := (array.get(vpKey, hi) + 1.0) * vpBin
            vaLow := array.get(vpKey, lo) * vpBin

// ── naked POC ledger (measured) ──────────────────────────────────────────────
//  Capped by input rather than at a hard twelve: twelve amber dotted lines on a
//  long chart stop being a reference and become the chart.
var array<float> vpNpY = array.new<float>()
var array<int>   vpNpX = array.new<int>()
if barstate.isconfirmed and array.size(vpNpY) > 0
    int kn = array.size(vpNpY) - 1
    while kn >= 0
        if high >= array.get(vpNpY, kn) and low <= array.get(vpNpY, kn)
            array.remove(vpNpY, kn)
            array.remove(vpNpX, kn)
        kn -= 1
if vpCtxOn and vpNewSess and not na(prevPoc)
    bool dupN = false
    if array.size(vpNpY) > 0
        for qn = 0 to array.size(vpNpY) - 1
            if math.abs(array.get(vpNpY, qn) - prevPoc) <= vpATR * 0.1
                dupN := true
                break
    if not dupN
        array.push(vpNpY, prevPoc)
        array.push(vpNpX, bar_index)
        while array.size(vpNpY) > inpVpNakedMax
            array.shift(vpNpY)
            array.shift(vpNpX)
while array.size(vpNpX) > 0
    if bar_index - array.get(vpNpX, 0) > inpVpNakedAge
        array.shift(vpNpY)
        array.shift(vpNpX)
    else
        break

// ── session VWAP + Initial Balance (measured) ─────────────────────────────────
var float vpSessPv = 0.0
var float vpSessVv = 0.0
var float vpIbh    = na
var float vpIbl    = na
var int   vpSessStart = na
if vpChartOn
    if vpNewSess
        vpSessPv    := 0.0
        vpSessVv    := 0.0
        vpIbh       := high
        vpIbl       := low
        vpSessStart := bar_index
    float wV = vpVolOff ? 1.0 : math.max(nz(volume), 1e-10)
    vpSessPv += hlc3 * wV
    vpSessVv += wV
    if bar_index - nz(vpSessStart, bar_index) < inpVpIbBars       // bars 0 … n-1 = n bars
        vpIbh := math.max(nz(vpIbh, high), high)
        vpIbl := math.min(nz(vpIbl, low), low)
float vpVwap = vpSessVv > 0 ? vpSessPv / vpSessVv : na

// ── right-edge tag column + collision control ─────────────────────────────────
int VP_GUTTER = 4
int VP_XBACK  = 5000          // furthest-back x any profile drawing may take
int VP_OFFSET = 3             // extra bars of air between the last candle and the profile
int vpLeft    = bar_index + 3 + VP_OFFSET
int vpRight   = vpLeft + inpVpWidth
var int tagX  = 0
var array<float> tagRows = array.new<float>()

f_rowFree(float y) =>
    float minGap = vpATR * 0.30
    bool  free   = true
    if array.size(tagRows) > 0
        for yy in tagRows
            if math.abs(yy - y) < minGap
                free := false
                break
    free

f_vpTag(label lb, float y, string txt, color col, bool on) =>
    label.delete(lb)
    bool draw = on and not na(y) and f_rowFree(y)
    if draw
        array.push(tagRows, y)
    draw ? label.new(tagX, y, txt + "  " + str.tostring(y, format.mintick),
              xloc.bar_index, yloc.price, color.new(col, 88), label.style_label_left,
              col, size.tiny, force_overlay = true) : label(na)

// ── developing-level trails + VWAP curve (force_overlay) ──────────────────────
// Blank on the session-open bar so yesterday's finals never connect to today.
// force_overlay relocates these to the MAIN chart pane, so they render as price-
// level curves and never touch the oscillator's ±100 autoscale.
plot(vpTrailOn and vpVaOn  and not vpNewSess ? vah   : na, "VP · VAH trail", color.new(C_VA, 75), 1, plot.style_linebr, force_overlay = true, display = display.pane + display.price_scale)
plot(vpTrailOn and vpVaOn  and not vpNewSess ? vaLow : na, "VP · VAL trail", color.new(C_VA, 75), 1, plot.style_linebr, force_overlay = true, display = display.pane + display.price_scale)
plot(vpTrailOn and vpPocOn and not vpNewSess ? poc   : na, "VP · POC trail", color.new(C_POC, 65), 1, plot.style_linebr, force_overlay = true, display = display.pane + display.price_scale)
plot(vpVwapOn ? vpVwap : na, "VP · Session VWAP", color.new(C_VWAP, 0), 1, plot.style_linebr, force_overlay = true, display = display.pane + display.price_scale)

// ── histogram (render aggregation into ≤110 rows) ─────────────────────────────
var array<box> vpBoxes = array.new<box>()
if barstate.islast
    for b in vpBoxes
        box.delete(b)
    array.clear(vpBoxes)
    tagX := (vpHistOn ? vpRight : bar_index) + VP_GUTTER
    if vpHistOn and not na(vpBin) and vpBin > 0 and pocVol > 0
        int maxRight = vpLeft
        int nB = array.size(vpKey)
        if nB > 0
            int ROWS_MAX = 110
            int step = math.max(1, int(math.ceil(nB / float(ROWS_MAX))))
            int nR   = int(math.ceil(nB / float(step)))
            array<float> rV  = array.new<float>(nR, 0.0)
            array<float> rB  = array.new<float>(nR, 0.0)
            array<float> rS  = array.new<float>(nR, 0.0)
            array<float> rLo = array.new<float>(nR, 0.0)
            array<float> rHi = array.new<float>(nR, 0.0)
            array<bool>  rP  = array.new<bool>(nR, false)
            [pIdx, pFound] = f_vpLocate(vpKey, pocKey)
            for i = 0 to nB - 1
                int rr = int(math.floor(i / float(step)))
                array.set(rV, rr, array.get(rV, rr) + array.get(vpVol, i))
                array.set(rB, rr, array.get(rB, rr) + array.get(vpBuy, i))
                array.set(rS, rr, array.get(rS, rr) + array.get(vpSell, i))
                if i % step == 0
                    array.set(rLo, rr, array.get(vpKey, i) * vpBin)
                if i % step == step - 1 or i == nB - 1
                    array.set(rHi, rr, (array.get(vpKey, i) + 1.0) * vpBin)
                if pFound and i == pIdx
                    array.set(rP, rr, true)
            // Row width is normalised by the WIDEST RENDERED ROW, not by the raw
            // POC bin. Above ROWS_MAX bins the rows are aggregated in groups of
            // `step`, so an aggregated row holds several bins' worth and can
            // exceed pocVol — which made the histogram overshoot its declared
            // width by up to `step` times and drag the tag column off the screen
            // with it. Any session finer than 110 bins hit this.
            float rMax   = 0.0
            float hvMean = 0.0
            int   hvCnt  = 0
            for r0 = 0 to nR - 1
                float tv0 = array.get(rV, r0)
                rMax := math.max(rMax, tv0)
                if tv0 > 0
                    hvMean += tv0
                    hvCnt  += 1
            hvMean := hvCnt > 0 ? hvMean / hvCnt : 0.0
            float hvVar = 0.0
            for r0 = 0 to nR - 1
                float tv0 = array.get(rV, r0)
                if tv0 > 0
                    hvVar += math.pow(tv0 - hvMean, 2)
            float hvSd  = hvCnt > 0 ? math.sqrt(hvVar / hvCnt) : 0.0
            float hvnTh = hvMean + 1.2 * hvSd
            float lvnTh = hvMean - 0.6 * hvSd
            array<float> srtV = array.new<float>()
            for r0 = 0 to nR - 1
                if array.get(rV, r0) > 0
                    array.push(srtV, array.get(rV, r0))
            if array.size(srtV) > 10
                array.sort(srtV, order.ascending)
                lvnTh := array.get(srtV, math.max(0, int(math.floor(array.size(srtV) * 0.10))))
            for r = 0 to nR - 1
                if array.size(vpBoxes) >= 470
                    break
                float tV = array.get(rV, r)
                int wTot = tV > 0 and rMax > 0 ? int(math.round(inpVpWidth * tV / rMax)) : 0
                if wTot > 0
                    float yB    = array.get(rLo, r)
                    float ySpan = array.get(rHi, r) - yB
                    float yT    = yB + ySpan * 0.85
                    float pc    = yB + ySpan / 2.0
                    bool  isPoc = array.get(rP, r)
                    maxRight := math.max(maxRight, vpLeft + wTot)
                    bool inVa = not na(vah) and not na(vaLow) and pc >= vaLow and pc <= vah
                    int  fade = inVa ? 48 : 72
                    if vpMono
                        // MONOCHROME: one box per row, hue = identity never
                        // direction — amber POC row, blue value-area rows, grey
                        // out-of-value tails. POC row is a denser amber FILL.
                        color rowCol = isPoc ? C_POC : inVa ? C_VA : C_NODE
                        int   rowFd  = isPoc ? 25 : fade
                        array.push(vpBoxes, box.new(vpLeft, yT, vpLeft + wTot, yB, xloc = xloc.bar_index, border_color = na, bgcolor = color.new(rowCol, rowFd), force_overlay = true))
                    else
                        // DELTA SPLIT: green inferred-buy / red inferred-sell, with
                        // the POC row marked by an amber OUTLINE so the split keeps
                        // its colour.
                        float bV = array.get(rB, r)
                        int   wB = tV > 0 ? int(math.round(wTot * bV / tV)) : 0
                        if wB > 0
                            array.push(vpBoxes, box.new(vpLeft, yT, vpLeft + wB, yB, xloc = xloc.bar_index, border_color = na, bgcolor = color.new(C_BULL, fade), force_overlay = true))
                        if wTot - wB > 0
                            array.push(vpBoxes, box.new(vpLeft + wB, yT, vpLeft + wTot, yB, xloc = xloc.bar_index, border_color = na, bgcolor = color.new(C_BEAR, fade), force_overlay = true))
                        if isPoc
                            array.push(vpBoxes, box.new(vpLeft, yT, vpLeft + wTot, yB, xloc = xloc.bar_index, border_color = color.new(C_POC, 0), border_width = 1, bgcolor = na, force_overlay = true))
                    if vpNodesOn and r > 0 and r < nR - 1 and not isPoc and tV > 0
                        float vL = array.get(rV, r - 1)
                        float vR = array.get(rV, r + 1)
                        bool  peak   = tV > vL and tV > vR
                        bool  trough = tV < vL and tV < vR
                        // Node marks sit just LEFT of the histogram (vpLeft-2..vpLeft-1),
                        // so they travel with the profile's offset and keep a one-bar gap.
                        // HVN dense violet / LVN pale violet — disjoint from BOTH the
                        // monochrome grey tails and the delta-split green/red, so the
                        // colour reads in either style. Form still separates them:
                        // full-height block = node, centred tick = vacuum.
                        if tV >= hvnTh and peak
                            array.push(vpBoxes, box.new(vpLeft - 2, yT, vpLeft - 1, yB, xloc = xloc.bar_index, border_color = na, bgcolor = color.new(C_HVN, 40), force_overlay = true))
                        else if tV <= lvnTh and trough
                            array.push(vpBoxes, box.new(vpLeft - 2, pc - vpBin * 0.08, vpLeft - 1, pc + vpBin * 0.08, xloc = xloc.bar_index, border_color = na, bgcolor = color.new(C_LVN, 40), force_overlay = true))
            if maxRight > vpLeft
                tagX := maxRight + VP_GUTTER

// ── right-edge tags for the live levels ───────────────────────────────────────
var label vpLPoc  = na
var label vpLVah  = na
var label vpLVal  = na
var label vpLVwap = na
var label vpLIbh  = na
var label vpLIbl  = na
if barstate.islast
    array.clear(tagRows)
    vpLPoc  := f_vpTag(vpLPoc,  poc,    "POC",  C_POC,    vpPocOn)
    vpLVah  := f_vpTag(vpLVah,  vah,    "VAH",  C_VA,     vpVaOn)
    vpLVal  := f_vpTag(vpLVal,  vaLow,  "VAL",  C_VA,     vpVaOn)
    vpLVwap := f_vpTag(vpLVwap, vpVwap, "VWAP", C_VWAP,   vpVwapOn)
    vpLIbh  := f_vpTag(vpLIbh,  vpIbh,  "IBH",  C_VIOLET, vpIbOn)
    vpLIbl  := f_vpTag(vpLIbl,  vpIbl,  "IBL",  C_VIOLET, vpIbOn)

// ── managed level lines · live tier, IB, prior session, naked POCs ────────────
var line  vpLnPoc  = na
var line  vpLnVah  = na
var line  vpLnVal  = na
var box   vpVaBand = na
var line  vpLnIbh  = na
var line  vpLnIbl  = na
var line  vpPlnPoc = na
var line  vpPlnVah = na
var line  vpPlnVal = na
var label vpLPp    = na
var label vpLPvh   = na
var label vpLPvl   = na
var array<line> vpNpLines = array.new<line>()
if barstate.islast
    line.delete(vpLnPoc)
    line.delete(vpLnVah)
    line.delete(vpLnVal)
    box.delete(vpVaBand)
    line.delete(vpLnIbh)
    line.delete(vpLnIbl)
    line.delete(vpPlnPoc)
    line.delete(vpPlnVah)
    line.delete(vpPlnVal)
    label.delete(vpLPp)
    label.delete(vpLPvh)
    label.delete(vpLPvl)
    for lnN in vpNpLines
        line.delete(lnN)
    array.clear(vpNpLines)
    // Session-anchored. Pine refuses drawings placed more than 10,000 bars into
    // history; every x in this block is bounded by VP_XBACK well inside that. The
    // old bound of 400 cited max_bars_back, which does not govern drawings, and
    // cut session lines short on 24-hour 1-minute charts (1,440 bars a session).
    int xSess = math.max(math.max(not na(vpSessStart) ? vpSessStart : 0, bar_index - VP_XBACK), 0)
    if vpPocOn and not na(poc)
        vpLnPoc := line.new(xSess, poc, tagX, poc, color = color.new(C_POC, 0), width = 2, force_overlay = true)
    if vpVaOn and not na(vah) and not na(vaLow)
        vpVaBand := box.new(xSess, vah, bar_index, vaLow, border_color = na, bgcolor = color.new(C_VA, 93), force_overlay = true)
        vpLnVah  := line.new(xSess, vah,   tagX, vah,   color = color.new(C_VA, 20), width = 1, force_overlay = true)
        vpLnVal  := line.new(xSess, vaLow, tagX, vaLow, color = color.new(C_VA, 20), width = 1, force_overlay = true)
    if vpIbOn and not na(vpIbh)
        vpLnIbh := line.new(xSess, vpIbh, tagX, vpIbh, color = color.new(C_VIOLET, 20), width = 1, style = line.style_dashed, force_overlay = true)
        vpLnIbl := line.new(xSess, vpIbl, tagX, vpIbl, color = color.new(C_VIOLET, 20), width = 1, style = line.style_dashed, force_overlay = true)
    if vpPrevOn and not na(prevPoc)
        int xA = math.max(0, bar_index - 30)
        vpPlnPoc := line.new(xA, prevPoc, tagX, prevPoc, color = color.new(C_POC, 55), width = 1, style = line.style_dotted, force_overlay = true)
        vpPlnVah := line.new(xA, prevVah, tagX, prevVah, color = color.new(C_VA, 55),  width = 1, style = line.style_dotted, force_overlay = true)
        vpPlnVal := line.new(xA, prevVal, tagX, prevVal, color = color.new(C_VA, 55),  width = 1, style = line.style_dotted, force_overlay = true)
        vpLPp  := f_vpTag(vpLPp,  prevPoc, "pPOC", C_POC, vpPrevOn)
        vpLPvh := f_vpTag(vpLPvh, prevVah, "pVAH", C_VA,  vpPrevOn)
        vpLPvl := f_vpTag(vpLPvl, prevVal, "pVAL", C_VA,  vpPrevOn)
    if vpNakedOn and array.size(vpNpY) > 0
        int xMinN = math.max(0, bar_index - math.min(inpVpNakedAge, VP_XBACK))
        int nN    = array.size(vpNpY)
        for i = 0 to nN - 1
            float ny = array.get(vpNpY, i)
            int   x1 = math.max(xMinN, array.get(vpNpX, i))
            array.push(vpNpLines, line.new(x1, ny, bar_index, ny, color = color.new(C_POC, 30), width = 1, style = line.style_dotted, force_overlay = true))

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  9 · PRESENTATION STATE        display-only · nothing below reaches the engine
//
//  Every visual in the pane answers ONE question, on ONE channel:
//
//      column hue         which way is the push          side of osc − signal
//      column lightness   pressing or fading             Δhist, Δosc, |hist| vs thr
//      column saturation  is the reading real            rawSd against its history
//      gold cast          is the move paid for by exits  open interest · opt-in
//      pane background    the standing declaration       the last ▲ or ▼ (opt-in)
//      trace intensity    how far has the trace run      |osc| against its rails
//
//  None of this is new information. Each is a panel row, or an engine state the
//  panel already describes, moved onto the chart so it can be read at a glance.
//
//  HUE IS NOT CONTROL. Control is conviction's side of zero. A column is the gap
//  between the trace and its own signal line, so its side says which way the
//  trace is being pushed - on the Conviction trace a green column under a trace
//  at -45 is sellers in control, easing. Every marker drawn while the regime is quiet is drawn faded, so a
//  trigger that fires on an amplified reading says so on its own glyph.
//
//  LIGHTNESS · THE TIERS
//      PRESSING      column growing away from zero
//                      below ±thr   moderate     not yet an impulse
//                      past  ±thr   near-solid   qualifies as an impulse
//      DECELERATING  column shrinking, trace still moving with it
//      TURNING       column shrinking, trace flat or against it
//
//  THE KNEE SITS AT THE TRIGGER'S OWN THRESHOLD, k·σ(hist). A column only goes
//  bright where the impulse gate would accept it, so the brightest thing in the pane is the thing the state machines are waiting for.
//
//  WHY THE SLOPE ONLY SPLITS SHRINKING COLUMNS. With sig = EMA(osc), α = 2/(n+1):
//
//      Δhist = (1−α)·Δosc − α·hist[1]
//
//  Above zero, Δhist > 0 forces Δosc > 0: a growing column always has the trace
//  rising under it. A shrinking column is the ambiguous case - the trace may be
//  rising slower than the signal catches up (deceleration), or it may have turned
//  (rollover). The slope separates them. A trace move inside 0.1σ of its own
//  bar-to-bar change counts as flat, so a wobbling trace reads as turning rather
//  than flickering between tiers.
//
//  SATURATION · THE QUIET REGIME. Adaptive scaling divides by 3σ of the raw
//  share, so when that σ collapses a small imbalance scales into a large reading.
//  When rawSd sits in the bottom fifth of its own history the columns lose about
//  half their colour, so a loud pane in a quiet market LOOKS quiet - the check
//  the panel's raw-share row has always asked you to make by hand.
//
//  BACKGROUND · DECLARATION. Off by default: a faint tint for the standing ▲ or ▼.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
f_mix(color a, color b, float t) =>
    color.rgb(color.r(a) + (color.r(b) - color.r(a)) * t,
              color.g(a) + (color.g(b) - color.g(a)) * t,
              color.b(a) + (color.b(b) - color.b(a)) * t,
              color.t(a))

// trace slope, with a dead band scaled to its own bar-to-bar dispersion
float dOsc    = osc - nz(osc[1], osc)
float dOscSd  = ta.stdev(dOsc, inpNorm)                      // evaluated every bar
float slopeDb = 0.1 * nz(dOscSd, 0.0)

// column tiers
float hPrev    = nz(hist[1], 0.0)
float hMag     = math.abs(hist)
bool  hAbove   = hist >= 0.0
bool  hExpand  = hAbove ? hist > hPrev : hist < hPrev       // growing away from zero
bool  trWith   = hAbove ? dOsc > slopeDb : dOsc < -slopeDb   // trace moving with the column
bool  useSlope = inpHLogic == "Expansion + slope"
bool  hImp     = hMag >= thr                                 // past the impulse gate
float hHi      = math.max(2.0 * nz(histSd, 0.0), 1.5 * thr, 1e-9)

// conviction's own tiers - the grid reads these, as Pragyam does, whichever
// trace is drawn. On the Conviction trace they are the tiers above, exactly.
float dCv      = cvOsc - nz(cvOsc[1], cvOsc)
float cvDb     = 0.1 * nz(ta.stdev(dCv, inpNorm), 0.0)
float cvHistSd = ta.stdev(cvHist, inpNorm)
float cvThr    = inpK * nz(cvHistSd, 0.0)
float cvMag    = math.abs(cvHist)
bool  cvAbove  = cvHist >= 0.0
bool  cvExpand = cvAbove ? cvHist > nz(cvHist[1], 0.0) : cvHist < nz(cvHist[1], 0.0)
bool  cvWith   = cvAbove ? dCv > cvDb : dCv < -cvDb
bool  cvImp    = cvMag >= cvThr
float cvHi     = math.max(2.0 * nz(cvHistSd, 0.0), 1.5 * cvThr, 1e-9)

// quiet regime - rawSd in the bottom fifth of its own history. Conviction's,
// so it desaturates the columns only while the trace carries conviction.
int   Q_LEN    = math.min(4 * inpNorm, 4999)
float rawSdPr  = ta.percentrank(rawSd, Q_LEN)                // evaluated every bar
bool  quietReg = inpQuiet and trHasC and cvReady and not na(rawSdPr) and rawSdPr < 20.0

// DECLARATION BACKGROUND - opt-in. Declarations are committed on closed bars, so
// the tint never flashes on a forming bar.
bool  tintLong   = inpStateBg and stackOk and decl ==  1
bool  tintShort  = inpStateBg and stackOk and decl == -1

// markers fade in a quiet regime - the same statement the columns make
f_q(color c, int t) =>
    color.new(c, quietReg ? int(t + (100 - t) * 0.55) : t)

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  INFORMATION PANEL
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
bool tblOn   = inpPanel
bool tblFull = inpTbl == "Detailed"
var table tb = na

f_num(float v, string fmt) => na(v) ? "n/a" : str.tostring(v, fmt)
f_px(float v) => na(v) ? "—" : str.tostring(v, format.mintick)

f_row(t, rw, k, v, col, tip) =>
    table.cell(t, 0, rw, k, text_color = C_SOFT, text_size = size.tiny, text_halign = text.align_left, tooltip = tip)
    table.cell(t, 1, rw, v, text_color = col, text_size = size.tiny, text_halign = text.align_right)

f_sep(t, rw, lbl) =>
    table.cell(t, 0, rw, lbl, text_color = color.new(C_NEUT, 25), text_size = size.tiny, text_halign = text.align_left)
    table.cell(t, 1, rw, "", text_color = C_SOFT, text_size = size.tiny)

// the forming bar has almost no volume yet, so report the last closed weight -
// and the branch that produced THAT weight, not this bar's
float  wShow    = barstate.isconfirmed ? w : nz(w[1], w)
string wSrcShow = barstate.isconfirmed ? wSrc : wSrc[1]

string vpLocTxt = na(poc) ? "no profile" :
     not na(vah)   and close > vah   ? "above value" :
     not na(vaLow) and close < vaLow ? "below value" :
     not na(vpBin) and math.abs(close - poc) < vpBin ? "at POC" : "in value"
// GOLD, not red/green. Above or below value is UNACCEPTED price, and that can
// resolve either way - rejection back into value, or acceptance and a new
// range. Painting above-value red was a mean-reversion claim the profile cannot
// make; the qualifier is caution, and caution is gold.
color vpLocCol = na(poc) ? C_SOFT :
     (not na(vah) and close > vah) or (not na(vaLow) and close < vaLow) ? C_GOLD : C_SOFT

// Built ONCE and written into. The row count depends only on inputs, which are
// constant for the life of the script, so there is nothing to rebuild - and
// deleting and recreating the table on every realtime tick was pure churn.
if tblOn and barstate.islast
    if na(tb)
        tb := table.new(position.middle_right, 2, tblFull ? (vpChartOn ? 20 : 17) : 8, bgcolor = C_PANEL,
             frame_color = C_GRID, frame_width = 1, border_color = color.new(C_NEUT, 88),
             border_width = 1)

    string head = not ready ? "WARMING UP" :
         trConv ? (osc >=  inpZ2 ? "BUYERS · extreme" : osc >=  inpZ1 ? "BUYERS · stretched" : osc > 0.0 ? "buyers in control" :
                   osc <= -inpZ2 ? "SELLERS · extreme" : osc <= -inpZ1 ? "SELLERS · stretched" : "sellers in control") :
         trVal ?  (osc >=  trOut ? "RICH · extreme" : osc >=  trIn ? "RICH · past θ" : osc > 0.0 ? "above fair" :
                   osc <= -trOut ? "CHEAP · extreme" : osc <= -trIn ? "CHEAP · past θ" : "below fair") :
                  (osc >=  trOut ? "STRETCHED UP · extreme" : osc >=  trIn ? "STRETCHED UP" : osc > 0.0 ? "leaning up" :
                   osc <= -trOut ? "STRETCHED DOWN · extreme" : osc <= -trIn ? "STRETCHED DOWN" : "leaning down")
    color hc = not ready ? C_GOLD : osc >= 0 ? C_BULL : C_BEAR

    table.cell(tb, 0, 0, " " + head, text_color = hc, text_size = size.tiny,
         text_halign = text.align_left, bgcolor = color.new(hc, 86),
         tooltip = trConv ? "The participation-weighted share of the window's effort that converted into net displacement, scaled against its own dispersion.\n\nBeyond the inner zone the effort is heavily one-sided; beyond the outer zone it is near-uniform, which is where trends run and also where they end.\n\nA zone reading is NOT a signal. It is the setting a signal has to arrive in." :
              trVal ? "SAMANVAYA'S VALUE ON THIS CHART - where price stands against fair value: its RV leg and breadth blended in σ and bounded on Samanvaya's scale. Past θ a dislocation; past ±60 a 3σ one.\n\nA stretch reading is NOT a signal. Its crossing back through θ is what arms a ▲▼." :
              "CONVICTION × VALUE - how far the move is STRETCHED on this chart: conviction in σ and Samanvaya's value in σ, blended with their measured correlation and bounded once on Samanvaya's scale. Past θ the stretch is one a ▲▼ can come out of as it releases; past ±60 it is 3σ. The 'trace' row gives both ingredients.\n\nA stretch reading is NOT a signal. Its crossing back through θ is what arms a ▲▼.")
    table.cell(tb, 1, 0, f_num(osc, "+#;-#") + " ", text_color = hc,
         text_size = size.tiny, text_halign = text.align_right, bgcolor = color.new(hc, 86))

    f_sep(tb, 1, "SIGNALS · " + str.tostring(inpLen) + "b")
    string armTxt = armedBuy  ? "  · ▲ armed " + str.tostring(bar_index - armBuyBar + 1) + "/" + str.tostring(inpConfirm) :
         armedSell ? "  · ▼ armed " + str.tostring(bar_index - armSellBar + 1) + "/" + str.tostring(inpConfirm) : ""
    f_row(tb, 2, " declaration",
         not stackOk ? "paused · " + stackWhy :
         (decl ==  1 ? "BUY ▲ · " + str.tostring(bar_index - declBar) + "b ago" :
          decl == -1 ? "SELL ▼ · " + str.tostring(bar_index - declBar) + "b ago" : "none yet") + armTxt,
         not stackOk ? C_GOLD : decl == 1 ? C_BULL : decl == -1 ? C_BEAR : C_SOFT,
         "The standing declaration: BUY from a ▲ until the next ▼, SELL from a ▼ until the next ▲, with bars since it was made. A statement about the market at that bar, not a position - it has no exit, and its age is the reminder of how old the statement is.\n\n'▲ armed n/N' means Samanvaya's crossing has armed a ▲ and the other layers have N bars to confirm it.\n\nPAUSED means a layer of the stack is missing and no signal can fire; the reason is named, and the same reason is drawn at the pane's right edge.")
    f_row(tb, 3, " value · chart",
         f_num(unified_osc, "+#;-#") + " vs ±" + f_num(osc_entry, "#") + (svTradable ? "" : " · basket settling"),
         not svTradable ? C_GOLD : math.abs(unified_osc) >= osc_entry ? (unified_osc > 0 ? C_BULL : C_BEAR) : C_SOFT,
         "Samanvaya's own value reading on this chart, against θ. On the Conviction trace its crossing back UP through −θ arms a ▲ and back DOWN through +θ a ▼; on the other traces the trace's own crossing arms them - on the Value trace they are one series. Coloured as the value tape is: GREEN rich, RED cheap, grey inside θ.\n\n'basket settling' means the macro basket rotated recently or the anchor sample is still short, so Samanvaya's trigger is not yet tradable - gold, and no ▲▼ can arm.")
    f_row(tb, 4, " MTF · conv · value",
         (mtfCOk ? f_num(mC, "+#;-#") + (ladFallback ? " ↺" : "") : "—") + " · " +
         (mtfVOk ? (mV > 0 ? "rich " : "cheap ") + f_num(mV, "+#;-#") + (tpFallback ? " ↺" : "") : "—"),
         C_SOFT,
         "The two tapes every signal reads. CONVICTION (" + inpTape + "): with Ladder down the inside of the bars, faster than chart conviction; with Ladder up the frames above, slower. VALUE (" + inpVLad + "): Samanvaya's MTF bias - rich (+) or cheap (−), judged against θ " + f_num(osc_entry, "#") + ".\n\n↺ means that tape fell back to the other ladder direction because the chosen one has no frames on this chart. — means the tape is off, warming, or has no frames at all; every signal needs both.")
    f_row(tb, 5, " MTF momentum",
         (not inpMomC ? "off" : mtfCOk and ladMomReady ? f_num(ladMom, "+#;-#") + " " + ladMomTag : "—") + " · " +
         (not inpMomV ? "off" : mtfVOk ? f_num(tpMom, "+#;-#") + (tpRev ? " reverting" : " widening") : "—"),
         C_SOFT,
         "The two momentum tapes: each ladder's faster view minus its slower, in its tape's own points. Context only - no signal reads either.\n\nCONVICTION (" + inpTape + ", Dhṛti's): Ladder down is the bar's insides minus the bar - TURNING INSIDE when it points against chart conviction, the inside of the bars turning before the chart does; BUILDING INSIDE when with it. Ladder up is the chart minus the frames above - RUNNING AHEAD with the conviction tape, PULLBACK against it.\n\nVALUE (" + inpVLad + ", Samanvaya's): positive toward rich. WIDENING when it agrees with the value tape, REVERTING when it opposes it - the fast end already turning back toward fair value.")
    f_row(tb, 6, " impulse", f_num(hist, "+#.#;-#.#") + " vs ±" + f_num(thr, "#.#"),
         math.abs(hist) >= thr ? (hist > 0 ? C_BULL : C_BEAR) : C_SOFT,
         "The histogram against the magnitude threshold a ◆ must cross. NATIVE values - the display gain is not applied here. Its SIDE is the push layer of the ▲▼ - the trace's histogram: above zero to confirm a ▲, below for a ▼.\n\nThe threshold is " + f_num(inpK, "0.0") + "σ of the histogram's own distribution over " + str.tostring(inpNorm) + " bars.")

    f_row(tb, 7, " trace · " + (trConv ? "conviction" : trVal ? "value" : "conv × value"),
         trConv ? "Nishchaya v3 · zones ±" + f_num(inpZ1, "#") + " · ±" + f_num(inpZ2, "#") :
         trVal ? "Samanvaya · θ ±" + f_num(trIn, "#") + (basketWarm ? "" : " · settling") :
         "C " + f_num(zcS, "+0.0;-0.0") + "σ · V " + f_num(unified_z, "+0.0;-0.0") + "σ · ρ " + f_num(rhoCV, "+0.00;-0.00") + (trSplit ? " · split" : "") + (basketWarm ? "" : " · settling"),
         trDim ? C_GOLD : C_SOFT,
         "WHAT THE PANE DRAWS - the 'Trace' setting.\n\nCONVICTION × VALUE shows its two ingredients: C, conviction in σ (the raw share over its own σ, smoothed), and V, Samanvaya's unified value in σ, with ρ their correlation over 200 bars - what restores the blend's variance. SPLIT, in gold, means they point opposite ways: the trace sits near zero because they cancel - cheap with buyers pushing is accumulation, rich with sellers pushing distribution - and it is drawn dimmed. SETTLING means the value basket rotated recently; a trace carrying value is dimmed while it settles.\n\nCONVICTION is the Nishchaya v3 engine alone, on its own zones. VALUE is Samanvaya's value on this chart alone, against θ.")

    if tblFull
        f_sep(tb, 8, "DETAIL")
        f_row(tb, 9, " raw share · σ",
             f_num(raw, "+#.#;-#.#") + " · " + f_num(rawSd, "#.#") + (quietReg ? " · quiet" : ""),
             quietReg ? C_GOLD : C_SOFT,
             "QUIET means this σ sits in the bottom fifth of its own history: the scaling is stretching small imbalances, and the histogram is desaturated while it holds.\n\nThe untransformed quantity and its standard deviation over the normalization window - what the adaptive scaling is working from.\n\nThe raw share rarely leaves ±15 intraday, which is exactly why fixed zones on it never fire and why the scaling exists. Glance here when the pane looks dramatic and the chart does not: a large scaled reading built on a tiny raw share means the instrument is quiet, not that conviction is enormous.")
        f_row(tb, 10, " this bar · conviction", f_num(100.0 * conv, "+#;-#") + "% of range",
             conv >= 0 ? C_BULL : C_BEAR,
             "How much of this bar's true range became net displacement. +100% is a close at the extreme high, −100% at the extreme low, 0% a bar that travelled and finished where it started.\n\nTrue range, so a gap counts as displacement the market actually made.")
        f_row(tb, 11, " participation",
             inpPart == "Off" ? "off · equal weight" :
             wSrcShow + " · ×" + f_num(wShow, "0.0"),
             wShow >= inpCap - 0.01 ? C_GOLD : C_SOFT,
             "What ACTUALLY weighted the last closed bar's conviction, and its weight. 'no print · flat' means Volume mode met a bar with no volume and fell back to an equal weight of 1.0. The forming bar is excluded here because a bar that is two minutes old has almost no volume yet and would always read near zero.\n\nA figure at the cap means participation was extreme and has been limited - expiry, rebalance or a block print.\n\nSwitch participation Off as a control: if the oscillator barely changes, volume is telling you nothing on this instrument.")
        bool effLow = not na(effPct) and effPct <= 20.0
        f_row(tb, 12, " effort → result",
             na(effShare) ? "—" : f_num(100.0 * effShare, "#") + "% · " + f_num(effPct, "#") + " pct" + (effLow ? " · absorbed" : ""),
             effLow ? C_GOLD : C_SOFT,
             "WYCKOFF'S LAW, READ DIRECTLY. The share of the window's participation that became directional displacement at all: Σ|c|·w / Σw over " + str.tostring(inpLen) + " bars. 100% would mean every bar closed at its extreme; low values mean heavy participation is producing little progress either way.\n\nThe second figure is where that share sits against its own last " + str.tostring(inpNorm) + " bars. In the bottom fifth the row turns gold and reads ABSORBED: effort is being met by opposing orders. That is a condition, like a zone - it can resolve by continuation once the opposition is consumed, or by reversal.\n\nThis is the reading the default Agreement engine cannot see, because a bar that goes nowhere drops out of both sides of its ratio. It is computed under both denominators.\n\nIt is also a SIGNAL LAYER (group 4, switchable): a ▲▼ needs absorption inside the dislocation window - the exhaustion the turn comes out of - and a ◆ needs the signal bar NOT absorbed.")

        f_row(tb, 13, " value · macro share",
             modelBuilt ? f_num(100.0 * hedgeW, "#") + "% hedged · " + (anchorTF == "W" ? "weekly" : "daily") + " clock" :
             "building · " + str.tostring(math.min(anchN, svNeed)) + "/" + str.tostring(svNeed),
             not modelBuilt ? C_GOLD : C_SOFT,
             "How much of the value reading is RELATIVE value. The RV leg hedges the target against its macro basket in proportion to the hedge's own measured out-of-sample skill; this is that proportion (" + hedgeMode + "). Near 0% the RV leg is the target's own detrended price path, and value is then a price-only dislocation reading with no macro information in it.\n\nEach anchor observation is the SUM of the chart-bar returns over one clock period, so this figure means the same on every chart timeframe.")
        f_row(tb, 14, " layer overlap · ρ",
             "C " + f_num(rhoC, "+0.00;-0.00") + " · V " + f_num(rhoV, "+0.00;-0.00"),
             math.abs(nz(rhoC)) >= 0.8 or math.abs(nz(rhoV)) >= 0.8 ? C_GOLD : C_SOFT,
             "How much of each tape the trace already knows: the correlation of the conviction tape (C) and the value tape (V) with this chart's trace over the normalization window.\n\nThe signals require every layer, but a layer that moves with the trace adds timing, not independent evidence. GOLD at |ρ| ≥ 0.8: that tape is mostly restating the chart. Expect C to sit high with Ladder down - its rungs are the inside of the same bars - and V lower, most of all when the macro share above is large. On the Conviction × Value trace each tape's chart rung is one of the trace's own ingredients, so both sit higher by construction.")
        f_row(tb, 15, " rungs live",
             "C " + str.tostring(math.max(zCnt - 1, 0)) + " · V " + str.tostring(math.max(tpCnt - 1, 0)),
             C_SOFT,
             "How many ladder frames feed each tape on this bar, the chart excluded. Lower frames have a capped intrabar history and higher frames need their own warm-up, so older bars run on FEWER rungs than recent ones: a signal on the left of the chart was made by a thinner ladder than one on the right.")
        f_row(tb, 16, " value · driver timing",
             str.tostring(drvLagged) + " read a bar late · ≤" + f_num(drvKeptH, "#.#") + "h kept",
             drvKeptH > 0.0 ? C_GOLD : C_SOFT,
             "The value model's macro drivers, checked against this bar's close. A driver closing more than a third of a bar LATER - TVC:US10Y closes about eleven hours after NSE - would carry information from after Y's close into history, and history would read better than realtime. Those are read at their PREVIOUS close, the value known when Y closed: the first figure counts them.\n\nThe second is the largest lateness KEPT - less than a third of a bar, where keeping the driver's bar retains more genuine overlap than lagging it would. It is the most the last bar's value reading - and so a ▲▼ - can still revise by; GOLD when above zero. Intraday bars are contemporaneous and read 0.")

        if vpChartOn
            f_sep(tb, 17, "PROFILE · " + vpAnchorTf)
            f_row(tb, 18, " Profile Mode",
                 (vpVolOk ? "Volume" : vpTpoAuto ? "TPO · auto" : "TPO") + " · " + (vpIntraOk ? vpLtf : "bar"),
                 vpVolOk and vpIntraOk ? C_BULL : C_GOLD,
                 "WHAT THE PROFILE ACTUALLY DID, not what was requested of it.\n\nLeft: volume-weighted, or time-at-price. TPO · AUTO means volume weighting was ASKED FOR and could not be delivered - fewer than half the bars on this symbol carry real volume, so the profile fell back to a time-at-price distribution rather than filling every row with zero and vanishing. Right: the intrabar resolution actually being distributed, or 'bar' when that data is unavailable and volume is spread across each bar's range.\n\nThis row is deliberately a report and not an echo. A panel that repeats your settings back to you cannot tell you when a setting did not take.")
            f_row(tb, 19, " Location",
                 na(poc) ? "no profile" : vpLocTxt + " · POC " + f_px(poc),
                 vpLocCol,
                 "Where price sits in the developing " + vpAnchorTf + " profile.\n\nABOVE / BELOW VALUE is an unaccepted price — the market has not yet agreed to trade there, which is what makes a divergence at such a level a statement rather than a guess. It is shown in GOLD, not red or green: unaccepted price can resolve by rejection or by acceptance, and the colour does not pretend to know which.\n\nNothing in the oscillator reads this: the profile is display-only in this build. It is the WHERE the pane cannot supply on its own. Value area " + f_px(vaLow) + " – " + f_px(vah) + ", VWAP " + f_px(vpVwap) + ", naked POCs carried " + str.tostring(array.size(vpNpY)) + " of " + str.tostring(inpVpNakedMax) + ".")

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  OPEN INTEREST · INSTITUTIONAL READOUT      (a readout row, display-only)
//  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  READS NOTHING INTO THE ENGINE. The oscillator, the divergence engine, the
//  triggers and the profile are untouched by everything below; this block only
//  fetches the chart symbol's open-interest companion series and reports what it
//  says. ONE DISPLAY COUPLING EXISTS: the pane's opt-in gold cast (oiCast, off by
//  default) reads oiHas, oiRoll, oiTrust and the exit/build counts from here, so
//  deleting this block means deleting oiCast too. No signal changes either way.
//
//  WHY OPEN INTEREST AT ALL. Volume answers HOW MUCH traded. Open interest answers
//  WHAT KIND — whether the trading created open risk or destroyed it. |ΔOI| is
//  correlated with volume, but the SIGN of ΔOI is orthogonal to it, and that sign
//  is the information volume cannot carry:
//
//      price ↑  OI ↑     new longs            genuine — risk was committed
//      price ↑  OI ↓     short covering       exhausting — fuel being consumed
//      price ↓  OI ↑     new shorts           genuine — risk was committed
//      price ↓  OI ↓     long liquidation     exhausting — fuel being consumed
//
//  THE COLOURING FOLLOWS GENUINENESS, NOT DIRECTION, and that is a deliberate
//  departure from how this table is usually painted. A rally on short covering is
//  conventionally drawn green, which reads as a bullish claim when it is close to
//  the opposite one: positions are being closed, closing flow is finite, and the
//  move is running out of the thing propelling it. Builds get the side's own
//  colour; covering and unwinding get gold, because both are READ as self-
//  limiting.
//
//  EVIDENCE GRADE, STATED. The four-quadrant reading is a PRACTITIONER framework.
//  The academic open-interest evidence is real but lives at a different horizon:
//  Hong and Yogo (2012) find open-interest growth predicts commodity, bond and,
//  more weakly, stock returns on monthly aggregates, and note that OI is non-
//  directional by construction; Bessembinder and Seguin (1993) treat it as a
//  proxy for depth and hedging demand. Nothing validates the bar-level quadrant.
//  "Self-limiting" is a hypothesis this table colours by, not a result.
//
//  THREE THINGS THE TABLE WILL NOT DO.
//  · It will not pretend a stale feed is a live one. An end-of-day OI series viewed
//    on a 5-minute chart is a step function whose z-scores are degenerate; when
//    fewer than one bar in five carries a change, the readout says STALE and the
//    character goes blank rather than reporting the same buildup for eighty bars.
//  · It will not read an expiry or a contract roll as conviction. Indian monthly
//    expiries collapse the book toward zero and continuous (1!) series step at the
//    roll. A bar is STRUCTURAL when it moves more than half the book on its own,
//    or when it is both beyond 3σ AND more than a fifth of the book. The σ test is
//    no longer sufficient alone: OI changes are fat-tailed, and a 3σ bar is far
//    more often a heavy genuine build than a roll - discarding every one of them
//    threw away exactly the bars the readout exists to show. Structural bars are
//    also kept OUT of the statistics, so one roll cannot inflate σ for the next
//    fifty bars and hide the builds that follow it.
//  · It will not imply the series is informative when it is not. The test is
//    ρ(|ΔOI|, activity) - whether the SIZE of the book's change tracks volume (or
//    true range where there is no volume). The previous test, ρ(Δprice, ΔOI), was
//    wrong by construction: open interest rises on long AND short buildups, so on
//    a perfectly informative series the signed correlation sits near zero, and
//    the readout was dimming itself exactly where it worked. Below 0.20 the OI
//    row dims and the gold cast stands down.
//
//  THE SIGN OF THE CHARACTER IS THE SIGN OF ΔOI. The build figure is the bar's
//  log change over the RMS of recent accepted changes - zero-centred. The earlier
//  z-score subtracted the mean change, so during a steady monthly build an
//  ordinary small INCREASE scored below zero and printed LONG UNWINDING or SHORT
//  COVERING while the book was growing.
//
//  THE COMPANION TICKER IS BUILT FROM prefix AND ticker, NEVER FROM tickerid. On a
//  chart carrying a symbol modifier — settlement-as-close, an adjustment, an
//  extended session — tickerid is a JSON envelope, and appending "_OI" to it makes
//  a string that is not a symbol in any format. ignore_invalid_symbol suppresses
//  symbols that do not EXIST; it does not suppress symbols that are MALFORMED, and
//  that distinction is a runtime error rather than a silent na.
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
var string G_OI = "10 · Open interest · price chart"
bool   inpOi    = input.bool(true, "Open interest row", group=G_OI, tooltip="The OI row of the readout: what kind of positioning this bar represents (STATE), who is doing it and how full the book is (WHY), how large the change is against its own history (PUSH, in σ) and how long the character has held (SPAN). A feed that cannot be measured says so in STATE.\n\nDRAWN ON THE PRICE CHART, not in this pane. Positioning is a statement about price, and it belongs beside the bars it describes rather than beneath an oscillator that does not read it. It also means the readout survives collapsing the pane.\n\nDisplay-only either way: nothing here reaches the oscillator, the divergence engine or the profile.", display=display.none)
string inpOiPos = input.string("Bottom left", "    Readout position", options=["Bottom left", "Bottom centre", "Bottom right", "Middle left", "Top centre", "Top right"], group=G_OI, tooltip="Where the readout - the OI row and the Conviction-Value Grid row, one table - sits on the price chart.\n\nDefault BOTTOM LEFT. The right-hand side is taken: the volume profile draws its histogram in the gutter past the last bar and hangs its POC/VAH/VAL/VWAP tags in a column beyond that. Bottom left is the one corner nothing else claims — top left belongs to the chart legend.\n\nMove it if you run the profile off.", display=display.none)
bool   inpOiShade = input.bool(false, "    Gold cast on histogram (exits)", group=G_OI, tooltip="OFF BY DEFAULT, and the one place this readout reaches the oscillator pane - as COLOUR only. The engine still reads nothing from open interest.\n\nWhen on, a column is cast toward gold if the push on its side over the last signal-length bars was paid for more by EXITS than by new positions: short covering outnumbering long buildup above zero, long unwinding outnumbering short buildup below. At least a third of that window must be exits.\n\nGold means what it means in the table: closing flow is finite, and the move is READ as consuming the positions that propel it - a practitioner hypothesis, not an established result.\n\nWindowed rather than per-bar because a histogram column describes several bars; a one-bar OI character laid over it would flicker.\n\nOnly applies when the feed is LIVE, not in a structural (expiry/roll) bar, and ρ(|ΔOI|, activity) ≥ 0.20 - the same conditions under which the table itself asks to be believed.", display=display.none)

int    OI_BASE     = 50      // accepted observations in the σ baseline, N > 30
float  OI_ROLL     = 3.0     // σ cut-off - structural only together with OI_ROLL_ABS
float  OI_ROLL_ABS = 0.20    // share of the prior book a >3σ bar must also move
float  OI_STRU     = 0.50    // share of the prior book that is structural on its own
float  OI_ACT      = 0.20    // minimum share of bars that must actually update
float  OI_DEAD     = 0.50    // |z| below this is 'balanced', not a build or an unwind
float  OI_RHO      = 0.20    // minimum ρ(|ΔOI|, activity) for the readout to be believed
int    OI_PCT      = 250     // window for how full the book is, historically
string OI_NULL     = "NONE:NONE_OI"   // well-formed, nonexistent: ignored, fetches nothing

string oiPre  = syminfo.prefix
string oiTck  = syminfo.ticker
bool   oiIdBad = str.contains(oiPre, "{") or str.contains(oiTck, "{") or
                 str.contains(oiPre, "\"") or str.contains(oiTck, "\"")
string oiAlt  = str.replace_all(str.replace_all(oiPre, "_DLY", ""), "_DL", "")

// A malformed ticker used to request the chart's OWN symbol and throw the price
// series away. It now requests a well-formed symbol that does not exist, which
// ignore_invalid_symbol turns into na without fetching anything.
string oiSym1 = oiIdBad ? OI_NULL : (oiPre == "" ? "" : oiPre + ":") + oiTck + "_OI"
string oiSym2 = oiIdBad ? OI_NULL : (oiAlt == "" ? "" : oiAlt + ":") + oiTck + "_OI"
string oiSym3 = oiIdBad ? OI_NULL : oiTck + "_OI"

float oiF1 = request.security(oiSym1, timeframe.period, close, ignore_invalid_symbol = true, lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_off)
float oiF2 = request.security(oiSym2, timeframe.period, close, ignore_invalid_symbol = true, lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_off)
float oiF3 = request.security(oiSym3, timeframe.period, close, ignore_invalid_symbol = true, lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_off)
float oiFD = request.security(oiSym1, "D", close, ignore_invalid_symbol = true, lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_off)

float oiVal = oiIdBad ? na : not na(oiF1) ? oiF1 : not na(oiF2) ? oiF2 : oiF3

// One na bar must not flip the readout between modes mid-history.
var bool oiSeen = false
if not na(oiVal) and oiVal > 0
    oiSeen := true

// Staleness. An EOD-only feed on an intraday chart is a step function, and its
// z-scores are degenerate — the same buildup would be reported for eighty bars.
// A gap in the feed is not a change: na-to-value no longer counts as a move.
float oiMoved = nz(ta.change(oiVal), 0.0) != 0 ? 1.0 : 0.0
float oiAct   = nz(ta.sma(oiMoved, OI_BASE), 0.0)
bool  oiHas   = oiSeen and not na(oiVal) and oiVal > 0 and oiAct >= OI_ACT

// Log change, so a halving and a doubling of the book are equal and opposite.
float oiPrev  = oiVal[1]
bool  oiPair  = not na(oiVal) and oiVal > 0 and not na(oiPrev) and oiPrev > 0
float oiLog   = oiPair ? math.log(oiVal / oiPrev) : 0.0
float oiDlt   = oiPair ? oiVal - oiPrev : na
float oiShare = oiPair ? math.abs(oiDlt) / oiPrev : 0.0

// ZERO-CENTRED SCALE from ACCEPTED observations only. The baseline is the RMS of
// the last OI_BASE non-structural log changes, measured BEFORE this bar joins it,
// so a bar is judged against its past and never against itself. RMS rather than
// stdev keeps zero at zero: the sign of oiZ is the sign of ΔOI.
var array<float> oiSq = array.new<float>()
float oiRms = array.size(oiSq) >= 10 ? math.sqrt(array.avg(oiSq)) : na
float oiZ   = nz(oiRms, 0.0) > 1e-12 ? oiLog / oiRms : 0.0

bool  oiRoll = oiHas and oiPair and
     (oiShare > OI_STRU or (math.abs(oiZ) > OI_ROLL and oiShare > OI_ROLL_ABS))

if oiHas and oiPair and not oiRoll
    array.push(oiSq, oiLog * oiLog)
    if array.size(oiSq) > OI_BASE
        array.shift(oiSq)

// Computed on EVERY bar and masked afterwards. Inside the ternary it only ran
// while the feed was live, so its window had holes wherever the feed flickered.
float oiRankAll = ta.percentrank(oiVal, OI_PCT)
float oiRank    = oiHas ? oiRankAll : na

// CREDIBILITY: does the SIZE of the book's change track activity? Structural
// bars are zeroed so one roll cannot manufacture a correlation.
float oiActv  = vpVolUsable ? nz(volume) : tr
float oiAbsD  = oiPair and not oiRoll ? math.abs(oiDlt) : 0.0
float oiRho   = ta.correlation(oiAbsD, oiActv, OI_BASE)
bool  oiTrust = not na(oiRho) and oiRho >= OI_RHO

float oiDpx  = nz(ta.change(close), 0.0)

string oiFeed = oiIdBad ? "no symbol" :
     oiHas   ? "live" :
     oiSeen  ? "stale" :
     not na(oiFD) ? "daily only" : "none"

string oiChar = not oiHas ? "—" : oiRoll ? "EXPIRY / ROLLOVER" :
     oiZ >  OI_DEAD and oiDpx > 0 ? "LONG BUILDUP"   :
     oiZ >  OI_DEAD and oiDpx < 0 ? "SHORT BUILDUP"  :
     oiZ < -OI_DEAD and oiDpx > 0 ? "SHORT COVERING" :
     oiZ < -OI_DEAD and oiDpx < 0 ? "LONG UNWINDING" : "balanced"

// Genuineness, not direction. Builds take the side's colour; covering and
// unwinding take gold, because closing flow is finite and self-limiting.
color oiCharCol = not oiHas or oiRoll ? C_GOLD :
     oiChar == "LONG BUILDUP"  ? C_BULL :
     oiChar == "SHORT BUILDUP" ? C_BEAR :
     oiChar == "SHORT COVERING" or oiChar == "LONG UNWINDING" ? C_GOLD : C_SOFT

// Windowed over the SIGNAL length - the span a histogram column describes - so the
// optional gold cast in the pane reads what paid for the column, not one bar.
float oiExitUp  = nz(math.sum(oiChar == "SHORT COVERING" ? 1.0 : 0.0, inpSig))
float oiBuildUp = nz(math.sum(oiChar == "LONG BUILDUP"   ? 1.0 : 0.0, inpSig))
float oiExitDn  = nz(math.sum(oiChar == "LONG UNWINDING" ? 1.0 : 0.0, inpSig))
float oiBuildDn = nz(math.sum(oiChar == "SHORT BUILDUP"  ? 1.0 : 0.0, inpSig))

// ── the readout's OI row ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// How long the book has shown this character - committed at each close, so a
// live bar is never counted twice.
var string oiPrevChar = ""
var int    oiSpan     = 0
oiSpan     := oiHas and oiChar == oiPrevChar ? oiSpan + 1 : oiHas ? 1 : 0
oiPrevChar := oiHas ? oiChar : ""

// The character in the readout's words, who is doing it, and a crowded book.
string oiState = not oiHas ? (oiFeed == "stale" ? "Stale feed" : oiFeed == "daily only" ? "Daily only" : oiFeed == "no symbol" ? "No symbol" : "No OI") :
     oiRoll ? "Expiry / roll" : oiChar == "LONG BUILDUP" ? "Long build-up" : oiChar == "SHORT BUILDUP" ? "Short build-up" :
     oiChar == "SHORT COVERING" ? "Short covering" : oiChar == "LONG UNWINDING" ? "Long unwinding" : "Balanced"
string oiWho   = not oiHas ? (oiFeed == "stale" ? "too few updates here" : oiFeed == "daily only" ? "not at this timeframe" : oiFeed == "no symbol" ? "chart modifier" : "no open interest") :
     oiRoll ? "structural" : oiChar == "LONG BUILDUP" ? "new longs" : oiChar == "SHORT BUILDUP" ? "new shorts" :
     oiChar == "SHORT COVERING" ? "shorts exiting" : oiChar == "LONG UNWINDING" ? "longs exiting" : "no net change"
bool   oiCrowd = oiHas and nz(oiRank, 0.0) >= 80

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  CONVICTION-VALUE GRID · PRAGYAM'S STYLE, READ ON THIS CHART   (display-only)
//  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  The portfolio style Pragyam builds from this indicator (cvgrid.py), ported
//  line for line and read for the chart symbol: the CVG row of the readout,
//  under OI. NOTHING here reaches a signal, a tape or the pane.
//
//  FAMILY   the 3 × 3 of the conviction tape (inner zone) and value tape (θ).
//           Rows move only on a push conviction's own histogram CONFIRMS -
//           whichever trace the pane draws - its three channels read as
//           Pragyam reads them: hue on the move's side, tier
//           not TURNING, regime not QUIET. Quiet is read whatever the display's
//           desaturation switch says: it is a reading, not a display choice.
//  PHASE    the 5 × 5. Each momentum's side is held until it crosses its own
//           knee; conviction is confirmed while RUNNING on the row's side,
//           value once REVERTING. An unconfirmed edge sits half-way to the
//           middle.
//  UNITS    the nine families' units read at the name's shaded position - the
//           Pine's own tape ramps, the held row's push - exactly as Pragyam's
//           graded map reads them. CELL is the 5 × 5 cell unshaded.
//  WORDS    the readout's, and only the readout's: each family named as the
//           action it implies, what separates the 5 × 5 cell from its
//           neighbours, the histogram's push in five levels, and where the
//           chart alone would stand. The engine above them is the port.
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
var string G_GR = "11 · Conviction-Value Grid · price chart"
bool   inpGrid    = input.bool(true, "Grid row", group=G_GR, tooltip="The CVG row of the readout - Pragyam's Conviction-Value Grid, read for this chart.\n\nSTATE - the action its family implies (Buy, Add, Hold, Accumulate, Wait, Trim, Watch, Reduce, Exit) and the units the grid holds. WHY - what separates its 5 × 5 cell from its neighbours: each momentum tape's phase on its own edge. PUSH - conviction's own histogram as it runs the rows, Pragyam's gate. CHART - where this chart alone would put the name. SPAN - how long in the action.\n\nDRAWN ON THE PRICE CHART, in the readout under OI; '10 · Open interest' sets where the readout sits. Display only: no signal, tape or pane value reads it.\n\nPragyam reads both tapes on D · W. On a daily chart, set both ladders to Ladder up to read exactly what Pragyam sizes from.", display=display.none)

// ── conviction's histogram gate, exactly as Pragyam reads it ─────────────────
// Conviction's own histogram, whichever trace the pane draws - the pane's
// columns only on the Conviction trace.
// Tier from the column's own channels, whatever the shading display is set to:
// 0 impulse · 1 building · 2 decelerating · 3 turning. The drawn intensity -
// direction × ink × saturation - is what a HELD row keeps its cell by.
bool  gQuiet = cvReady and not na(rawSdPr) and rawSdPr < 20.0
int   gTier  = cvExpand and cvImp ? 0 : cvExpand ? 1 : cvWith ? 2 : 3
float gGate  = not cvReady ? na : gTier != 3 and not gQuiet ? (cvAbove ? 1.0 : -1.0) : 0.0
f_gInk(float m, float lo, float hi, float t0, float t1) =>
    float f = hi - lo > 1e-12 ? math.max(0.0, math.min(1.0, (m - lo) / (hi - lo))) : 0.0
    1.0 - (t0 + (t1 - t0) * f) / 100.0
float gInk   = gTier == 0 ? f_gInk(cvMag, cvThr, cvHi, 18.0, 0.0) : gTier == 1 ? f_gInk(cvMag, 0.0, math.max(cvThr, 1e-9), 60.0, 38.0) : gTier == 2 ? f_gInk(cvMag, 0.0, cvHi, 70.0, 50.0) : f_gInk(cvMag, 0.0, cvHi, 88.0, 72.0)
float gPush  = not cvReady ? na : (cvAbove ? 1.0 : -1.0) * gInk * (gQuiet ? 0.45 : 1.0)

// ── the grid's pieces: units, names, the map read at a position ──────────────
f_gUnit(int r, int c) =>
    r == 1 ? (c == 2 ? 1.5 : 3.0) : r == 0 ? (c == 0 ? 1.5 : c == 1 ? 1.0 : 0.75) : (c == 0 ? 1.0 : c == 1 ? 0.5 : 0.25)
f_gName(int r, int c) =>
    r == 1 ? (c == 0 ? "TURNED" : c == 1 ? "BUILDING" : "PAID") : r == 0 ? (c == 0 ? "BASING" : c == 1 ? "IDLE" : "STALLING") : (c == 0 ? "DISLOCATED" : c == 1 ? "FADING" : "DISTRIBUTION")
// The nine units read at a position on the map - rows −1 … +1 (DOWN … UP),
// columns −1 … +1 (CHEAP … RICH) - bilinear between the cells, as Pragyam does.
f_gAt(float rp, float cp) =>
    float r  = math.max(-1.0, math.min(1.0, rp))
    float c  = math.max(-1.0, math.min(1.0, cp))
    int   r0 = r < 0 ? -1 : 0
    int   c0 = c < 0 ? -1 : 0
    float wr = r - r0
    float wc = c - c0
    float lo = (1.0 - wc) * f_gUnit(r0, c0 + 1) + wc * f_gUnit(r0, c0 + 2)
    float hi = (1.0 - wc) * f_gUnit(r0 + 1, c0 + 1) + wc * f_gUnit(r0 + 1, c0 + 2)
    (1.0 - wr) * lo + wr * hi
// A tape's ink on the Pine's own ramp: faint 0.12 → 0.45 inside the knee, a
// step, bright 0.65 → 1.0 toward solid.
f_gTapeInk(float x, float knee, float solid) =>
    float a = math.abs(x)
    a >= knee ? 0.65 + 0.35 * math.max(0.0, math.min(1.0, (a - knee) / math.max(solid - knee, 1e-9))) : 0.12 + 0.33 * math.max(0.0, math.min(1.0, a / math.max(knee, 1e-9)))
// A momentum's standing side, its own knee the hysteresis; the sign until the
// knee is calibrated; no side without a reading.
f_gSide(float prev, float m, float knee) =>
    na(m) ? 0.0 : na(knee) or knee <= 0.0 ? math.sign(m) : m >= knee ? 1.0 : m <= -knee ? -1.0 : prev != 0.0 ? prev : math.sign(m)

// ── the state engine: the row memory and the two momentum sides ──────────────
bool  gRead = mtfCOk and mtfVOk
int   gTgt  = mC >= inpZ1 ? 1 : mC <= -inpZ1 ? -1 : 0
int   gCol  = mV <= -osc_entry ? 0 : mV >= osc_entry ? 2 : 1
var int   gRow   = na
var float gSideC = 0.0
var float gSideV = 0.0
if not gRead
    gRow   := na
    gSideC := 0.0
    gSideV := 0.0
else
    if na(gRow) or na(gGate)
        gRow := gTgt
    else if gTgt > gRow and gGate > 0
        gRow := gTgt
    else if gTgt < gRow and gGate < 0
        gRow := gTgt
    gSideC := f_gSide(gSideC, ladMomReady ? ladMom : na, ladMomThr)
    gSideV := f_gSide(gSideV, tpReady ? tpMom : na, tpThr)
bool  gHeld = gRead and gRow != gTgt
// phases: +1 confirmed, −1 not, 0 no edge or no reading
int   gCPh  = not gRead or gRow == 0 or gSideC == 0.0 ? 0 : gSideC == gRow ? 1 : -1
int   gVPh  = not gRead or gCol == 1 or gSideV == 0.0 ? 0 : gSideV == (gCol == 0 ? 1.0 : -1.0) ? 1 : -1

// ── where the graded map places it, and what it holds there ─────────────────
float gRPos = 0.0
float gCPos = 0.0
if gRead
    if gHeld
        float gHs = gRow > gTgt ? 1.0 : -1.0
        float gHh = na(gPush) ? 0.0 : math.max(0.0, math.min(1.0, gPush * gHs))
        int   gNb = gRow + (gTgt > gRow ? 1 : -1)
        gRPos := gRow * (0.5 + 0.5 * gHh) + gNb * (0.5 - 0.5 * gHh)
    else if gRow != 0
        gRPos := gRow * f_gTapeInk(mC, inpZ1, inpZ2)
    else
        gRPos := (mC > 0 ? 1.0 : -1.0) * (f_gTapeInk(mC, inpZ1, inpZ2) - 0.12)
    gCPos := gCol != 1 ? (gCol - 1) * f_gTapeInk(mV, osc_entry, 70.0) : (mV > 0 ? 1.0 : -1.0) * (f_gTapeInk(mV, osc_entry, 70.0) - 0.12)
    if gRow != 0 and gCPh == -1
        gRPos := gRPos * 0.5
    if gCol != 1 and gVPh == -1
        gCPos := gCPos * 0.5
float  gUnits = gRead ? f_gAt(gRPos, gCPos) : na
float  gCell  = gRead ? f_gAt(gRow * (gCPh == -1 ? 0.5 : 1.0), (gCol - 1) * (gVPh == -1 ? 0.5 : 1.0)) : na
string gName  = gRead ? f_gName(gRow, gCol) : ""

// days in the family, committed at each close - never counted twice live
var string gPrevName = ""
var int    gDays     = 0
gDays     := gName != "" and gName == gPrevName ? gDays + 1 : gName != "" ? 1 : 0
gPrevName := gName

// ── the readout's words ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// ACTION - each family named for what it says to do, at its own units, so the
// word is the weight. Every name is held: the action says how much, never
// whether. WORKS - the side an action works: +1 buying, −1 selling, 0 for the
// three that change nothing (Hold, Wait, Watch).
f_gAct(int r, int c) =>
    r == 1 ? (c == 0 ? "Buy" : c == 1 ? "Add" : "Hold") : r == 0 ? (c == 0 ? "Accumulate" : c == 1 ? "Wait" : "Trim") : (c == 0 ? "Watch" : c == 1 ? "Reduce" : "Exit")
f_gWorks(int r, int c) =>
    (r == 1 and c < 2) or (r == 0 and c == 0) ? 1 : (r == 0 and c == 2) or (r == -1 and c > 0) ? -1 : 0
// A span in the chart's own unit where it has one: days, weeks, months.
f_span(int n) =>
    string u = timeframe.multiplier != 1 ? " bars" : timeframe.isdaily ? "d" : timeframe.isweekly ? "w" : timeframe.ismonthly ? "mo" : " bars"
    n <= 0 ? "" : n == 1 ? "1 bar" : str.tostring(n) + u

// STATE - the action and the units Pragyam sizes from; the 5 × 5 cell, named
// as cvgrid names it, for the tooltip.
int    gWorks = gRead ? f_gWorks(gRow, gCol) : 0
color  gTone  = not gRead ? C_NEUT : gWorks > 0 ? C_BULL : gWorks < 0 ? C_BEAR : C_SOFT
string gState = gRead ? f_gAct(gRow, gCol) + " " + f_num(gUnits, "0.00") : "—"
string gR5    = gRow == 0 ? "FAINT" : (gRow == 1 ? "UP" : "DOWN") + (gCPh == 1 ? " · running" : gCPh == -1 ? " · pullback" : "")
string gC5    = gCol == 1 ? "fair" : (gCol == 0 ? "cheap" : "rich") + (gVPh == 1 ? " · reverting" : gVPh == -1 ? " · widening" : "")

// WHY - what separates the 5 × 5 cell from its neighbours inside its family:
// each momentum's phase on its own edge, in plain words, the row's first. An
// edge without a phase reading adds nothing; with neither, the family itself.
string gWhyC = gRow == 0 or gCPh == 0 ? "" : (gRow > 0 ? "buyers " : "sellers ") + (gCPh == 1 ? "running" : "pausing")
string gWhyV = gCol == 1 or gVPh == 0 ? "" : gCol == 0 ? (gVPh == 1 ? "turning up" : "still cheapening") : (gVPh == 1 ? "turning down" : "still richening")
string gWhy  = not gRead ? (not mtfCOk ? "conviction tape not ready" : "value tape not ready") :
     (gWhyC != "" and gWhyV != "" ? gWhyC + " · " + gWhyV : gWhyC != "" ? gWhyC : gWhyV != "" ? gWhyV :
      gRow == 0 and gCol == 1 ? "no edge" : str.lower(gName)) + (gHeld ? " · held" : "")

// PUSH - conviction's histogram as it runs the rows: ↑↑ an impulse, ↑ a push (pressing
// or decelerating), TURNING or QUIET no push, ↓ and ↓↓ mirrored.
string gPushTxt = not gRead or na(gGate) ? "—" : gGate > 0 ? (gTier == 0 ? "↑↑" : "↑") : gGate < 0 ? (gTier == 0 ? "↓↓" : "↓") : gQuiet ? "quiet" : "turning"
color  gPushCol = not gRead or na(gGate) ? C_SOFT : gGate > 0 ? C_BULL : gGate < 0 ? C_BEAR : gQuiet ? C_GOLD : C_SOFT

// CHART - where this chart alone would put the name: its conviction and
// Samanvaya's value on this chart, on the tapes' own cuts. Each is one rung of its tape, so
// the chart moves first. ↑ when its family carries more units than the
// state's, ↓ fewer, = the same units in another family.
bool   gChOk   = gRead and cvReady and modelBuilt
int    gChR    = cvOsc >= inpZ1 ? 1 : cvOsc <= -inpZ1 ? -1 : 0
int    gChC    = unified_osc <= -osc_entry ? 0 : unified_osc >= osc_entry ? 2 : 1
float  gChD    = gChOk ? f_gUnit(gChR, gChC) - f_gUnit(gRow, gCol) : na
string gChTxt  = not gChOk ? "—" : gChR == gRow and gChC == gCol ? "same" :
     (gChD > 0 ? "↑ " : gChD < 0 ? "↓ " : "= ") + f_gAct(gChR, gChC)
color  gChTone = not gChOk ? C_SOFT : gChD > 0 ? C_BULL : gChD < 0 ? C_BEAR : C_SOFT

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  THE READOUT · ONE TABLE ON THE PRICE CHART                    (display-only)
//  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  A row per layer and a column per question, so every column means the same
//  thing on every row:
//      STATE   where the layer stands - OI's character, the grid's action
//      WHY     what makes it so - who is doing it; what separates the cell
//      PUSH    what pushes it now - the book's change in σ; conviction's histogram
//      CHART   the grid's only - where this chart alone would put the name
//      SPAN    how long it has stood
//  Each cell is coloured by its own meaning: the state by the side it works,
//  push and chart by their direction, caution gold, the rest neutral.
//
//  force_overlay puts the table on the PRICE chart rather than in this
//  script's own pane. The oscillator reads neither layer, so they belong next
//  to the bars they describe, and they stay visible when the pane is
//  collapsed. The position is mapped explicitly and falls through to bottom
//  LEFT, the one corner the volume profile's tag column does not claim.
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
var table rdTb = na
if (inpOi or inpGrid) and not coreView and barstate.islast
    if na(rdTb)
        rdTb := table.new(
             inpOiPos == "Top centre"    ? position.top_center    :
             inpOiPos == "Top right"     ? position.top_right     :
             inpOiPos == "Bottom centre" ? position.bottom_center :
             inpOiPos == "Middle left"   ? position.middle_left   :
             inpOiPos == "Bottom right"  ? position.bottom_right  : position.bottom_left,
             6, 1 + (inpOi ? 1 : 0) + (inpGrid ? 1 : 0), bgcolor = C_PANEL, frame_color = C_GRID,
             frame_width = 1, border_color = color.new(C_NEUT, 88), border_width = 1,
             force_overlay = true)
    color rdHead = color.new(C_NEUT, 25)
    table.cell(rdTb, 0, 0, "", text_size = size.tiny)
    table.cell(rdTb, 1, 0, " STATE", text_color = rdHead, text_size = size.tiny, text_halign = text.align_left)
    table.cell(rdTb, 2, 0, " WHY", text_color = rdHead, text_size = size.tiny, text_halign = text.align_left)
    table.cell(rdTb, 3, 0, " PUSH ", text_color = rdHead, text_size = size.tiny, text_halign = text.align_center)
    table.cell(rdTb, 4, 0, " CHART ", text_color = rdHead, text_size = size.tiny, text_halign = text.align_center)
    table.cell(rdTb, 5, 0, "SPAN ", text_color = rdHead, text_size = size.tiny, text_halign = text.align_right)

    if inpOi
        // An untrusted series is still shown, but faded: it describes two
        // series that do not move together. A crowded book is gold.
        bool   oiFade = oiHas and not oiTrust
        color  oiStC  = oiFade ? color.new(oiCharCol, 50) : oiCharCol
        color  oiTxC  = oiFade ? color.new(C_SOFT, 55) : C_SOFT
        string oiBook = oiHas and not na(oiRank) ? " · book " + f_num(oiRank, "#") + " pct" : ""
        table.cell(rdTb, 0, 1, " OI", text_color = C_SOFT, text_size = size.tiny,
             text_halign = text.align_left,
             tooltip = "WHAT KIND of positioning this bar represents, which volume cannot tell you. Volume answers how much traded; open interest answers whether the trading created open risk or destroyed it.\n\nLONG BUILDUP   price up, book growing — new longs, risk committed.\nSHORT BUILDUP  price down, book growing — new shorts, risk committed.\nSHORT COVERING price up, book shrinking — a rally paid for by exits.\nLONG UNWINDING price down, book shrinking — a decline paid for by exits.\nBALANCED       the change is inside " + f_num(OI_DEAD, "0.0") + "σ of zero; nothing to read.\nEXPIRY / ROLLOVER  the book moved more than " + f_num(OI_STRU * 100, "#") + "% in one bar, or more than " + f_num(OI_ROLL, "0.0") + "σ AND more than " + f_num(OI_ROLL_ABS * 100, "#") + "%. Structural, not positioning - and kept out of the statistics.\n\nThe word always agrees with the book: BUILDUP means open interest ROSE on this bar, COVERING and UNWINDING mean it FELL.\n\nTHE COLOUR FOLLOWS GENUINENESS, NOT DIRECTION. Builds take the side's own colour. Covering and unwinding take GOLD even though one of them is a rally, because closing flow is finite — the move is consuming the very positions propelling it. That is a practitioner reading, not an established result: the academic open-interest evidence (Hong & Yogo 2012) is monthly and aggregate, and says nothing about a single bar. Gold here means what it means everywhere in this script: read with caution. Painting short covering green reads as a bullish claim and is close to the opposite one.\n\nDisplay-only: nothing here reaches the oscillator below.")
        table.cell(rdTb, 1, 1, " " + oiState + " ", text_color = oiStC, text_size = size.tiny,
             text_halign = text.align_left, bgcolor = color.new(oiCharCol, 86))
        table.cell(rdTb, 2, 1, " " + oiWho + oiBook + " ", text_color = oiCrowd and not oiFade ? C_GOLD : oiTxC,
             text_size = size.tiny, text_halign = text.align_left,
             tooltip = "WHO IS DOING IT - new longs, new shorts, shorts exiting, longs exiting, or no net change; STRUCTURAL for an expiry or a roll.\n\nBOOK — where total open interest sits against its own last " + str.tostring(OI_PCT) + " bars. This is the context the character cannot give you: a long buildup at the 95th percentile is a crowded trade being added to, and the same buildup at the 20th is fresh positioning into a cleaned-out book. They resolve differently and they look identical one bar at a time. GOLD from the 80th percentile: crowded.\n\nFEED — named in STATE when it is not live. STALE FEED means the series exists but fewer than " + f_num(OI_ACT * 100, "#") + "% of bars carry a change, which on an intraday chart usually means an end-of-day feed: the z-scores are degenerate and the character is withheld rather than repeated. DAILY ONLY means TradingView carries open interest for this symbol but not at your timeframe. NO SYMBOL means the chart carries a modifier whose ticker cannot be turned into an OI companion. NO OI means this instrument has no open interest at all, which is the correct answer for cash equity and spot.\n\nρ(|ΔOI|, activity) = " + f_num(oiRho, "+0.00;-0.00") + " over " + str.tostring(OI_BASE) + " bars - whether the SIZE of the book's change tracks " + (vpVolUsable ? "volume" : "true range") + ". The row DIMS below " + f_num(OI_RHO, "0.00") + ": the character is then describing two series that do not move together and should not be traded from.\n\nWhy not ρ(Δprice, ΔOI)? Open interest rises on long AND short buildups, so on a perfectly informative series the SIGNED correlation with price sits near zero. That test dimmed the readout exactly where it worked.")
        table.cell(rdTb, 3, 1, oiHas ? " " + f_num(oiZ, "+0.0;-0.0") + "σ " : "", text_color = oiTxC,
             text_size = size.tiny, text_halign = text.align_center,
             tooltip = "BUILD — the push on the book this bar: its log change in open interest, in units of the typical (RMS) change over the last " + str.tostring(OI_BASE) + " non-structural bars. ZERO-CENTRED: its sign is the sign of the change, so +1.8σ always means the book grew. Log, so a halving and a doubling of the book are equal and opposite rather than the second dwarfing the first. Inside ±" + f_num(OI_DEAD, "0.0") + "σ the character reads BALANCED.")
        table.cell(rdTb, 4, 1, "", text_size = size.tiny)
        table.cell(rdTb, 5, 1, f_span(oiSpan) + " ", text_color = oiTxC, text_size = size.tiny,
             text_halign = text.align_right,
             tooltip = "How long the book has shown this character - days on a daily chart, weeks on a weekly, otherwise bars. Counted at each close, never twice on a live bar.")

    if inpGrid
        int rGr = inpOi ? 2 : 1
        table.cell(rdTb, 0, rGr, " CVG", text_color = C_SOFT, text_size = size.tiny,
             text_halign = text.align_left,
             tooltip = "THE CONVICTION-VALUE GRID - the portfolio style Pragyam builds from this indicator, read for this chart. Display only: no signal, tape or pane value reads it.\n\nTHE 3 × 3 FAMILIES. Rows by the conviction tape: UP past +inner zone, DOWN past −inner zone, FAINT between. Columns by the value tape: CHEAP past −θ, RICH past +θ, FAIR between. Each is named for what it says to do, at its own units - the word is the weight:\n                 cheap · fair · rich\n   UP      Buy 3 · Add 3 · Hold 1.5\n   FAINT   Accumulate 1.5 · Wait 1 · Trim 0.75\n   DOWN    Watch 1 · Reduce 0.5 · Exit 0.25\nEvery name is held: the action says how much, never whether.\n\nCONVICTION'S HISTOGRAM RUNS THE ROWS: a row changes only while conviction's own histogram - Pragyam's gate, the pane's own on the Conviction trace - confirms a push that way - on the move's side, not TURNING, not QUIET. Otherwise the row is HELD against its tape.\n\nTHE 5 × 5. Each momentum tape confirms its own instrument's edge; an unconfirmed edge sits half-way to the middle (WHY says which).\n\nPragyam reads both tapes on D · W. On a daily chart, set both ladders to Ladder up to read exactly what Pragyam sizes from. Measured in Pragyam: level with equal weight; the momentum phases added +0.06 to +0.40%/yr over the 3 × 3.")
        table.cell(rdTb, 1, rGr, " " + gState + " ", text_color = gTone, text_size = size.tiny,
             text_halign = text.align_left, bgcolor = color.new(gTone, 86),
             tooltip = gRead ? "PRAGYAM'S FAMILY  " + gName + "\n5 × 5 CELL  " + gR5 + "  ×  " + gC5 + "\n\n" + f_num(gUnits, "0.00") + " UNITS - what Pragyam sizes this name from, before the book normalises across its universe: the family's units read at the name's position on the map, shaded inside its cell by how intensely each tape is drawn, half a cell toward the middle for an unconfirmed edge" + (gHeld ? ", and between half and all of the HELD row by how firmly the push holding it is drawn" : "") + ". The cell itself, unshaded, holds " + f_num(gCell, "0.00") + ".\n\nColoured by the side the action works: green buying, red selling, neutral for Hold, Wait and Watch, which change nothing." :
                  "The grid reads once both level tapes are ready.")
        table.cell(rdTb, 2, rGr, " " + gWhy + " ", text_color = gHeld ? C_GOLD : C_SOFT, text_size = size.tiny,
             text_halign = text.align_left,
             tooltip = "WHAT SEPARATES THIS CELL FROM ITS NEIGHBOURS - each momentum tape's phase on its own edge, in plain words.\n\nTHE ROW'S EDGE, conviction momentum. BUYERS / SELLERS RUNNING - the faster view stands on the row's side: the edge is confirmed and the name holds the full row. PAUSING - a pullback against it: half-way to the middle.\n\nTHE COLUMN'S EDGE, value momentum. TURNING UP from cheap, TURNING DOWN from rich - the fast end has turned back toward fair: confirmed, the full column. STILL CHEAPENING / STILL RICHENING - widening: half-way to the middle.\n\nA momentum changes side only past its own knee. NO EDGE - faint and fair, nothing to confirm. The family's own name alone - its momentum has no reading yet, and the cell is the 3 × 3's.\n\nHELD, in gold - the conviction tape has left the row and conviction's histogram has not confirmed a push that way, so the row holds.")
        table.cell(rdTb, 3, rGr, " " + gPushTxt + " ", text_color = gPushCol, text_size = size.tiny,
             text_halign = text.align_center,
             tooltip = "CONVICTION'S HISTOGRAM, AS IT RUNS THE ROWS - the one Pragyam reads. On the Conviction trace it is the pane's own; on the others it can differ from the columns drawn.\n   ↑↑  an impulse - growing past its impulse gate\n   ↑   a push - above zero, pressing or decelerating\n   TURNING  shrinking with the trace flat or against it - no push\n   QUIET  conviction amplifying a quiet regime - no push, gold\n   ↓  ↓↓  mirrored\nA row moves to its tape only on a push that way - any push, impulse or not; with no push it holds.\n\nREAD IT AGAINST THE ACTION - a reading, not a rule. Buy, Add and Accumulate are best done on a push ↑: with none, wait for one; against ↓, not yet. Trim, Reduce and Exit can be done now, or into a push ↑. Hold, Wait and Watch change nothing - the push says which way the row may move next.")
        table.cell(rdTb, 4, rGr, " " + gChTxt + " ", text_color = gChTone, text_size = size.tiny,
             text_halign = text.align_center,
             tooltip = "WHERE THIS CHART ALONE WOULD PUT THE NAME - this chart's conviction (" + f_num(cvOsc, "+#;-#") + ", against the inner zone) and Samanvaya's value on this chart (" + f_num(unified_osc, "+#;-#") + ", against θ), on the tapes' own cuts. Each is one rung of its tape, so the chart moves first.\n\n↑ - the chart's family carries MORE units than the state's: the chart has moved into a better cell before the ladder. ↓ - fewer. = - the same units in another family. SAME - the chart agrees with the ladder.\n\nA Watch with the chart ↑ and a push ↑ is the earliest a turn shows here.")
        table.cell(rdTb, 5, rGr, (gRead ? f_span(gDays) : "") + " ", text_color = C_SOFT, text_size = size.tiny,
             text_halign = text.align_right,
             tooltip = "How long the name has stood in this action - its family. Days on a daily chart, weeks on a weekly, otherwise bars. Counted at each close, never twice on a live bar.")

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  PANE
//
//  Placed after the open-interest block so the opt-in gold cast can read it.
//  Draw order is unchanged: background, trace, signal and shade behind; the
//  histogram in front.
//
//  COLOUR IS COMPOSED IN A FIXED ORDER: identity (which way the push), then what paid
//  for it (gold, if enabled), then whether the reading is real (quiet). Quiet is
//  applied LAST so it dims everything, a gold cast included - an amplified
//  reading is not a reading, whatever paid for it. Lightness (the tier) is
//  applied on top of the composed hue, so every tier still reads in every state.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
bool oiCast = inpOiShade and not coreView and ready and oiHas and not oiRoll and oiTrust and
     (hAbove ? (oiExitUp > oiBuildUp and oiExitUp >= inpSig / 3.0) :
               (oiExitDn > oiBuildDn and oiExitDn >= inpSig / 3.0))

color hHue = hAbove ? C_BULL : C_BEAR
if oiCast
    hHue := f_mix(hHue, C_GOLD, 0.65)
if quietReg
    hHue := f_mix(hHue, C_NEUT, 0.55)

// Each tier's colour is resolved on its own line, then selected. Pine does not
// accept a comment on a wrapped line, so the labels live here instead:
//     cImpulse    pressing, past the impulse threshold      near-solid
//     cBuilding   pressing, not yet an impulse              moderate
//     cFading     shrinking, two-tier mode                  pale
//     cDecel      shrinking, trace still with the column    mid
//     cTurning    shrinking, trace flat or against it       pale
color cImpulse  = color.from_gradient(hMag, thr, hHi, color.new(hHue, 18), color.new(hHue, 0))
color cBuilding = color.from_gradient(hMag, 0.0, math.max(thr, 1e-9), color.new(hHue, 60), color.new(hHue, 38))
color cFading   = color.from_gradient(hMag, 0.0, hHi, color.new(hHue, 80), color.new(hHue, 55))
color cDecel    = color.from_gradient(hMag, 0.0, hHi, color.new(hHue, 70), color.new(hHue, 50))
color cTurning  = color.from_gradient(hMag, 0.0, hHi, color.new(hHue, 88), color.new(hHue, 72))

color histCol = not ready ? color.new(C_NEUT, 75) : hExpand and hImp ? cImpulse : hExpand ? cBuilding : not useSlope ? cFading : trWith ? cDecel : cTurning

// ── background · the standing declaration (opt-in) ─────────────────────────
bgcolor(tintLong ? color.new(C_BULL, 94) : tintShort ? color.new(C_BEAR, 94) : na,
     title = "Declaration")

// ── secondary · trace, signal and shade recede ─────────────────────────────
float oAbs = math.abs(osc)
int   oT0  = not inpTraceZone ? 40 : oAbs >= trOut ? 5 : oAbs >= trIn ? 30 : 55
// a blend whose ingredients disagree, or whose value basket settles, dims
int   oT   = trDim ? int(oT0 + (100 - oT0) * 0.5) : oT0

pOsc = plot(ready ? osc : na, "Trace", color.new(oscCol, oT), 1)
pSig = plot(ready ? sig : na, "Signal", color.new(C_NEUT, 65), 1)
pMid = plot(0.0, "Zero", color.new(C_NEUT, 55), 1, display = display.pane)

fill(pOsc, pMid, inpFill and ready ? color.new(osc >= 0 ? C_BULL : C_BEAR, 95) : na,
     title = "Control")

// ── primary · histogram drawn last so it sits in front ─────────────────────
plot(inpHist ? hist * inpHGain : na, "Histogram", histCol, 1, plot.style_columns,
     histbase = 0.0, display = display.pane)
plot(inpThr and inpHist ?  thr * inpHGain : na, "Impulse threshold +", color.new(C_NEUT, 55), 1, display = display.pane)
plot(inpThr and inpHist ? -thr * inpHGain : na, "Impulse threshold −", color.new(C_NEUT, 55), 1, display = display.pane)
// the true value, for the Data Window only — the plotted column is scaled
plot(hist, "Histogram (native)", display = display.data_window)
plot(na(effShare) ? na : 100.0 * effShare, "Effort → result %", display = display.data_window)
// How far the reconstructed parent landed from the parent's own settled value at
// its last close, in trace points; the largest across rungs. Near zero on most
// venues. Larger only where a parent bar is not the sum of the chart's bars -
// a gap in the chart's data, or a print booked only on the higher frame.
plot(multiUp ? recErr : na, "Ladder reconstruction error (pts)", display = display.data_window)
plot(multi and ladReady ? ladAgree : na, "MTF conviction", display = display.data_window)
plot(tpReady ? tpBias : na, "MTF value (+ rich / − cheap)", display = display.data_window)
plot(modelBuilt ? unified_osc : na, "Value · chart (Samanvaya)", display = display.data_window)

// ── the ladder tapes ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Four bands of columns, one per bar, clear of every marker: the R labels hang
// from the ±60 rails and reach to roughly ±72. Each instrument's momentum band
// sits just outside its level tape, a gap between, so a pair reads as one
// instrument - where it stands inside, which way it is being led outside. Hue
// is direction, intensity is how far it has run, so nothing new is learned.
//
// MTF CONVICTION  (+82) Pragati's own ladder. Stepped on the trace's zones, the
//            same knee the trace itself uses: faint → moderate inside the inner
//            zone, a visible step brighter at it, solid at the outer zone.
// MTF VALUE       (−82) Samanvaya's MTF bias, graded exactly as Samanvaya grades
//            it - faint inside θ entry, a clear step brighter at it, solid toward
//            ±70 - with the colours FLIPPED: rich is GREEN, cheap is RED.
// MOMENTUM (±90) the lead on each ladder, on the pane columns' three tiers:
//            near-solid while the pull grows past its own impulse threshold,
//            moderate while it grows below it, pale while it shrinks. The
//            conviction side uses the pane's own column gradients, as Dhṛti
//            drew it; the value side Samanvaya's, in the value tape's colours.
// A tape that is still warming is neutral grey - warming is not a direction.
const float TAPE_Y = 82.0
const float TAPE_H = 3.0
const float MOM_Y  = 90.0

float agAbs = math.abs(ladAgree)
color agHue = ladAgree >= 0 ? C_BULL : C_BEAR
color agLo  = color.from_gradient(agAbs, 0.0, inpZ1, color.new(agHue, 88), color.new(agHue, 55))
color agHi  = color.from_gradient(agAbs, inpZ1, inpZ2, color.new(agHue, 35), color.new(agHue, 0))
color tapeAg = not ladReady ? color.new(C_NEUT, 80) : agAbs >= inpZ1 ? agHi : agLo

float vaAbs = math.abs(tpBias)
color vaHue = tpBias > 0 ? C_BULL : C_BEAR          // flipped: Samanvaya drew rich red
color vaLo  = color.from_gradient(vaAbs, 0.0, osc_entry, color.new(vaHue, 88), color.new(vaHue, 55))
color vaHi  = color.from_gradient(vaAbs, osc_entry, 70.0, color.new(vaHue, 35), color.new(vaHue, 0))
color tapeVa = not tpReady ? color.new(C_NEUT, 80) : vaAbs >= osc_entry ? vaHi : vaLo

float lmAbs  = math.abs(ladMom)
bool  lmGrow = ladMom >= 0.0 ? ladMom > nz(ladMom[1], 0.0) : ladMom < nz(ladMom[1], 0.0)
bool  lmImp  = ladMomThr > 0.0 and lmAbs >= ladMomThr
float lmHi   = math.max(2.0 * nz(ladMomSd, 0.0), 1.5 * ladMomThr, 1e-9)
color lmHue  = ladMom >= 0.0 ? C_BULL : C_BEAR
color lmImpC = color.from_gradient(lmAbs, ladMomThr, lmHi, color.new(lmHue, 18), color.new(lmHue, 0))
color lmBldC = color.from_gradient(lmAbs, 0.0, math.max(ladMomThr, 1e-9), color.new(lmHue, 60), color.new(lmHue, 38))
color lmFadC = color.from_gradient(lmAbs, 0.0, lmHi, color.new(lmHue, 80), color.new(lmHue, 55))
color tapeLm = not ladMomReady ? color.new(C_NEUT, 80) : lmGrow and lmImp ? lmImpC : lmGrow ? lmBldC : lmFadC

float tpMA   = math.abs(tpMom)
bool  tpMUp  = tpMom >= 0.0
bool  tpGrow = tpMUp ? tpMom > nz(tpMom[1], 0.0) : tpMom < nz(tpMom[1], 0.0)
bool  tpImp  = tpThr > 0.0 and tpMA >= tpThr
float tpHiM  = math.max(2.0 * nz(tpMomSd, 0.0), 1.5 * tpThr, 1e-9)
color tpMHue = tpMom > 0 ? C_BULL : C_BEAR          // flipped: Samanvaya drew toward-rich red
color tpMImp = color.from_gradient(tpMA, tpThr, tpHiM, color.new(tpMHue, 18), color.new(tpMHue, 0))
color tpMBld = color.from_gradient(tpMA, 0.0, math.max(tpThr, 1e-9), color.new(tpMHue, 62), color.new(tpMHue, 38))
color tpMFad = color.from_gradient(tpMA, 0.0, tpHiM, color.new(tpMHue, 88), color.new(tpMHue, 62))
color tpMCol = not tpReady ? color.new(C_NEUT, 80) : tpGrow and tpImp ? tpMImp : tpGrow ? tpMBld : tpMFad

plot(multi ?  TAPE_Y + TAPE_H : na, "MTF conviction tape", tapeAg, 1, plot.style_columns,
     histbase =  TAPE_Y - TAPE_H, display = display.pane)
plot(tpOn  ? -TAPE_Y + TAPE_H : na, "MTF value tape", tapeVa, 1, plot.style_columns,
     histbase = -TAPE_Y - TAPE_H, display = display.pane)
plot(inpMomC and multi ?  MOM_Y + TAPE_H : na, "MTF conviction momentum tape", tapeLm, 1, plot.style_columns,
     histbase =  MOM_Y - TAPE_H, display = display.pane)
plot(inpMomV and tpOn  ? -MOM_Y + TAPE_H : na, "MTF value momentum tape", tpMCol, 1, plot.style_columns,
     histbase = -MOM_Y - TAPE_H, display = display.pane)

// Right-edge names, so a tape is never read without knowing what it spans - or
// without knowing that it is not there. ↺ marks a FALLBACK ladder direction.
// ONE label per ladder, carrying its tape's reading and then its momentum's,
// centred on the pair while that ladder's momentum tape is on - two labels
// eight points apart would overlap on any pane of ordinary height.
float lblYC = inpMomC ? (TAPE_Y + MOM_Y) / 2.0 : TAPE_Y
float lblYV = inpMomV ? (TAPE_Y + MOM_Y) / 2.0 : TAPE_Y
string momTip = "MOMENTUM - the faster view minus the slower, on the outer band. Conviction: BUILDING / TURNING INSIDE on Ladder down (the bar's insides with or against chart conviction), RUNNING AHEAD / PULLBACK on Ladder up (the chart with or against the frames above). Value: WIDENING when it agrees with the value tape, REVERTING when it opposes it. Context only - no signal reads it."
var label tapeLbA = na
var label tapeLbV = na
var label stackLb = na
var label traceLb = na
if barstate.islast
    label.delete(tapeLbA)
    label.delete(tapeLbV)
    label.delete(stackLb)
    label.delete(traceLb)
    traceLb := label.new(bar_index + 2, trIn, (trConv ? "CONVICTION" : trVal ? "VALUE" : "CONVICTION × VALUE") + " TRACE" + (trConv ? "" : " · θ"),
         style = label.style_label_left, color = color.new(C_NEUT, 100), textcolor = trDim ? C_GOLD : C_SOFT, size = size.tiny,
         tooltip = "What the pane draws - the 'Trace' setting under 2 · Trace and MTF tapes. " +
         (trConv ? "The dotted rails are the conviction zones, the dashed the outer zone." : "The dotted rails are θ, the dashed ±60 a 3σ stretch.") +
         (trDim ? " GOLD: the trace is dimmed - " + (trSplit ? "its ingredients point opposite ways." : "the value basket is settling.") : ""))
    if inpTape != "Off"
        string cTxt = not multi ? "MTF CONVICTION · no ladder frames on this chart" :
             "MTF CONVICTION · " + ladLbl + (ladFallback ? " ↺" : "") + (ladReady ? "  " + f_num(ladAgree, "+#;-#") : "  warming") +
             (inpMomC ? "  ·  momentum " + (ladMomReady ? ladMomTag + " " + f_num(ladMom, "+#;-#") : "warming") : "")
        tapeLbA := label.new(bar_index + 2, lblYC, cTxt, style = label.style_label_left,
             color = color.new(C_NEUT, 100), textcolor = multi ? C_SOFT : C_GOLD, size = size.tiny,
             tooltip = (ladFallback ? "↺ FALLBACK - the chosen ladder direction has no frames on this chart, so the other direction is read.\n\n" : "") +
             (inpMomC ? momTip : ""))
    if inpVLad != "Off"
        string vTxt = not tpOn ? "MTF VALUE · no ladder frames on this chart" :
             "MTF VALUE · rich/cheap · " + tpLadLbl + (tpFallback ? " ↺" : "") +
             (tpReady ? "  " + (tpBias > 0 ? "rich " : "cheap ") + f_num(tpBias, "+#;-#") :
              not modelBuilt ? "  model building " + str.tostring(math.min(anchN, svNeed)) + "/" + str.tostring(svNeed) : "  warming") +
             (inpMomV and tpOn ? "  ·  momentum " + (tpReady ? (tpRev ? "reverting " : "widening ") + f_num(tpMom, "+#;-#") : "warming") : "")
        tapeLbV := label.new(bar_index + 2, -lblYV, vTxt, style = label.style_label_left,
             color = color.new(C_NEUT, 100), textcolor = tpOn ? C_SOFT : C_GOLD, size = size.tiny,
             tooltip = (tpFallback ? "↺ FALLBACK - the chosen ladder direction has no frames on this chart, so the other direction is read.\n\n" : "") +
             (inpMomV ? momTip : ""))
    if not stackOk and (inpState or inpCon or inpDiv or inpHid)
        stackLb := label.new(bar_index + 2, 0.0, "SIGNALS PAUSED · " + stackWhy, style = label.style_label_left,
             color = color.new(C_NEUT, 100), textcolor = C_GOLD, size = size.tiny)

// The rails follow the trace: the conviction zones on the Conviction trace,
// θ and ±60 on the others. Both are inputs, so hline takes them.
hZ2u = hline( trOut, "", color.new(C_BEAR, 55), hline.style_dashed, 1)
hZ1u = hline( trIn,  "", color.new(C_NEUT, 72), hline.style_dotted, 1)
hZ1d = hline(-trIn,  "", color.new(C_NEUT, 72), hline.style_dotted, 1)
hZ2d = hline(-trOut, "", color.new(C_BULL, 55), hline.style_dashed, 1)
fill(hZ2u, hZ1u, color.new(C_BEAR, 94), title = "Upper zone")
fill(hZ1d, hZ2d, color.new(C_BULL, 94), title = "Lower zone")

// ── rails ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
bool  rails = inpPlace == "Threshold rails"
float yDivU = rails ?  trOut : osc
float yDivD = rails ? -trOut : osc
float ySigU = rails ?  trIn  : osc
float ySigD = rails ? -trIn  : osc

// Every marker is sized automatically, so the rails stay legible at any zoom.
// Evidence weight is carried by intensity instead: R and the ▲▼ solid, the
// instrument-dependent ◆ and the edgeless H lighter.
//
// Every marker FADES when it is drawn in a quiet regime. The engine still fires -
// no signal logic changes - but a trigger on an amplified reading now says so on
// its own glyph instead of contradicting the washed-out columns beneath it.
//
// Every marker below already carries BOTH tapes: no signal can be drawn unless
// the chart, MTF conviction and MTF value agreed on its bar. There is no grade
// to show, so there is no separate confirmation mark.
plotshape(divBear ? yDivU : na, "Regular bearish divergence", shape.labeldown,
     location.absolute, f_q(C_BEAR, 0), text = "R", textcolor = C_INK, size = size.auto, display = display.pane)
plotshape(divBull ? yDivD : na, "Regular bullish divergence", shape.labelup,
     location.absolute, f_q(C_BULL, 0), text = "R", textcolor = C_INK, size = size.auto, display = display.pane)
plotshape(hidBear ? yDivU : na, "Hidden bearish divergence", shape.labeldown,
     location.absolute, f_q(C_BEAR, 45), text = "H", textcolor = C_INK, size = size.auto, display = display.pane)
plotshape(hidBull ? yDivD : na, "Hidden bullish divergence", shape.labelup,
     location.absolute, f_q(C_BULL, 45), text = "H", textcolor = C_INK, size = size.auto, display = display.pane)

plotshape(sellSig ? ySigU : na, "SELL declaration ▼", shape.triangledown,
     location.absolute, f_q(C_BEAR, 0), size = size.auto, display = display.pane)
plotshape(buySig  ? ySigD : na, "BUY declaration ▲", shape.triangleup,
     location.absolute, f_q(C_BULL, 0), size = size.auto, display = display.pane)
// Both directions share one plot: an up-impulse and a down-impulse cannot occur
// on the same bar, and a series colour costs the plot budget two either way.
plotshape(conShort ? ySigU : conLong ? ySigD : na, "Continuation", shape.diamond,
     location.absolute, conShort ? f_q(C_BEAR, 30) : f_q(C_BULL, 30), size = size.auto, display = display.pane)

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  ALERTS
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
alertcondition(buySig, title="Pragati · BUY declared ▲",
     message="BUY declared. The trace crossed back up through −θ (on the Conviction trace, Samanvaya's value on this chart did) with the value basket settled, and within the confirmation window the MTF value ladder had been cheap past θ, the trace's histogram turned above zero, the conviction tape was above zero or rising and (if enabled) participation had been absorbed - effort exhausted. A declaration about the market at this bar, standing until a SELL. Not a trade.")
alertcondition(sellSig, title="Pragati · SELL declared ▼",
     message="SELL declared. The trace crossed back down through +θ (on the Conviction trace, Samanvaya's value on this chart did) with the value basket settled, and within the confirmation window the MTF value ladder had been rich past θ, the trace's histogram turned below zero, the conviction tape was below zero or falling and (if enabled) participation had been absorbed - effort exhausted. A declaration about the market at this bar, standing until a BUY. Not a trade.")
alertcondition(conLong, title="Pragati · continuation long ◆",
     message="Buyer control held through a pullback and the push resumed past k·σ, with the conviction tape past the inner zone, the MTF value ladder not rich past θ and (if enabled) the push producing result rather than being absorbed. If the marker is faded it fired in a quiet regime.")
alertcondition(conShort, title="Pragati · continuation short ◆",
     message="Seller control held through a pullback and the push resumed past k·σ, with the conviction tape past the inner zone, the MTF value ladder not cheap past θ and (if enabled) the push producing result rather than being absorbed. If the marker is faded it fired in a quiet regime.")
alertcondition(divBear and confirmed, title="Pragati · regular bearish divergence R",
     message="Price made a higher high while conviction made a lower high, the MTF conviction ladder also made a lower high, and the MTF value ladder is on the rich side. The advance is not confirmed on this chart or across the ladder. A condition, not a trigger.")
alertcondition(divBull and confirmed, title="Pragati · regular bullish divergence R",
     message="Price made a lower low while conviction made a higher low, the MTF conviction ladder also made a higher low, and the MTF value ladder is on the cheap side. The decline is not confirmed on this chart or across the ladder. A condition, not a trigger.")
alertcondition(hidBear and confirmed, title="Pragati · hidden bearish divergence H",
     message="Price made a lower high while conviction made a higher high, inside a ladder in seller control with value not cheap past θ: a failed bounce in a downtrend.")
alertcondition(hidBull and confirmed, title="Pragati · hidden bullish divergence H",
     message="Price made a higher low while conviction made a lower low, inside a ladder in buyer control with value not rich past θ: a pullback in an uptrend.")
